  "office_action": "Decision on Applicant's A1 submission and continued action\nSummary\n- The prior 35 U.S.C. 112(b) (pre-AIA second paragraph) rejection of claims 1-11 is withdrawn in view of Applicant's amendment to independent claim 1 and the specification support. The amendment to define the \"complex structure\" as a \"mountain range-shaped microstructure and nano-fiber structures having a wire shape or a rod shape\" resolves the prior indefiniteness concern. - The prior rejections under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102/103 remain outstanding for the reasons below. The Examiner sets forth the grounds and the reasoning and identifies the evidence of record. Applicant is invited to reply with arguments, evidence, or an interview request. Continued 35 U.S.C. 102/103 grounds (summary and reasoning)\n1) Claims 1-3 and 6-8 (anticipation/obviousness over Wang; in the alternative obviousness in view of Raghuraman): The Office's November 30, 2016 action explained that Wang (as characterized in that action) teaches anodizing aluminum to produce an anodized (aluminum oxide) film having micro- and nano-scale features and further teaches formation of a hydrophobic monomolecular layer (trichlorooctadecyl-silane) on that anodized surface. The Office also noted that substitution of hydrophobic silane species (e.g., Applicant's recited HDFS) is an obvious or predictable variation supported by Raghuraman (or equivalent disclosures), and that a person of ordinary skill would understand that a silane monolayer will conform to the underlying surface topography. The record citation in the prior action is: \"Claim(s) 1-3 and 6-8 is/are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) ... as anticipated by Wang\" (IW40PYD7RXEAPX2). Applicant argues that Wang does not disclose the specific mountain-range/nano-fiber morphology recited in amended claim 1 and that the claimed surface provides different droplet removal behavior. The Examiner acknowledges Applicant's arguments and the amendment, but finds that the record (as relied upon in the Office action) still supports a prima facie showing that the combination of anodizing and monolayer surface modification in the prior art renders the claimed structural and functional result within the scope of the applied prior art. If Applicant disputes the factual premise (for example, that Wang lacks the claimed morphology), Applicant must either (a) point to specific passages/figures in the applied references that contradict the Office's characterization, or (b) supply objective evidence (declaration, comparative SEM data, or other experimental data) showing the prior art lack or showing an unexpected property achieved only by the claimed morphology that would rebut the Examiner's position. Absent such evidence, the rejection will be maintained. 2) Claims 4-5 (obviousness over Wang in view of Lerner): The prior action explained that Wang discloses anodizing and that Lerner discloses anodizing voltage as a result-effective variable and provides examples of higher voltages (up to 100 V) depending on material and film thickness; the Office relied upon this teaching to support selection of the claimed anodizing voltage/time/temperature ranges as within the ordinary skill of the art. See: \"Claim 4 and 5 is rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) ... as obvious over Wang ... and further in view of Lerner\" (IW40PYD7RXEAPX2). Applicant's reply traversed this rejection but provided no comparative data or evidence of criticality or unexpected results across the claimed ranges. The Examiner finds the combination to present a prima facie case of obviousness because the cited references would have suggested selecting workable anodizing parameters (temperature, voltage, time) within the recited ranges by routine optimization. If Applicant believes the recited ranges are critical (i.e., produce unexpected results), submit comparative data or a declaration explaining the unexpected result over the closest prior art. 3) Claims 9-11 (obviousness over Wang in view of Gentleman and Shah): The prior action explained that Gentleman teaches condensers and tubes with hydrophobic coatings and that Wang teaches an anodized hydrophobic aluminum surface; combining these teachings to form coolant tubes or evaporator tubes with an anodized hydrophobic surface was found to be an obvious design choice. Shah was cited for teaching extended-surface fins and zigzag/ waveform fins. See: \"Claim 9 is rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) ... as obvious over Wang ... and further in view of Gentleman\" and \"Claims 10 and 11 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) ... as obvious over Wang ... and further in view of Gentleman and Shah\" (IW40PYD7RXEAPX2). Applicant argues foreign allowances and that the combination fails to teach the claimed evaporator having the specific fabricated super-hydrophobic surface, but offers no comparative evidence demonstrating non-obviousness or a patentable distinction. The Examiner therefore maintains the 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections. Applicant options and response requirements\n- Applicant may file a substantive reply traversing the outstanding 102/103 rejections with specific factual support (citations to the applied references showing absence of claimed features) or experimental/declaratory evidence showing unexpected results or structural distinctions not taught by the applied art. - Applicant may request an interview to discuss the outstanding rejections. If an interview is desired, provide at least three proposed dates and times and the best telephone number. - If Applicant believes allowance is proper without further amendment, submit a detailed explanation specifically addressing the cited references and why each applied reference fails to teach or suggest each asserted limitation. Examiner notes on evidence and record\n- Examiner relied on the November 30, 2016 Office action for the factual characterizations of the cited prior art and the combinations made therein (document IW40PYD7RXEAPX2). That Office action is part of the record and is the basis for the outstanding rejections. - Applicant's amendments that overcame the 35 U.S.C. 112 issue are accepted into the record. Procedural and administrative\nThis is a non-final action on the merits (continued rejection). Respond within the statutory period or the application will be subject to final disposition in accord with practice. The Examiner is available for a conference if Applicant believes it would aid in resolving the outstanding issues.",
  "limitations": [
    "The Office relied on the prior Office action IW40PYD7RXEAPX2 for the factual characterization of Wang and other applied references; full text and figures of Wang and the other cited prior-art documents are not included in the present record provided to the applicant in this communication. If Applicant disputes the Office's factual characterizations, Applicant should cite specific passages/figures from the applied references or submit declarations/experimental data to rebut them.",
    "Applicant referenced foreign allowances; foreign prosecution outcomes are not dispositive for U.S. patentability and do not, by themselves, overcome the applied 102/103 rejections.",
    "Evidence_status entries note where the record supports a proposition, and where the Office's characterization rests on the prior Office action. If additional primary-source prior-art documents are needed to resolve issues, those documents should be submitted by Applicant."
  ]
}

