  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [1,11],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues Hutchins teaches gas-generating pathways and uses crosslinked polymers, whereas Applicant's invention uses non-crosslinked water-dispersible polymers to form gels that plug and seal flow channels; Applicant submitted a declaration and field-comparison plot showing better injectivity reduction for the Applicant's SPI treatments than a crosslinked-polymer treatment (Marcit). Applicant amended claims to clarify plugging/sealing of flow channels and single-stage alkaline fluid composition.",
      "response": "The Examiner acknowledges the Applicant's arguments and the submission of field data and a declaration. However, the record shows Hutchins discloses gel-forming compositions that include silicates and polymeric gel-formers together with delayed-acting esters/anhydrides and gas precursors and discloses placing such compositions in subterranean formation pores to reduce permeability. The mere presence in Hutchins of colloidal/silicate and polymeric gel-formers (with delayed reaction chemistries) reads on the broad, generic claim limitations. Hutchins' teaching that ‘‘gel-forming ingredient’’ may be colloids and that delayed acting substances (e.g., esters, anhydrides) transform species in-situ supports the Examiner's position that Hutchins anticipates or renders obvious the claimed subject matter in absence of a clear limiting distinction in claim language. With respect to the asserted distinction (crosslinked vs non-crosslinked), the Hutchins disclosure includes a broad genus of gel-formers and does not limit the gel to only permanently crosslinked polymer networks; moreover, claim 1 and claim 11 as presently drafted do not require a particular, structural mechanism of gelation that would exclude Hutchins' teaching. Regarding the field data, the Examiner finds the submitted data and declaration do not, on their face, establish a commensurate, unexpected technical effect across the full scope of the claims. The declaration lacks the detailed, controlled comparative tests required to persuasively rebut a prima facie case of anticipation/obviousness: testing must compare treatments under the same operating conditions, using the same formation and treatment volumes, materials and procedures, and must show that the asserted improvement is attributable to the claimed structural limitation (here, the use of non-crosslinked polymers) rather than to other differences in operational parameters or compositions. Absent such persuasive, comparative evidence, the prior-art rejections are maintained. Applicant may rebut by (a) amending claims to recite structural or process limitations that distinguish over Hutchins (for example, explicit non-crosslinked polymer limitations tied to measurable properties or exclusion of gas- generating precursors), and/or (b) submitting detailed comparative data and an accompanying sworn declaration that demonstrates a statistically significant, unexpected advantage attributable to the claimed limitation and commensurate in scope with the claims.",
      "support_doc_id": "IP70AMJ4RXEAPX1",
      "support_span": "Claims 1-7 and claim 19 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by US Patent 5268112 (Hutchins)."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [19],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant did not concede that claim 19 is improper dependent form and amended other claims; Applicant contends amendments were for clarification and do not change scope.",
      "response": "The Examiner maintains that claim 19 is still in improper dependent form because it recites carbonic acid or CO2, which are not within the organic reactive carbonyl species required by claim 11/18. The amendment of other claims does not resolve the statutory dependent-form requirement for claim 19. To place claim 19 in proper dependent form, Applicant must either: (1) amend claim 19 so that it further limits claim 11/18 by specifying an organic reactive carbonyl species that is within the genus recited in claim 11/18 (e.g., an organic carbonate, propylene carbonate, glyoxal, etc.), or (2) rewrite claim 19 as an independent claim directed to CO2/carbonic acid initiators (i.e., remove its dependent reference to claim 18), or (3) cancel claim 19. Absent such amendment, the 35 U.S.C. 112(d) rejection is maintained.",
      "support_doc_id": "IP70AMJ4RXEAPX1",
      "support_span": "Claim 19 is rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112(d) ... as being of improper dependent form"
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Action on Application (Continuation-in-Part) — substantive reply to Applicant's A1 amendment and remarks\nA. Summary\nThis action continues and maintains the Office’s prior rejections. The application remains under pre-AIA first-to-invent provisions. Applicant elected Group I claims (claims 1–7 and 11–19) for examination. The Examiner has considered Applicant’s September 9, 2016 amendments and arguments (including the submitted declaration and field data). The rejections under 35 U.S.C. 112(d) (improper dependent form), pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) (anticipation), and pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) (obviousness) are maintained for the reasons set forth below. The prior Office action (June 10, 2016) was made final; Applicant’s response did not overcome the deficiencies identified. This action is a continuation of that final action and continues the position that claims are unallowable in their present form.\nB. Claim-form (35 U.S.C. 112) — Claim 19\n1. Grounds and reasoning\nClaim 19 depends from claim 18 (and ultimately claim 11). Claim 11 requires a \"water soluble initiator containing at least one organic reactive carbonyl compound.\" Claim 18 enumerates exemplars: \"esters, amides, carbonates, anhydrides, ketones, carbohydrates, carbamates, ureas, and hydantoins.\" Claim 19 as written recites \"carbonic acid, or carbon dioxide in a gaseous, liquid or supercritical state.\" Carbonic acid/CO2 are inorganic carbonyl species, not organic reactive carbonyl compounds as required by claim 11/18. A claim in dependent form must incorporate all limitations of the claim upon which it depends and additionally limit that subject matter. Because claim 19 fails to incorporate and further limit the organic-reactive-carbonyl limitation, it is in improper dependent form. See 35 U.S.C. 112(d) (pre-AIA 4th paragraph). The Examiner therefore maintains the 35 U.S.C. 112(d) rejection for claim 19.\n2. Guidance to Applicant\nTo place claim 19 in proper form, Applicant must do one of the following: (a) amend claim 19 so that it properly depends from claim 18 by reciting an initiator that is an organic reactive carbonyl (for example: \"The method of claim 18, wherein the at least one reactive carbonyl compound is propylene carbonate\"), (b) cancel claim 19, or (c) rewrite claim 19 in independent form directed specifically to carbonic acid/CO2 initiators and remove its dependency on claim 18/11. If Applicant chooses (c), the Examiner will treat the new independent claim as a new ground for search/examination.\nC. Anticipation (pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b)) — Claims 1–7 and 19\n1. Grounds and reasoning\nThe Examiner maintains the rejection that claims 1–7 and 19 are anticipated by Hutchins (U.S. Pat. No. 5,268,112). Hutchins discloses mixing aqueous gel-forming ingredients including silicates and polymeric gel formers together with delayed-acting substances (esters, anhydrides) and/or gaseous precursors and placing the resultant materials into subterranean formations to alter pore-occupancy and reduce permeability. The Hutchins disclosure (as cited in the prior action) specifically teaches the use of esters and anhydrides as delay agents and also teaches injecting gellable compositions into a subterranean formation to occupy pores and reduce permeability. Those teachings read on the broad, generic limitations recited in claims 1–7 and 19, such as providing a ratio of a water soluble silicate, providing a water-dispersible polymer, providing a delayed initiator (esters/anhydrides), combining and placing into the formation to modify permeability. The Examiner therefore finds Hutchins to be anticipatory of the broad claim language now before the Office.\n2. Response to Applicant's arguments\nApplicant argues Hutchins is gas-generating and uses crosslinked polymers, while Applicant's claimed invention uses non-crosslinked polymers to plug and seal flow channels. Two points warrant emphasis: (a) Hutchins’ disclosure is broad and describes ‘‘gel-forming ingredient[s]’’ and includes silicates and polymeric materials as gel-formers; the cited Hutchins passages support a reading that the reference teaches forming gellable compositions employing silicates and polymeric ingredients together with esters/anhydrides/delayed agents and placing same in formations to affect pore occupancy; (b) Applicant’s claims, as presently drafted, do not contain a structural limitation that excludes the materials and approaches described in Hutchins — e.g., claim 1 and claim 11 do not require a specific, measurable non-crosslinked polymer architecture or exclude the presence of gas precursors or crosslinking mechanisms. Where claim language is broad and reads on the prior art disclosure, a rejection stands unless claim language is amended to recite distinguishing features not disclosed in the cited reference or persuasive evidence is submitted that demonstrates that the prior art does not, in fact, disclose the claimed subject matter. Applicant’s field data and declaration have been considered but do not, without further detail, rebut the prima facie case of anticipation given the breadth of the claims and the express teachings in Hutchins.\nD. Obviousness (pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a)) — Claims 11–18\n1. Grounds and reasoning\nClaims 11–18 are rejected as obvious over Hutchins. Hutchins teaches the same set of components and the concept of delivering gel-forming species and delayed-acting agents into subterranean formations. Hutchins also suggests choices among gel reagents and delay agents, and the use of gas or delayed reagents in formation treatments. Where Hutchins does not explicitly recite the particular order of injecting a first portion of initiator, then the composition, then a second portion of initiator, established precedent treats the selection of order of process steps or mixing as prima facie obvious absent demonstration of new or unexpected results arising from the particular ordering. See In re Burhans and In re Gibson (cited in the prior action). The Examiner therefore maintains the 103 rejections for claims 11–18 in view of Hutchins and the lack of persuasive comparative evidence showing an unexpected effect arising from the claimed ordering.\n2. Response to Applicant's argument regarding non-crosslinked polymers and comparative field data\nApplicant argues the claimed non-crosslinked polymers yield superior, unexpected results and submitted a declaration plus a field-plot comparing two SPI treatments (non-crosslinked system) versus a Marcit (crosslinked) treatment. The Examiner has reviewed the submission. To overcome a prima facie obviousness rejection on the basis of unexpected results, Applicant must present persuasive, well-documented comparative data that (a) compares the claimed invention and the closest prior-art alternative under substantially the same conditions, (b) shows that the advantages are due to the claimed distinguishing feature(s) (here, non-crosslinked polymer usage), and (c) that the observed advantage is commensurate in scope with the claims. The submitted field plot and declaration, as currently in the record, do not supply sufficient experimental detail (e.g., exact formulations, volumes injected, pre-treatment and post-treatment test procedures, reservoir and completion specifics, statistical analysis) to permit the Examiner to find the unexpected results are attributable to the claimed limitation and are broadly representative of the claim scope. Consequently, the Examiner finds the declaration/plot insufficient to rebut the prima facie case of obviousness.\nE. What Applicant can do to place the application in condition for allowance\nTo overcome the outstanding rejections, Applicant may pursue one or more of the following paths (not all are required; choices depend on Applicant’s prosecution strategy):\n1. Amend claims to recite clear, limiting features that distinguish over Hutchins and other cited art. Examples of limiting language that might be effective include: (a) explicitly reciting \"non-crosslinked\" and further reciting a measurable property or preparation technique that excludes crosslinked systems (e.g., \"non-crosslinked water-dispersible polymer having no covalent interchain crosslinks and with a solution intrinsic viscosity within X–Y\"), (b) excluding gas precursors or gas-generation chemistries (if Applicant’s invention does not use such chemicals), or (c) reciting a specific initiator class (e.g., propylene carbonate, glyoxal) that is not disclosed by Hutchins in the claimed combination. Any new independent claim introduced will be searched and examined anew.\n2. Provide persuasive, corroborated experimental evidence and a supporting sworn declaration (37 C.F.R. 1.132) that: (a) specifies the exact formulations and concentrations used for the Applicant treatment(s) and the comparative prior-art treatment(s); (b) describes the test/field procedures in detail, including reservoir conditions, injection volumes, rates, temperatures, and monitoring methods; (c) demonstrates that the observed performance advantage is due to the claimed structural limitation (for example a direct head-to-head comparison where the only difference is the non-crosslinked vs crosslinked polymer, all else held constant); and (d) shows the improvement is statistically significant and commensurate with the claim scope. Merely asserting superiority, or presenting a plot without sufficient experimental detail and controlling for confounding variables, is insufficient to overcome the Examiner's findings.\n3. Cancel or properly re-draft claim 19 so that it is in proper dependent form or in independent form if carbonic acid/CO2 initiators are desired.\nF. Procedural posture — continued finality\nAs noted in the Examiner’s June 10, 2016 action, the restriction requirement and the prior rejections were made final. Applicant’s subsequent amendment and remarks have not placed the application in condition for allowance. Accordingly, the Examiner maintains the outstanding rejections and enters this continued-final position. If Applicant files a reply that introduces new claim language or substantial new evidence (for example, a properly detailed sworn declaration with head-to-head comparative tests), the Examiner will consider whether action reopening or withdrawal of finality is appropriate and whether a supplemental search is required.\nG. Summary of required applicant response\n1. For claim 19: amend claim 19 to be in proper dependent form (or cancel or re-write it as independent), or follow the Examiner’s guidance above. 35 U.S.C. 112(d) rejection will be maintained until corrected.\n2. For claims 1–7 and 11–18: either (a) amend the claims to include distinguishing limitations that avoid the teachings of Hutchins, or (b) submit persuasive, corroborated comparative data and a supporting sworn declaration that meets the criteria set forth above and demonstrates unexpected results commensurate with the claim scope.\n3. If Applicant files amended independent claims with new subject matter, note that a supplemental search and examination may be required; fees for new claims may apply.\nH. Miscellaneous\nIf Applicant wishes to discuss this action telephonically, call the Examiner at the telephone number listed on the file; if unreachable, the Examiner’s Supervisor contact is listed in the file wrapper. Please be specific in any telephone call so the Examiner can be prepared and so that prosecution will be efficient.\nRespectfully submitted,\nExaminer, Art Unit 1768\nI. Limitations\n[1] The Examiner relied on Hutchins (U.S. Pat. No. 5,268,112) as the primary prior art for anticipation and obviousness. If Applicant believes a different interpretation of Hutchins is warranted, Applicant must point to the exact Hutchins passages and the exact claim limitation that they contend Hutchins does not disclose.\n[2] The Examiner found Applicant’s declaration and field plot to be insufficient as presented. The record lacks detailed comparative experimental procedures and compositional specifics required to support an unexpected-results showing that is commensurate in scope with the claims. Applicant may supply such evidence with a proper sworn declaration and detailed experimental data.\n[3] The Examiner’s maintenance of the 35 U.S.C. 112(d) rejection for claim 19 rests on the dependency relationship and the inconsistency between an \"organic reactive carbonyl\" limitation and recitation of carbonic acid/CO2. If Applicant demonstrates that \"carbonic acid/CO2\" is reasonably and inherently encompassed by the prior recited genus in claim 11/18 (with supporting arguments and evidence), the Examiner will reconsider.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "Primary prior-art reliance is Hutchins (US 5,268,112) as cited in the record; if Applicant contests Hutchins’ disclosure, the record must show exact passages and detailed dispute.",
    "Applicant submitted field data and a declaration, but the record lacks sufficiently detailed head-to-head experimental conditions and statistical analysis to support unexpected-results arguments; additional experimental detail and a proper sworn declaration are needed to rebut the prima facie case.",
    "Claim 19 remains improperly dependent because it recites carbonic acid/CO2 while depending from claims that require an organic reactive carbonyl; Applicant must amend or rewrite claim 19 to resolve the 112(d) defect."
  ]
}

