      "response": "The Examiner acknowledges the Applicant's arguments and the submission of field data and a declaration. However, the record shows Hutchins discloses gel-forming compositions that include silicates and polymeric gel-formers together with delayed-acting esters/anhydrides and gas precursors and discloses placing such compositions in subterranean formation pores to reduce permeability. The mere presence in Hutchins of colloidal/silicate and polymeric gel-formers (with delayed reaction chemistries) reads on the broad, generic claim limitations. Hutchins' teaching that ‘‘gel-forming ingredient’’ may be colloids and that delayed acting substances (e.g., esters, anhydrides) transform species in-situ supports the Examiner's position that Hutchins anticipates or renders obvious the claimed subject matter in absence of a clear limiting distinction in claim language. With respect to the asserted distinction (crosslinked vs non-crosslinked), the Hutchins disclosure includes a broad genus of gel-formers and does not limit the gel to only permanently crosslinked polymer networks; moreover, claim 1 and claim 11 as presently drafted do not require a particular, structural mechanism of gelation that would exclude Hutchins' teaching. Regarding the field data, the Examiner finds the submitted data and declaration do not, on their face, establish a commensurate, unexpected technical effect across the full scope of the claims. The declaration lacks the detailed, controlled comparative tests required to persuasively rebut a prima facie case of anticipation/obviousness: testing must compare treatments under the same operating conditions, using the same formation and treatment volumes, materials and procedures, and must show that the asserted improvement is attributable to the claimed structural limitation (here, the use of non-crosslinked polymers) rather than to other differences in operational parameters or compositions. Absent such persuasive, comparative evidence, the prior-art rejections are maintained. Applicant may rebut by (a) amending claims to recite structural or process limitations that distinguish over Hutchins (for example, explicit non-crosslinked polymer limitations tied to measurable properties or exclusion of gas- generating precursors), and/or (b) submitting detailed comparative data and an accompanying sworn declaration that demonstrates a statistically significant, unexpected advantage attributable to the claimed limitation and commensurate in scope with the claims.",
      "support_doc_id": "IP70AMJ4RXEAPX1",
      "support_span": "Claims 1-7 and claim 19 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by US Patent 5268112 (Hutchins)."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [19],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant did not concede that claim 19 is improper dependent form and amended other claims; Applicant contends amendments were for clarification and do not change scope.",
      "response": "The Examiner maintains that claim 19 is still in improper dependent form because it recites carbonic acid or CO2, which are not within the organic reactive carbonyl species required by claim 11/18. The amendment of other claims does not resolve the statutory dependent-form requirement for claim 19. To place claim 19 in proper dependent form, Applicant must either: (1) amend claim 19 so that it further limits claim 11/18 by specifying an organic reactive carbonyl species that is within the genus recited in claim 11/18 (e.g., an organic carbonate, propylene carbonate, glyoxal, etc.), or (2) rewrite claim 19 as an independent claim directed to CO2/carbonic acid initiators (i.e., remove its dependent reference to claim 18), or (3) cancel claim 19. Absent such amendment, the 35 U.S.C. 112(d) rejection is maintained.",
      "support_doc_id": "IP70AMJ4RXEAPX1",
      "support_span": "Claim 19 is rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112(d) ... as being of improper dependent form"
    }
