  "office_action": "Examiner Response to Applicant's A1 (Amendment and Remarks)\nSummary\n- The applicant filed amended claims (A1 document ISS0O53CRXEAPX3) and arguments (ISS0O53DRXEAPX3) in response to the Office action mailed May 5, 2016 (INUFX76HRXEAPX2). The applicant's amendments recite in claims 1-4 that the magnet is a 'toroidally wound magnet.' Applicant argues the cited prior art (MIT, Young, Kobayashi) do not disclose the specific radial/within-pancake conductor-width or plate strand-spacing features alleged in the claims and therefore the prior rejections should be withdrawn.\n- After review of the record (including the May 5, 2016 Office action and the A1 amendment and remarks filed September 6, 2016), the examiner maintains the rejections set forth in the May 5, 2016 Office action and provides the following explanation and guidance. The application remains under active examination; the current action is non-final and the applicant is invited to reply.\nDiscussion of Applicant Amendments and Arguments\n1) Claims 1 and 5 — 35 U.S.C. 102 (anticipated by MIT (WO 2013/180802))\n- Applicant amended claim 1 to require a 'toroidally wound magnet.' The May 5, 2016 Office action relied on MIT to teach epitaxially deposited YBCO/ReBCO tapes and multi-width tape embodiments that result in a relatively constant operating-current-to-critical-current margin across a coil assembly. O1 expressly cited MIT as the anticipation basis for claims 1 and 5 (INUFX76HRXEAPX2: \"Claims 1 and 5 rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1)/(a)(2) as being anticipated by MIT (WO 2013/180802).\").\n- Applicant's principal argument is that MIT varies tape widths between separate pancakes in an axial stack (i.e., different pancakes in the stack have different constant tape widths) and does not teach varying tape width radially within a single pancake/turn. Applicant contends MIT teaches away from within-pancake width variation and that the present disclosure instead requires radial-within-a-turn variation to maintain a relatively constant margin throughout an individual coil.\n- Examiner response: The amendment narrowing the claim to a 'toroidally wound magnet' does not, on the face of the present record, remove the examiner's basis for the prior anticipation finding because O1 relied upon MIT's multi-width conductor teachings and also identified toroidal magnet embodiments in the applied art (see O1 combination references). The O1 rejection remains supported by the record citations in INUFX76HRXEAPX2. If applicant wishes to pursue a clear distinction over MIT based on the asserted intra-pancake radial-width limitation, applicant should (a) point to the precise passages in the specification that explicitly support radial-within-a-turn tape-width variation and (b) consider further amending the claims to expressly recite the intra-pancake/radial-within-a-turn limitation (for example, language to the effect of 'wherein the width of each conductor tape is varied along a single turn from an inner radial position to an outer radial position within the same coil') so the examiner and the record can unambiguously evaluate whether MIT (or other art on the record) discloses or suggests that precise limitation. Absent an amendment or persuasive record evidence that MIT contains no teaching or suggestion of the specific intra-turn radial-width variation now argued by applicant, the anticipation rejection is maintained.\n2) Claims 2-4, 6 — 35 U.S.C. 103 (MIT in view of Young)\n- O1 rejected these dependent claims as obvious over MIT in view of Young (INUFX76HRXEAPX2). Applicant argues Young addresses structural supports/plates but does not teach conductor arrangement required to create a uniform field in a toroid or radial-within-a-turn grading.\n- Examiner response: The combination relied upon in O1 pairs the conductor-width/gradings concepts of MIT with the toroidal plates/shells/support teachings in Young to address the 'toroidally wound' limitations of the amended claims. On the current record, the combination of references remains an appropriate basis for the asserted obviousness rejection of the amended claims because the amended language (specifying a toroidal magnet) is within the scope of the toroidal teachings cited by O1 and MIT supplies the conductor-grading teachings asserted to be at issue. If applicant can (i) identify explicit specification passages that demonstrate the claimed radial-within-a-turn conductor-width variation, and (ii) either amend claims to recite that precise feature or provide persuasive arguments and evidence why the combined prior art fails to suggest the claimed structure, the examiner will re-evaluate the obviousness findings.\n3) Claims 7-9 — 35 U.S.C. 103 (Young in view of MIT)\n- O1 rejected these claims as obvious over Young in view of MIT. Applicant contends MIT varies widths between pancakes and does not disclose adjusting radial distance between strands in plates as recited in claim 7.\n- Examiner response: The prior rejection combined Young's plate/shell toroidal teachings and MIT's conductor arrangement/optimization teachings. On the current record the examiner finds the applied combination addresses the elements recited in claims 7-9. If applicant believes 'adjusting radial distance between superconductor strands or cables in the plates' requires a structural or positional feature not taught or suggested by the applied combination, applicant should (a) supply the specific disclosure support in the present specification for that precise plate-level strand-spacing limitation and (b) consider claim language clarification (for example explicitly stating that discrete strands within a single plate are spaced non-uniformly along the plate radius to effect field uniformity). Clearer claim language and identification of supporting specification text will permit a re-assessment of the applied references against the clarified limitation.\n4) Claim 10 — 35 U.S.C. 102 (anticipated by Kobayashi)\n- O1 found Kobayashi anticipates the hybrid magnet having shells and radial plates, and cited Kobayashi's description of radial field variation. Applicant argues Kobayashi shows 1/r variation and does not teach providing radial-plate currents that maintain a near-constant magnetic field in the bore.\n- Examiner response: O1 relied on Kobayashi to supply the shell/plate toroidal structure. The anticipation finding in O1 stands on the record citations. Applicant's argument that Kobayashi shows 1/r variation and not a near-constant bore field is recorded and will be considered. If applicant can demonstrate with record citations that Kobayashi (or any other applied reference) cannot be read to disclose the claimed radial-plate-current arrangement 'required to maintain a near-constant magnetic field' (or alternatively, if applicant amends claim 10 to more narrowly and explicitly define the required current distribution or plate configuration), the examiner will re-evaluate the anticipation conclusion. As of this action, the anticipation rejection is maintained.\nGuidance to Applicant\n- To overcome the rejections presently maintained, applicant may consider one or more of the following actions:\n  a) Amend claims to more precisely recite the structural and functional limitations applicant relies upon as distinguishing over the applied art. Example narrowing language would explicitly state that tape width varies 'along an individual turn' from an inner radial position to an outer radial position within the same pancake or coil, or that radial plate strand spacing is varied 'within a single plate' along its radial extent to achieve the claimed near-constant bore field. Clear, unambiguous claim language tailored to the asserted distinguishing features will allow a focused re-examination of the cited references.\n  b) Submit a claim-chart style response identifying the exact passages in the specification that support any newly-emphasized limitation and explain why the cited prior art does not disclose or suggest that limitation. Cite specific paragraphs/figures for each asserted distinguishing point.\n  c) If applicant believes any of the cited references have been mischaracterized in O1, point to the exact passages in the applied reference establishing the correct teaching and explain why the prior characterization is incorrect.\n  d) Provide affidavit or declaration (evidence) showing non-analogousness or unexpected technical effects not taught or suggested by the applied references if such evidence is available and appropriate under the statutes and rules.\nNext steps\n- The prior Office action rejections (May 5, 2016) are maintained as to the claims identified in this action. This communication is non-final. The applicant is invited to reply to the maintained rejections by amendment and/or argument and to provide any evidence the applicant wishes the record to consider. The examiner will reconsider the rejections in light of any new claim language, arguments, or evidence submitted by applicant.\nLimitation and record note\n- The examiner's maintained positions are based on the Office action of May 5, 2016 (INUFX76HRXEAPX2) and the applicant's A1 submission (ISS0O53CRXEAPX3 and ISS0O53DRXEAPX3). The entry status of the A1 claims into the official claim record is not confirmed by a subsequent Office action in the record up to the present cutoff; however, the examiner has evaluated the amended claim wording as presented in the A1. If applicant wishes to ensure specific proposed claim language is entered for examination, applicant should confirm entry procedures and, where appropriate, provide marked-up claim sets conforming to the rules for amendments after final or rule-based entry when required.\n- If applicant believes a telephone interview would assist in clarifying claim language or evidentiary issues, the examiner is open to scheduling an interview after receiving a reply or request identifying specific points for discussion.\nConclusion\n- The rejections stated in the May 5, 2016 Office action are maintained as to the claims identified above. This is a non-final action. Applicant is invited to file a response with claim amendments, detailed arguments and/or evidence as indicated above. The examiner will reconsider in view of any substantive, supported reply.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The record does not contain verbatim passages from the applied prior art (MIT, Young, Kobayashi) other than the examiner's characterizations quoted in the May 5, 2016 Office action; the examiner's maintained positions rely on those O1 characterizations.",
    "Entry of the applicant-filed amended claims (ISS0O53CRXEAPX3) into the official claim record is not confirmed by any later Office action in the record up to the cutoff; the examiner has evaluated the amendment as submitted but applicant may need to ensure proper formal entry under the rules if entry is contested."
  ]
}

