  "office_action": "Office Action (Non-Final) — Response to Applicant's A1 Submission\nSummary\nThe Office has considered applicant’s Amendment and Reply filed November 26, 2014 (A1), the previously mailed non-final Office Action dated August 27, 2014 (CTNF; HZCRBTS5PXXIFW4), and the application file record. The earlier Office Action rejected claims 1–12 under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as anticipated by Takahashi (US 2006/0143418). Applicant has amended claim language (proposed claims filed with A1) and argued the cited reference does not teach two distinctions: (1) grouping storage apparatuses by their physical site, and (2) a copy-pair setting policy that explicitly includes an indicator of \"local copy\" versus \"remote copy.\" The Examiner has reviewed the amendments and arguments and responds as follows.\nAmendments and Procedural Status\n- Applicant submitted amended claims and a specification title change in the A1. The record shows the as-filed claims from the original publication (H52R47QJPXXIFW3) remain entered. The applicant-submitted amended claims in I2YYHZE4PXXIFW3 are recorded in the file as proposed amendments with entry status unknown. The Examiner has considered the arguments in A1 but cannot treat proposed claim text as entered until formally entered under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 (or until applicant confirms replacement of the pending claim set). Accordingly, the Examiner proceeds on the basis of the claims of record (the as-filed claims) and addresses applicant's arguments directed to Takahashi.\nSubstantive Response to Applicant's Arguments\n- Applicant's central arguments: Applicant contends Takahashi does not disclose (A) grouping of storage apparatuses by physical site (applicant's term: \"storage apparatuses disposed at an identical site are grouped as an identical storage group\") and (B) a copy pair setting policy that includes explicit local/remote copy information for pairs of virtual volumes.\n- Examiner analysis and determination: The August 27, 2014 Office Action applied Takahashi to claims 1–12. That action relied on Takahashi's disclosures of (i) user-definable grouping of volumes (migration groups and storage layers) and (ii) migration/copying control between volumes. The Office interpreted Takahashi's grouping by network zone or host access reports and its grouping/migration constructs as corresponding to claimed grouping functionality and the ability to effect copy/migration relationships between grouped volumes. On the present record, applicant has not shown that those interpretations are incorrect. Applicant's assertions that Takahashi is \"silent\" about 'site' membership and explicit local/remote copy policy are noted; however, the Examiner continues to find that Takahashi's grouping by network/zone or by host-access (host report) together with its migration control between groups reasonably correspond to the claimed grouping and copy-pair-setting concepts when read in light of the specification. The prior Office Action specifically cited Takahashi's FIG. 1, FIG. 11, and the description of grouping/migration behavior, and concluded those disclosures anticipate the claimed apparatus and method. Applicant has not pointed to a specific portion of Takahashi showing that the cited figures and description are inapposite. The record therefore supports maintaining the prior 35 U.S.C. 102(b) rejections.\nClaim-specific guidance and options for applicant\n- If applicant believes the Examiner’s application of Takahashi is incorrect, applicant should (i) identify precise, numbered paragraph(s) and figure(s) of Takahashi that the applicant asserts do not, in fact, disclose the particular limitation(s) (pinpoint citations), and provide explanation why those portions do not disclose the limitation(s) as applied in the Office action; and/or (ii) provide persuasive claim amendments that clearly distinguish the claim limitations from the Takahashi disclosure. For example, if a \"site\" limitation is intended to mean a particular physical location attribute stored and used by the management apparatus, add explicit claim language requiring a stored 'site identifier' or a specified data structure/field and identify where that support appears in the specification. Likewise, if the copy-pair setting policy must explicitly include a stored \"copy-type\" field indicating 'local' or 'remote', add such a limitation and cite the specification for support.\n- If applicant submits amended claims, please ensure that the proposed claim set is formally entered and that any new limitations have explicit support in the specification (cite paragraph(s) or figure(s)). If applicant requests withdrawal of the pending rejection based on claim amendments, be aware that the Examiner will re-assess novelty and, if applicable, nonobviousness in view of the cited art and any art of record.\nDisposition\n- The pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) rejections of the pending claims 1–12 over Takahashi are maintained on the present record. This communication is a non-final action. Applicant's options include: (1) file an amendment presenting claim language that overcomes the cited Takahashi disclosure (with support citations) and request entry of the amended claims; (2) file a persuasive written argument with pinpoint citations to the Takahashi reference showing the cited portions do not disclose the claimed limitations; (3) request an interview to discuss specific claim language and the cited reference; or (4) if applicant believes Takahashi has been mischaracterized, identify the precise passages and explain the mischaracterization.\n- If applicant elects to amend, include claims in a form suitable for entry and identify the support in the specification for any new or clarified limitations. If amendments are filed, the Examiner will consider them and determine whether any rejection remains appropriate in view of Takahashi and the rest of the application file.\nLimitations and procedural notes\n- The Examiner did not have the full text of Takahashi in the file beyond the prior Office Action’s characterization. Applicant is encouraged to cite specific portions of Takahashi (by paragraph and figure) that are relied upon in prosecution to assist the Examiner in verifying the exact content of that reference.\n- The Examiner has treated the applicant’s November 26, 2014 submission as arguments and proposed claim text. Proposed claims are not entered until formally admitted. If applicant wants the proposed claims entered and considered as the pending claims, inform the Office and provide a clean claim set in compliance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121.\nConclusion / Next steps\n- The rejections under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) over Takahashi are maintained for claims 1–12 on the present record.\n- Applicant is invited to respond by amendment and/or persuasive argument addressing the Examiner’s points above. If applicant elects to file an amendment, please: (a) provide a claim set in formal form for entry; (b) point to the specification support for any new limitations; and (c) if relying on prior art distinctions, provide pinpoint citations to the prior art relied upon.\nExaminer Note: For applicant convenience the Examiner is available for an interview to discuss claim language and the cited Takahashi reference.\nAll correspondence should identify the application number and be directed to the Examiner.",
  "limitations": [
    "The Takahashi reference itself is not in the record as a source document here; the Office Action referenced it and characterized its disclosure. If applicant submits specific Takahashi passages (paragraph/figure citations), the Examiner will reconsider the anticipation analysis accordingly.",
    "Applicant's amended claims in the A1 submission are recorded as proposed amendments with unknown entry status; they have not been treated as formally entered pending applicant request for entry or formal amendment under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121."
  ]
}

