{
  "sample_id": "14523635_t1",
  "next_action_type": "CTNF",
  "claim_ground_evidence": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        15,
        32
      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "IMNS5HH3PXXIFW3"
      ],
      "status": "unresolved",
      "rationale": "Claims 15 and 32 recite a display region/pixels and position marks that are optically read by a pointing device, and a greater total number of position marks in pixels of a color having lower luminosity factor than in pixels of a color having higher luminosity factor. The Examiner's prior cited art combination (Hitachi Maxell JP 2007-133824A and Toshiba Mobile Display JP 2010-60996A) supplies the basic system of an optically-readable position pattern on a display and the presence of sub-pixel structures. It would have been obvious to a person of ordinary skill to implement position marks in a display having colored sub-pixels and to arrange marks non-uniformly among sub-pixels so as to reduce visible unevenness, as suggested by Toshiba's teaching to alter mark/segment placement relative to pixel/sub-pixel structures and Hitachi's teaching of dot/mark formation to encode positions. Thus the claimed distinguishing numeric distribution of marks among lower- vs higher-luminosity sub-pixels is an obvious optimization in view of the combined teachings.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "IIUAYJKCPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claims 1-14 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "pgpub_description",
          "support_span": "the dot patterns are made of a material that absorbs infrared light, and thus, infrared light is not reflected at the dot patterns.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "pgpub_claims",
          "support_span": "each of the pixels includes sub pixels of a plurality of different colors",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        22
      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "IMNS5HH3PXXIFW3"
      ],
      "status": "unresolved",
      "rationale": "Claim 22 requires that at least one position mark is disposed in a light transparent portion inside a pixel. Hitachi teaches dot portions provided in spaces between pixels (dot portions 3 in the Hitachi specification) as a way to suppress optical interference (see Hitachi disclosure). Toshiba discloses segments/structures within pixel areas. Combining these teachings, it would have been an obvious modification to place marks within transmissive regions of sub-pixels (i.e., transparent areas of pixel regions) to enable optical reading while managing optical visibility. Applicant's assertion that Hitachi's teaching of placing dots in spaces between pixels would discourage placing dots within a pixel is noted, but Toshiba's disclosure of structures within pixel/subpixel regions provides motivation to vary location of marks to balance visibility and detection.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "pgpub_description",
          "support_span": "dots 33 are provided in the sub pixels 41",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "pgpub_description",
          "support_span": "dot portions 3 (the alleged position marks) are provided in the spaces 42 between the pixels 41, which can suppress interference patterns caused by optical interference",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "IIUAYJKCPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "TOSHIBA MOBILE DISPLAY teaches each of the pixels includes sub pixels of a plurality of different colors (Pg. 4, \u00061[0026])",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    }
  ],
  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        15,
        32
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues that Toshiba's 'segments' are part of scanning lines/electrodes and are not position marks like those in Hitachi, and thus Toshiba does not teach allocating more marks to lower-luminosity sub-pixels; further, Applicant contends there is no motivation to modify Hitachi's marks distribution based on Toshiba.",
      "response": "The Examiner acknowledges that Toshiba describes segment/line structures (e.g., first segment YS1 and second segment YS2). However, Toshiba also teaches placing structures (features) relative to sub-pixel geometry (see pgpub_description describing dots/sub-pixel placement). The legal question for an obviousness rejection is whether a skilled artisan would have been prompted to combine the teachings of the references to arrive at the claimed arrangement. Toshiba's disclosure of per-subpixel structural variation and Hitachi's disclosure of optically-detectable dot patterns that interact with pixel regions supply a rationale to distribute marks among sub-pixels to reduce visible unevenness. That optimization — allocating more marks to sub-pixels of a color with lower luminosity to mask visible unevenness — is a predictable design choice a person of ordinary skill would make given the combined teachings. Applicant's declaration of functional difference between scanning-line segments and position marks is noted, but does not persuasively negate the combination rationale because Toshiba's teaching shows per-subpixel structural manipulation that can be applied to optically-readable mark placement. Record support: \"dots 33 are provided in the sub pixels 41\" (pgpub_description) and examiner's prior 103 rationale (IIUAYJKCPXXIFW4).",
      "support_doc_id": "pgpub_description",
      "support_span": "dots 33 are provided in the sub pixels 41"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        22
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues that Hitachi's explicit teaching to put dots in spaces between pixels to suppress interference (paragraph [0086]) provides no motivation to place marks inside pixel transmissive regions, and that doing so would impair Hitachi's purpose.",
      "response": "The Examiner finds Applicant's point about Hitachi's preferred placement in spaces between pixels persuasive as to Hitachi alone. However, the combination of Hitachi with Toshiba supplies alternate placement possibilities: Toshiba teaches structures associated with individual sub-pixels (per-subpixel segments) and shows that modifying element placement relative to sub-pixel geometry is within the ordinary skill. In view of Toshiba, a skilled artisan would recognize alternative locations (including within transmissive portions) when seeking to balance optical detectability for a pen-reader and visible impact to the displayed image. Thus the Examiner finds an obvious motivation to locate a mark in a transmissive portion of a pixel in some implementations, and that claim 22 does not recite an unobvious structural exclusion that overcomes the combination rationale. Record support includes Hitachi's teaching regarding dot placement to control interference and Toshiba's per-subpixel structures (pgpub_description and IIUAYJKCPXXIFW4).",
      "support_doc_id": "IIUAYJKCPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "TOSHIBA MOBILE DISPLAY teaches each of the pixels includes sub pixels of a plurality of different colors"
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner Communication\nIntroduction and procedural status\nThis communication follows the Office's January 5, 2016 action (CTNF) in which claims 1-14 (originally filed) were rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103 over Hitachi Maxell (JP 2007-133824A) in view of Toshiba Mobile Display (JP 2010-060996A). Applicant's April 5, 2016 response cancels claims 1-14 and submits new claims 15-34 (amendment IMNS5HH3PXXIFW3) together with arguments (remarks IMNS5HH9PXXIFW3). Claims 15 and 32 are the independent claims added by Applicant.\nSummary of Examiner position\n- Claims 1-14: moot because Applicant cancelled them.\n- New claims 15 and 32 (independents): the Examiner maintains that the combination of the prior art references Hitachi Maxell and Toshiba Mobile Display renders the claimed subject matter obvious under 35 U.S.C. 103. Dependent claims 16-31 and 33-34 rise or fall with independents 15 and 32 and are treated below.\nGrounds and reasoning (35 U.S.C. 103)\n1) Mapping of claimed elements to prior art\n- Display device/display region, pixels, and optically readable position patterns/marks: Hitachi discloses a display device and dot patterns that encode position information and are optically read by a pen/reader. The specification describes dot patterns/dots used to obtain coordinates (pgpub_description: \"the dot pattern is an example of a position information pattern\" and \"the dot patterns are made of a material that absorbs infrared light\").\n- Pixel and sub-pixel structure (colored sub-pixels): Toshiba discloses pixel structures with sub-pixels of different colors and describes structural elements associated with sub-pixel regions (IIUAYJKCPXXIFW4; pgpub_claims; pgpub_description and figures of the published application illustrate per-subpixel geometry).\n- Distribution/placement of marks among sub-pixels: the combined teachings of Hitachi and Toshiba disclose both optically-detectable marks and per-subpixel structural manipulation. A person of ordinary skill would reasonably combine these teachings to locate optically-readable marks in relation to sub-pixel structure to improve pen-readability while reducing visible display non-uniformity.\n2) Motivation to combine and predictable result\n- The references are analogous: both concern display technology and the use of display-surface features to enable optical reading/input. Toshiba's teaching of sub-pixel-related structures provides motivation to consider mark placement with awareness of sub-pixel color/luminosity. Hitachi's teaching about how dots interact with display optics (including reflections/infrared absorption) provides the practical technique for optically encoding local position. Combining these disclosures to alter the distribution of marks among sub-pixels to reduce visible unevenness (for example, placing more marks in sub-pixels of a color having lower luminosity) is a design choice that yields predictable results to a skilled artisan.\n3) Specific claim limitations and Examiner view\n- Claims 15 and 32: recite that a total number of position marks in first pixels (lower luminosity) is greater than in second pixels (higher luminosity). Toshiba and Hitachi together teach per-subpixel placement of marks and the optical behavior of marks; applying the known desire to reduce visible unevenness, a skilled artisan would have been motivated to allocate marks to less visually conspicuous sub-pixels (i.e., lower-luminosity colors). Thus the claimed numeric inequality would have been an obvious optimization.\n- Claim 22 (position mark disposed in a light transparent portion inside a pixel): Applicant argues Hitachi teaches dot placement between pixels to avoid interference and thus opposes placing marks inside pixels. Examiner notes Toshiba's disclosure of sub-pixel-associated structures and concludes that Toshiba provides motivation/teaching to place structures associated with sub-pixel regions, including transmissive portions within a pixel. Therefore, placing a mark within a transmissive portion is a foreseeable design alternative and is an obvious variation when combining the disclosures.\nApplicant's arguments and Examiner response\nApplicant contends that Toshiba's 'segments' are scanning-line structures distinct from Hitachi's position marks and do not teach allocating marks preferentially to particular sub-pixel colors; Applicant argues there is no motivation to alter Hitachi's marks distribution. The Examiner acknowledges the structural differences but finds that Toshiba nonetheless teaches per-subpixel structural manipulation and positioning relative to sub-pixel geometry. That feature supplies a motivation to vary mark placement/distribution in a display that also uses optically-detectable marks (Hitachi). The combination of teachings gives the skilled artisan a reason to place marks so as to reduce visible unevenness, including biasing mark counts toward lower-luminosity sub-pixels. Applicant's point about Hitachi's preferred inter-pixel placement to avoid optical interference is noted, but the Examiner finds Toshiba's per-subpixel teachings supply a credible alternative placement rationale.\nDisposition and next steps\n- The Examiner maintains the rejection of the independent claims 15 and 32 and their dependent claims under 35 U.S.C. 103 over Hitachi Maxell in view of Toshiba Mobile Display for the reasons set forth above.\n- The Applicant is given opportunity to reply further. To traverse the maintained 103 rejection, Applicant may: (a) amend claims to recite additional structural or functional limitations not taught or suggested by the applied references; or (b) present persuasive objective evidence and/or technical reasoning (e.g., declaration by a qualified person) showing that the cited combination is unsound, that the cited references teach away from the combination, or that the particular arrangement yields unexpected results beyond predictable design choice. Applicant may also request an examiner interview to discuss claim language and possible amendment paths to allowance.\nClaim status summary\n- Claims 1-14: cancelled; original 103 rejection moot.\n- Claims 15-34: pending and rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over Hitachi Maxell (JP 2007-133824A) in view of Toshiba Mobile Display (JP 2010-060996A). The Examiner maintains the rejection for the reasons stated.\nReferences relied upon (as in prior action)\n- JP 2007-133824 A (Hitachi Maxell) (discloses ink/dot patterns and optically-readable dot patterns in a display and teaches that dot patterns are made of infrared-absorbing material; see pgpub_description: \"the dot patterns are made of a material that absorbs infrared light, and thus, infrared light is not reflected at the dot patterns\")\n- JP 2010-060996 A (Toshiba Mobile Display) (discloses per-subpixel structures and sub-pixel color arrangement; see IIUAYJKCPXXIFW4 and pgpub_claims stating \"each of the pixels includes sub pixels of a plurality of different colors\")\nProcedural note\nThis is a non-final (but continued) examination communication maintaining the prior art combination rejection as applied to the newly presented claims. Applicant has not persuasively shown entitlement to allowance of claims 15-34 over the cited art in the present record. Applicant may respond by amendment and/or by providing evidence or argument as indicated above. If Applicant files an amendment that places the claims in a condition for allowance, the Examiner will consider allowance; otherwise a final action may follow upon the next appropriate opportunity.\nLimitations\n- The Examiner's reasoning relies on the content of the two cited Japanese patent publications listed above and Applicant's submitted specification and claims. The Examiner's assessment that Toshiba teaches per-subpixel structural variation is drawn from the references on record. If there are additional references or evidence (e.g., priority documents, declarations, or unpublished prosecution history) not of record, the Examiner has not considered them here.\n- The entry status of claims 15-34 is recorded as proposed in Applicant's April 5, 2016 amendment; this Office action treats those claims as pending for examination but does not treat any assertion of claim entry beyond the amendment filing as an allowance of entry. If Applicant believes any procedural defect occurred in claim entry, Applicant should raise it specifically and provide supporting evidence.\nExaminer contact\nIf Applicant wishes to discuss claim amendments or this position, an examiner interview is recommended. The Examiner can be contacted per the previous Office Action information.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "Analysis depends on the two cited JP publications and Applicant's specification; no additional prior art or declarations were of record for the new claims.",
    "Entry status of claims 15-34 is recorded as proposed in the April 5, 2016 amendment; this action treats the proposed claims as pending for examination but does not assume allowance or final entry beyond the filing of the amendment."
  ]
}

