  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues Tamura does not teach distinct \"first communication module\" and \"second communication module\" and that Tamura only discloses a single mobile station without the claimed interplay between two modules (i.e., using an external device connected by a first module to obtain a communication profile to operate a second module).",
      "response": "The Examiner acknowledges Applicant's emphasis on language. However, Claim 1 uses functional claim language (modules configured to perform recited functions). Tamura describes a mobile station that (a) establishes a short-range (Bluetooth) connection to a terminal/device and (b) communicates through a base station to effect or resume data communication. The Examiner construes the claimed “first communication module” as the mobile station's short-range interface and the claimed “second communication module” as the mobile station's base-station interface. Tamura explicitly describes the mobile station mediating Bluetooth short-range sessions (S37-S39) and then transmitting a data-communication resume signal to the base station so that data communication is restarted between the base station and the terminal through the mobile station (S40-S41). These disclosed functions correspond to the claimed arrangement in which an electronic device uses a short-range connection to an external device and supports base-station communication via another interface. Therefore, the Examiner finds Tamura teaches the claimed elements. If Applicant seeks to distinguish by requiring physically separate modules or a specific architecture not recited in the claims, Applicant should propose claim amendments that add such structural limitations (e.g., \"a first transceiver distinct from a second transceiver\" or other structural language).",
