  "office_action": "RESPONSE TO APPLICANT REMARKS (A1) — SUMMARY AND FINDINGS\nThis communication responds to Applicant's February 16, 2017 Remarks (A1) traversing the prior-art anticipation rejections in the Office Action mailed November 16, 2016 (O1). The Examiner has considered the arguments in the Remarks and the claim language as filed. For the reasons explained below, the 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) rejections of Claims 1-3, 8, 11-13 and 18 over Tamura (US 2005/0070336 A1) are maintained. Claims 4-7, 9-10, 14-17 and 19-20 remain objected to as dependent on rejected claims but are allowable only if rewritten in independent form including the limitations of their base claims.\nRESPONDING TO THE APPLICANT'S ARGUMENTS\nApplicant's principal argument is that Tamura fails to teach the claimed distinction between a \"first communication module\" (that establishes a communication channel to an external electronic device based on wired or short-range communications) and a \"second communication module\" (that supports a communication service via a base station), and therefore Tamura cannot disclose a control module that \"obtains a communication profile required for operating the second communication module using the external electronic device connected through the first communication module.\" Applicant asserts Tamura only discloses a single mobile station and not two separate modules operating as in the claim.\nExaminer's analysis and response\n1) Functional claim language and the disclosure in Tamura The claims at issue are written in functional terms (\"first communication module configured to establish...\",\"second communication module that supports...\", and a \"control module configured to obtain...\"). The Examiner construes such functional recitations to be satisfied by structures in the prior art that perform the recited functions. Tamura explicitly describes a mobile station interacting via short-range wireless procedures (Bluetooth) with a terminal device and then transmitting a data-communication resume signal so that data communication is restarted between the base station and the terminal device \"through the mobile station\". The Examiner relies on the Examiner's Office Action record citation of Tamura (O1). The O1 action quotes Tamura describing Bluetooth-based session establishment and the mobile station relaying/resuming data communication with a base station. See the record text relied on in the O1 action: \"Then, the mobile station 2 (server) and the terminal device 3 (client) perform processing of transmission/reception of a data service connection request S37, transmission/reception of a response signal S38 and a session establishment S39 based on the Bluetooth standard procedure, thereby achieving the data communication in the BWC.\" and \"When this data communication is achieved, the mobile station 2 transmits a data communication resume signal to the base station 1 (S40). As a result, the data communication S41 is restarted between the base station 1 and the terminal device 3 through the mobile station 2.\" The Examiner's view is that Tamura discloses the functional arrangement recited in the claims: a device with a short-range interface used to establish a connection to an external device and a base-station interface used to effect a base-station communication, and control/session behavior that relays and resumes communication between the terminal and base station via the mobile station. The Examiner therefore finds Tamura anticipates the functional elements recited in Claims 1 and 11, and the related dependent elements recited in the rejected claims.\n2) Applicant's contention regarding physically distinct modules Applicant appears to argue the claims require a particular physical separation or architectural distinction between the two modules that Tamura does not teach. The present claims do not expressly recite a requirement that the two modules be separate physical devices or separate ICs; rather the claims recite modules by function. Under established examination practice, if Applicant requires a stricter physical/structural limitation (for example, \"a first transceiver distinct from a second transceiver\" or explicitly separate hardware elements), Applicant must amend the claims to include those limitations. If Applicant files amended claims including structural limitations that are not met by Tamura, the Examiner will re-evaluate the prior art with respect to the amended claims.\n3) Dependent claims and claims the Examiner found allowable in O1 The Examiner's prior Office Action explicitly stated that Claims 4-7, 9-10, 14-17 and 19-20 are objected to as depending from rejected claims but would be allowable if rewritten in independent form including their base claim limitations. The Applicant's Remarks acknowledges the Examiner found these claims allowable. That finding stands: those particular claims remain dependent on rejected claims and thus cannot be allowed in the present claim set unless rewritten appropriately.\nDECISION — MAINTENANCE OF 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) REJECTIONS\nFor the reasons above, the Examiner maintains the 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) rejections of Claims 1-3, 8, 11-13 and 18 over Tamura. The Examiner finds Tamura discloses the claimed functional elements for the reasons set forth above and as supported by the record text quoted in the prior Office Action.\nAPPLICANT OPTIONS\n1) Amend the claims If Applicant wishes to traverse the rejection on the ground that the claims should require structural features not found in Tamura, amend the claims to add those structural limitations (for example, explicitly recite physically distinct transceivers, or add structural detail about a separate provisioning/profile manager component). Claims amended to include structural limitations that are not met by Tamura will be examined afresh against the applied prior art and any other art of record. If amendments are made, please provide remarks explaining how the amended language changes claim scope and how the amended claims distinguish over Tamura and other cited art.\n2) Request interview The Examiner is available for an interview to discuss claim scope, claim amendment language, or other matters. Applicant may request an interview; if desired please propose times/dates and the proposed attendees. The Examiner can be contacted as noted in the prior Office Action.\n3) File persuasive reply/evidence If Applicant believes there is a factual error in the Examiner's reading of Tamura or can point to specific portions of Tamura (with full citations to the reference text) that show the Examiner's characterization is incorrect, Applicant may submit a reply with argument and/or supporting evidence. Note that a bare assertion that Tamura lacks the claimed functions without amendment of the claims to add the structural limitation Applicant seeks is not persuasive where the claims are functional in scope and the reference discloses the same functions.\nLIMITATIONS\n- The Examiner's maintained rejections rely on the characterization of Tamura set forth in the earlier Office Action (CTNF O1). The record contains the Examiner's quotations of Tamura (see O1). The complete Tamura publication text is not reproduced in full in the present record documents available in the application file; the Examiner relied on the cited passages set out in the prior Office Action. If Applicant submits the full Tamura reference showing different language or context, the Examiner will consider it.\n- If Applicant files amended claims or submits additional evidence/arguments, the Examiner will consider them and, as appropriate, may enter new or amended grounds of rejection or may allow claims that are patentable over the cited art.\nCONCLUSION\nThe Examiner maintains the 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) rejections of Claims 1-3, 8, 11-13 and 18 over Tamura for the reasons above. Applicant may respond by filing claim amendments, affidavits/arguments, or requesting an interview. If Applicant elects to amend, please include claim remarks explaining how the amendments overcome the applied references.\nExaminer contact information is provided in the prior Office Action. The Examiner is available for telephone interview as requested.\n(End of communication)",
  "limitations": [
    "The Examiner's maintenance of the rejections relies on the characterization of Tamura quoted in the earlier Office Action (CTNF O1). The complete Tamura reference text is not reproduced in full in the present record documents; if Applicant provides additional Tamura text showing differing material, the Examiner will consider it.",
    "If Applicant seeks a narrower claim construction (e.g., physically separate modules), the claims must be amended to recite such structural limitations; current claims are functional in scope and are read accordingly."
  ]
}

