  "office_action": "Examiner Action (Non-Final) — Substantive Response to Applicant's Amendment and Remarks\nSummary\n- Admissions and changes: Applicant amended claims 1–4 and 6. The amendment adding “at least one processor to implement:” to claim 1 is acknowledged. Applicant added a “starting point” and recited that buttons are spaced apart from that starting point in the character pad interface. Applicant provided remarks requesting withdrawal of certain formal objections and argued that the cited references do not disclose the claimed features.\n- Examiner determinations: The earlier rejections under 35 U.S.C. 112(f) (interpretation under the sixth paragraph) and certain 35 U.S.C. 112(b) objections are withdrawn in view of Applicant’s amendments. The prior art rejections under 35 U.S.C. 102 (Shin) and 35 U.S.C. 103 (Kornev) are maintained for the reasons below.\nDetailed Findings and Action\n1) 35 U.S.C. 112(f) / claim-interpretation objection — WITHDRAWN as to claims 1–3\n- Background: In the January 20, 2017 Office action the Examiner held that limitations reciting a \"gesture recognition unit\" and \"control unit\" invoked 35 U.S.C. 112(f) (pre-AIA sixth paragraph) because they used a generic placeholder (\"unit\") with functional language and lacked sufficient structure. The Office action requested identification of corresponding structure in the specification or amendment to avoid application of 112(f).\n- Applicant's amendment: Independent claim 1 was amended to recite \"at least one processor to implement:\" prior to the gesture recognizer and controller functional recitations (J1HXC0SNRXEAPX5). Applicant relied on that amendment to rebut the 112(f) interpretation.\n- Examiner response: The recitation of a structural entity \"at least one processor\" that implements the recited functions provides a concrete structural basis sufficient to withdraw the prior 112(f) interpretation for the current claim set. Therefore the 112(f) interpretational rejection (and the associated requirement to point to corresponding structure in the specification for the previously-protested generic \"unit\" terms) is withdrawn for claims 1–3 as currently amended.\n2) 35 U.S.C. 112(b) / cooperative-relationship objections — WITHDRAWN as to claims 3 and 6\n- Background: The previous Office action found claims 3 and 6 incomplete for omitting the positional/cooperative relationship between the \"reference position\" and the \"button of the character pad\" (i.e., whether the reference position is inside or outside a button). The Examiner identified specification paragraphs [0051]-[0055] as showing the reference position inside a button.\n- Applicant's amendment: Claims 3 and 6 were amended to state that the character pad interface comprises a plurality of buttons spaced apart from the starting point located in the central area of the character pad interface, and the dragging is from the starting point (J1HXC0SNRXEAPX5). This language supplies the positional/cooperative relationship previously identified as missing.\n- Examiner response: The amendment supplies the necessary positional relationship, and the previously issued 112(b) cooperative-relationship objection for claims 3 and 6 is withdrawn.\n3) 35 U.S.C. 102 — ANTICIPATION BY Shin (KR 2010/0064899) — MAINTAINED for claims 1–6\n- Prior Examiner analysis (January 20, 2017) characterized Shin as teaching a touch-screen input object with characters arranged around the input object and a drag/select/release operation that results in character recognition (IY3AQZKSRXEAPX5, paragraph summarizing Shin). The Examiner previously noted Shin's figures and description as teaching an input object with an initial/reference position and character groups corresponding to drag directions; release after drag causes selection/recognition of the character.\n- Applicant's argument: Applicant contends Shin does not disclose a character pad that \"comprises a character pad interface in which a starting point indicating a type of characteristics that is enabled to be input through a currently activated interface is positioned in a central area of the character pad interface,\" and therefore the anticipation should be withdrawn.\n- Examiner analysis and conclusion: The Applicant's amendment narrows claim 1 to recite a starting point centrally located in the character pad interface and that buttons are spaced apart from that starting point. However, Shin's disclosure of a touch-screen input object with characters arranged around the input object, combined with the described drag selection and release behavior, reasonably reads on a character pad that has a central starting region (input object) and selectable groups around it. The Examiner's prior characterization of Shin supports reading Shin as disclosing an input object with surrounding character groups and selection by drag direction back-to-release. On the present record and considering Applicant's arguments, the Examiner finds Shin to disclose or inherently teach the features recited in the claims and therefore maintains the anticipation rejection for claims 1–6.\n- Applicant action suggested: If Applicant believes Shin lacks the recited central starting point or that Shin's structure/operation is meaningfully different, Applicant should (a) identify specific Shin passages and figures that show a contrary structure or operation and explain why those differences are dispositive, or (b) propose further claim amendments to recite additional structural or functional features not present in Shin.\n4) 35 U.S.C. 103 — Kornev et al. (US 2010/0333011) — MAINTAINED for claims 1–2 and 4–5\n- Prior Examiner analysis: The Office action explained that Kornev teaches multi-character selection via sliding gestures and character menus and that it would have been obvious to modify Kornev's four-corner selection to an eight-group arrangement like the claimed one, such that horizontal, vertical and diagonal slide gestures are recognized (IY3AQZKSRXEAPX5). The Examiner provided a rationale: reducing the number of keys on small touch screens motivates arranging more characters per key and using slide gestures in additional directions to select among them.\n- Applicant's argument: Applicant traversed the rejection and emphasized the added central starting point limitation in claim 1.\n- Examiner response: The Examiner maintains that substituting or adapting Kornev's multi-character key arrangement to an eight-character-group arrangement and applying the same slide-selection technique yields the claimed recognition of horizontal, vertical, and diagonal gestures and that displaying the selected character as confirmation is within Kornev's teaching. Therefore the obviousness rejection over Kornev is maintained for claims 1–2 and 4–5 on the present record.\n- Applicant action suggested: Applicant may (a) point to specific, annotated portions of Kornev that they contend do not disclose or suggest the claimed central starting point or other claim limitations, (b) further amend the claims to add limitations not present in Kornev (for example, particular processor operations, temporal ordering, GUI behavior or labeling), or (c) provide persuasive evidence (e.g., expert declaration, contemporaneous industry documents) that the combination would not have been made for the reasons asserted by the Examiner.\nSummary of Examiner disposition\n- Withdrawn: 35 U.S.C. 112(f) interpretation objection (for claims 1–3) and the cooperative-relationship 35 U.S.C. 112(b) objection for claims 3 and 6 have been withdrawn in view of Applicant's amendments.\n- Maintained: Prior art rejections under 35 U.S.C. 102 (Shin) and 35 U.S.C. 103 (Kornev) are maintained for the claims indicated above. The Examiner has relied on the prior office action analysis and the record statements identifying how the cited references teach the claimed concept of a central input region and surrounding selectable character groups chosen by horizontal/vertical/diagonal drag gestures.\nNext steps available to Applicant\n- Amend the claims to further distinguish from Shin and/or Kornev (e.g., add specific processor-implemented steps, GUI element identifiers, temporal confirmation behaviors, or other structural limitations not found in the cited references),\n- Submit a reply arguing why the cited references do not disclose or suggest the claimed features with precise line/figure citations to the references, or\n- File evidence (e.g., annotated prior art, declarations) that supports non-anticipation or non-obviousness.\nLimitations and record notes\n- The Examiner relied on the previously-issued January 20, 2017 Office action for the primary prior-art and 112 analyses. Applicant's amendment adding \"at least one processor\" is credited and led to withdrawal of the 112(f) interpretational ground. The Examiner's maintenance of the 102/103 rejections is based on the Examiner's prior characterization of Shin and Kornev as found in the record (IY3AQZKSRXEAPX5). If Applicant provides pinpointed citation-based argumentation or further amendments, the Examiner will reconsider those in the next Office action.\nConclusion\nClaims 1–6 remain pending. Applicant's amendments cured the earlier 112 procedural/interpretation defects identified in the January 20, 2017 action; those objections are withdrawn. However, the prior art rejections under 35 U.S.C. 102 and 35 U.S.C. 103 remain. Applicant is invited to respond by amendment and/or argument addressing the maintained rejections. The Examiner is amenable to an interview to discuss claim amendments and the cited references.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The Examiner's maintenance of the 102/103 rejections rests on the Examiner's characterization of the cited references in the January 20, 2017 Office action; Applicant may submit pinpointed passages or annotated figures from the cited references to rebut those characterizations.",
    "The record does not include full copies or translations of the foreign reference Shin in the document set provided to the model; the Examiner relied on the Examiner's earlier summary of Shin in IY3AQZKSRXEAPX5."
  ]
}

