  "office_action": "Examiner Action (Non-Final) - Summary and Reasons for Action\nSummary\nThe Office action mailed January 5, 2015 (CTNF) rejected claims 1–19 under 35 U.S.C. 103 and rejected claims 9 and 15 under 35 U.S.C. 112(b). Applicant's reply filed February 27, 2015 amended claims 9 and 15 and traversed the § 103 rejections and the § 112 rejection. Applicant also confirmed election of the process invention (claims 1–19). The amendment to claims 9 and 15 as entered in the record contains unclear/garbled wording and therefore does not cure the previous 112(b) objection. The § 103 rejections are maintained as to the recited claims for the reasons set forth below. This action is non-final; applicant may respond by amendment and/or by persuasive argument or by submission of evidence in support of patentability.\nClaims under Consideration / Current Claim Scope\n- Entered claim set: claims 1–19 entered as originally filed; claim 20 withdrawn (elected process invention I was confirmed by applicant). Applicant's A1 proposed amendments for claims 9 and 15 were filed but the text as recorded in the file is unclear. The Examiner treats the recorded amendment as unclear for examination purposes and requests clarification.\nSubstantive Examination — 35 U.S.C. 112(b)\n- Status: maintained for claims 9 and 15.\n- Reason: The initial Office action identified ambiguity of the term 'low fluorine tungsten.' Applicant amended claims 9 and 15 to avoid 'low fluorine' language. However, the amended claim language as entered in the file is not intelligible (e.g., 'new-fte-iie tungsten' and '4*eva ie- tungsten by a levfucrine-tungsten process'), such that the Examiner cannot determine claim scope. Under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) claims must particularly point out and distinctly claim the invention. Applicant must present clear, conventional claim language. Please submit corrected claims 9 and 15 that unambiguously recite the intended limitation. Suggested clear phrasing (if consistent with applicant intent and supported by the specification) is: 'The method of claim X wherein depositing metal in the trench ... comprises depositing low-fluorine tungsten by a chemical vapor deposition process in which fluorine concentration at the deposition interface is controlled.' If applicant intends alternative terminology or a specific numerical fluorine concentration, please state it and identify corresponding support in the specification.\nSubstantive Examination — 35 U.S.C. 103\n- Status: maintained (non-final) for claims 1–7, 8, 10–19 as previously rejected.\n- Reason (overview): The Examiner's prior analysis combining Choi (US 2014/0179093 A1), Lin (US 2014/0264911 A1), and Cheng (US 2011/0287600 A1) provides articulated reasons with supporting passages for why the claimed limitations would have been obvious. Choi provides the replacement-gate structure, metal fill (including tungsten), and liner context. Lin teaches removal of excess conductive material by planarizing and states such planarizing 'may also be performed with etch-back techniques' and that the planarizing process may 'stop on the polish stop layer.' Cheng demonstrates that selective etch-back chemistries can remove one material (e.g., epitaxial SiGe stressor) while leaving adjacent unmasked metal gate material substantially unaffected, supporting the concept that selective chemistries can be chosen to remove a particular overburden and not attack a liner or other elements. Hirano and Yamaguchi teach known CVD processes for tungsten (including nitrogen-assisted and low-fluorine WF6-based approaches), and Aoyama and Quirk-Serda teach reactive ion etch practices for gate recessing. The Examiner finds that a person of ordinary skill would have had reason to combine these teachings to achieve the claimed result and that the combination would have had a reasonable expectation of success.\n- Specific points in response to applicant arguments:\n  - Applicant: Lin teaches CMP and the optional mention of 'etch-back' does not mean Lin teaches a pure non-mechanical chemical overburden removal as claimed. Examiner: Lin explicitly states that the planarizing process may be performed with etch-back techniques and that the process may stop on a polish-stop layer. That disclosure supports substituting or combining etch-back removal for the overburden removal step; the skilled artisan would understand and be motivated to employ etch-back chemistries to remove excess conductive fill and stop on a liner/polish-stop if desired.\n  - Applicant: Cheng etches SiGe and does not teach etching metals; Cheng's selective etch of SiGe does not demonstrate selective etching of a metal overburden. Examiner: Cheng establishes that selective chemistries and etch-back approaches can be configured to remove one material in the presence of adjacent unmasked materials; it therefore supports the general concept of selective etch-back to remove an overburden while leaving other materials substantially intact. Combined with Lin's etch-back/polish-stop teaching and Choi's gate structure, the combination provides the skilled artisan with a reasonable expectation that an appropriate selective chemistry (as further supported by other cited art for metal etch chemistries) could be used to remove a metal overburden and stop on a liner.\n  - Applicant has not presented persuasive documentary evidence that would rebut the Examiner's combination rationale (such as a contemporaneous teaching-away or experimental data demonstrating that the claimed selective metal overburden removal is inoperable or was unknown); absent such evidence the Examiner maintains the prior rejections.\nAction Required — How Applicant May Respond\n1) Clarify claims 9 and 15: File corrected claim language that unambiguously defines the intended limitation for those claims. If a numerical range for fluorine concentration or a specific process name is intended, recite it and point to support in the specification. This is required to address the maintained 112(b) objection.\n2) To traverse the § 103 rejections: submit persuasive argument and/or evidence explaining why the cited combination does not teach or suggest the claimed subject matter and/or why the combination would not have been made by a skilled artisan. Points that may be persuasive include: showing the cited references teach away from replacing CMP with an etch-back for metal overburden removal; contemporaneous art or data showing etch chemistries selective to tungsten (or other gate metals) while stopping on the recited liner were not known or were inoperative; or amending claims to recite additional limitations that distinguish over the combination (for example claim limitations identifying a specific etchant chemistry, process conditions, polish-stop material combination, or a structural limitation not taught by the cited art).\n3) Amendment option: Applicant may amend claims to include additional limitations supported by the specification to avoid the cited prior art combination. If claims are amended, please provide an election statement if new claim sets implicate the prior restriction requirement.\n4) Request for interview: The applicant requested a telephone interview in A1; the Examiner continues to be available. To schedule, please propose times and an agenda via the electronic file wrapper or by contacting the Examiner; confirm any proposed claim amendments prior to interview to facilitate productive discussion.\nLimitations\n- The record contains the Examiner's prior action (I4CTKN69PXXIFW4) and the applicant's A1 response (I6O2B534PXXIFW3, I6O2B53SPXXIFW3, I6O2B54PPXXIFW3). The A1 amendment text for claims 9 and 15 in the file as entered contains garbled wording; the Examiner has relied on the entered text and thus requests a corrected clear amendment. If applicant submits an unambiguous corrected claim set, the Examiner will reconsider the 112(b) objection in view of that corrected text.\nConclusion\n- The Examiner maintains the non-final obviousness (35 U.S.C. 103) rejections for the indicated claims and maintains the 112(b) objection to claims 9 and 15 as currently entered. This action is non-final. Applicant is invited to respond by amendment and/or by submitting argument or evidence. If applicant files a clear corrected amendment to claims 9 and 15 and/or persuasive evidence or argument distinguishing the claims from the applied references, the Examiner will reconsider the rejections and may withdraw or alter them as appropriate.",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant's A1 amendment for claims 9 and 15 as entered in the record contains garbled wording; Examiner treated the entered text as the operative amendment. Examiner requests a corrected clear amendment.",
    "No additional documentary evidence was submitted by applicant to refute the asserted combination rationale or to show a teaching-away or inoperability of the claimed etch-back for metal overburden removal. If such evidence exists it should be submitted.",
    "The Examiner relied on the prior Office action (I4CTKN69PXXIFW4) and the specification (HLSGYF7RPXXIFW3) for technical support; the Examiner has not introduced new external references beyond those in the prior action."
  ]
}

