      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "Examiner made obviousness rejections over Gasvoda (US 5,949,459) combined with Gilson (US 8,272,704). The examiner identified teachings in Gasvoda of a cartridge that is inserted/pivoted and a seal/ septum and cited Gilson for an operation surface/handle and surface protrusions. The applicant traversed asserting lack of disclosure for (i) lock surface at the upper face in Gasvoda and (ii) that Gilson's operation surface faces the insertion (front) direction rather than the claimed opposite direction. Examiner finds the combination rationale remains: Gasvoda shows a pivoting cartridge with latch features and an external location for the latch; moving a latch location to another outer face is an obvious design choice for the routine mechanical artisan when combined with Gilson's handle/operation surface teaching. Gilson is cited by the examiner as teaching an operation surface at the upper face and with protrusions. Absent persuasive technical evidence or claim amendment establishing patentable distinction, the rejection is maintained.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1",
          "support_span": "Claims 1, 3, 6-7 and 11-13 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as being unpatentable over USP 5,949,459 Gasvoda et al. “Gasvoda” as modified by USP 8,272,704 Gilson et al. “Gilson.”",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1",
          "support_span": "With regard to Claim 11, Gasvoda teaches further comprising a seal member (120 – compliant septum) positioned at the liquid supply portion and having a liquid supply opening formed therethrough, wherein the seal member (120) is configured to contact an outer surface of a liquid supply tube (92) while being elastically deformed when the liquid supply tube (92) is inserted through the liquid supply opening (fig. 7),",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1",
          "support_span": "However, Gilson teaches (figs. 1 and 2) an operation surface (17) positioned at the upper face and positioned further in the second direction. As depicted in the figures the operation surface (17) is extended from furthest point on the sidewall (12). Therefore it would not interfere with Gasvoda’s upper face. Furthermore, Gilson teaches (figs. 1 and 2) wherein the operation surface (17) faces the upward direction and the second direction (opposite the Y direction).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    }
  ],
  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant traverses the 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection over Gasvoda+Gilson arguing: (a) Gasvoda teaches a lock surface at the lower face not the upper face and the examiner's assumption of only two possible placements is incorrect because there are many possible locations; (b) the operation surface of Gilson (surface 17) faces the insertion/front direction rather than the claimed opposite (removal) direction, so Gilson does not supply the claimed operation-surface orientation.",
      "response": "Examiner's position is that the combination of Gasvoda and Gilson still teaches the claimed features or would render them obvious to a person of ordinary skill. The Office action identified Gasvoda as teaching a pivoting cartridge with latch elements and a compliant seal/ septum (support: IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1: \"Claims 1, 3, 6-7 and 11-13 are rejected ... over USP 5,949,459 Gasvoda ...\" and \"Gasvoda teaches further comprising a seal member (120 – compliant septum) ...\" ). The examiner relied on Gilson for an operation surface/handle at the upper face with surface protrusions (support: IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1: \"Gilson teaches (figs. 1 and 2) an operation surface (17) positioned at the upper face ... wherein the operation surface (17) faces the upward direction and the second direction\").\nResponse to (a): The examiner acknowledges there are many possible outer-face locations for a latch in a cartridge design; however, Gasvoda already teaches an arrangement in which a latch feature engages a receiving latching feature in the mounting station. Where a known function (a locking/latching interface) can be implemented at one of several exterior locations on a cartridge without altering the mechanical operation, it is a routine design choice for the skilled artisan to place the latch on a different exterior face for ergonomic, visibility, or packaging reasons. The Office relied on this line of reasoning in the July 29, 2016 action (IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1) and provided a motivation-to-combine: improving user access/operation and providing a handle/location for user interaction (see same doc). Absent persuasive technical evidence in the record that relocating the latch to the upper face creates a new and unexpected function or solves a recognized technical problem in a non-obvious way, the relocation is considered an obvious design alternative under 35 U.S.C. 103.\nResponse to (b): The examiner relied on Gilson to supply the claimed operation-surface structure positioned at the upper face and having protrusions. The characterization in the Office action (IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1) states that Gilson teaches an operation surface positioned at the upper face and facing upward and in the second direction (opposite insertion), and that Gilson's protrusions teach the nonskid/recognizable operation surface. If applicant contends that Gilson's illustrated surface faces the insertion/front direction as a matter of fact, applicant should point to explicit figures/col. line citations in Gilson showing the opposite orientation and explain why the examiner's reading is incorrect. Absent an articulated, reference-specific factual showing that Gilson cannot be read to supply an upper-face operation surface usable to grasp/push the cartridge in the claimed orientation, the examiner's reliance on Gilson as teaching an upper-face operation surface with protrusions remains supported by the record excerpt cited above.",
      "support_doc_id": "IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1",
      "support_span": "Claims 1, 3, 6-7 and 11-13 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as being unpatentable over USP 5,949,459 Gasvoda et al. “Gasvoda” as modified by USP 8,272,704 Gilson et al. “Gilson.”"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1,
        6,
        7,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant states a terminal disclaimer is being filed to overcome the double patenting provisional rejection.",
      "response": "A terminal disclaimer can overcome a nonstatutory double patenting rejection if properly executed and of record. Examiner notes applicant's remark asserting a terminal disclaimer has been filed (IUYJEAEJRXEAPX3: \"a terminal disclaimer is being filed in conjunction with this response to overcome the rejection.\"). However, that terminal disclaimer is not present in the application record accessible to the examiner at cutoff. To resolve the provisional double-patenting rejection, a terminal disclaimer must be timely submitted and entered in the file. If applicant has already filed a terminal disclaimer, submit a copy (or a transaction receipt number) showing it is of record. If a terminal disclaimer is not yet filed, applicant may file a signed terminal disclaimer under 37 CFR 1.321. Until a proper terminal disclaimer is of record, the provisional double-patenting rejection remains outstanding.",
      "support_doc_id": "IUYJEAEJRXEAPX3",
      "support_span": "a terminal disclaimer is being filed in conjunction with this response to overcome the rejection."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner Action (Non-Final) — Response to Applicant's October 31, 2016 Reply\nSummary\n- The July 29, 2016 Office action (restriction and rejections) remains the operative action. Applicant's October 31, 2016 filing amended the claim listing (claims 1–23 as previously presented; some claims previously withdrawn by the Office remain withdrawn) and added proposed claims 24 and 25. Applicant filed Remarks asserting a terminal disclaimer has been filed, and traversed the examiner's 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections. This non-final action continues the outstanding rejections and sets forth the examiner's reasons on the record in light of applicant's response.\nRecord status of applicant-submitted materials\n- I have considered the October 31, 2016 submission (claims listing and remarks). The examiner notes the applicant's remarks state: \"a terminal disclaimer is being filed in conjunction with this response to overcome the rejection.\" (IUYJEAEJRXEAPX3). The file as available to the examiner at cutoff does not show an entered terminal disclaimer document. If applicant has already filed a terminal disclaimer in this application, please provide the transaction receipt or submit a copy to the file to ensure the terminal disclaimer is entered and considered. If no terminal disclaimer has yet been filed, submit a signed terminal disclaimer under 37 C.F.R. 1.321 to overcome any nonstatutory double patenting rejection. Until a terminal disclaimer is of record, the provisional double patenting rejection identified below remains outstanding.\nOutstanding rejections and examiner position\n1) Nonstatutory double patenting (provisional) — claims 1, 6, 7, 12, 13\n- The July 29, 2016 action made a provisional nonstatutory double patenting rejection over certain claims of copending Application No. 14/838,438 (IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1: \"Claims 1, 6, 7, 12 and 13 are provisionally rejected on the ground of nonstatutory double patenting ...\"). Applicant's October 31, 2016 filing indicates a terminal disclaimer is being filed. As noted above, no terminal disclaimer document is of record for the examiner to process. The applicant may: (a) file a signed terminal disclaimer in this application naming the copending application/patent as required, or (b) submit argument and evidence showing patentable distinction between the claims such that a terminal disclaimer is unnecessary. Absent a terminal disclaimer of record or persuasive evidence of patentable distinction, the provisional double-patenting rejection is maintained.\nAction required (double patenting): Within the time set for reply applicant must either:\n- File and have entered a properly signed terminal disclaimer (37 C.F.R. 1.321) identifying the copending application as required; or\n- File a written traverse that specifically points out and substantiates with record evidence why the examiner's double-patenting conclusion is incorrect (i.e., demonstrate patentable distinction); or\n- If applicant believes a terminal disclaimer was already filed, provide a copy or filing receipt showing the terminal disclaimer is of record.\n2) 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections — claims 1, 3, 6, 7, 11-13 (over Gasvoda ± Gilson)\n- The July 29, 2016 Office action rejected claims 1, 3, 6, 7, and 11–13 under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over Gasvoda (U.S. Pat. No. 5,949,459) as modified by Gilson (U.S. Pat. No. 8,272,704) (IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1: \"Claims 1, 3, 6-7 and 11-13 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as being unpatentable over USP 5,949,459 Gasvoda ... as modified by USP 8,272,704 Gilson ...\"). The Office action explained the combination and supplied specific points of comparison including that Gasvoda teaches a pivoting cartridge, a seal/septum, and latch/latching features, and relied upon Gilson for the claimed operation surface positioned at the upper face and for protrusions on that operation surface.\n- Applicant traversed the 103 rejection on two general grounds: (a) that Gasvoda teaches the latch on the lower face and therefore does not disclose a lock surface at the upper face as claimed; and (b) that Gilson's operation surface 17 faces the insertion/front direction, not the claimed opposite (removal) direction, and thus Gilson does not supply the claimed operation-surface orientation. Applicant also argued there are many possible latch placements and criticized the examiner's characterization of 'finite choices.' Applicant pointed to specification passages describing advantages of the claimed arrangement.\nExaminer response to applicant arguments (substantive):\n- Location of lock surface: The examiner recognizes Gasvoda's latch illustration but also observes Gasvoda teaches an ink container with latch features designed to engage corresponding latching features of a receiving station to lock against urging springs (IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1: \"A known apparatus ... the latching features of the ink container engage the corresponding latching features of the container receiving portion, and thereby the ink container is locked ...\"). The claimed feature is a lock surface on an upper face configured to contact a lock portion. Relocating a known exterior latch/contacting surface from one exterior face to another exterior face to improve accessibility, visibility, or user ergonomics is a routine and predictable design choice for one of ordinary skill in this mechanical arts area when the underlying function (a latch that contacts a lock portion) is preserved. The Office acknowledged this line of reasoning in the July 29, 2016 action and applied it in forming the obviousness combination. Absent persuasive evidence in the record that moving the lock surface to the upper face produces unexpected results or a new non-obvious function, the relocation is an obvious design alternative.\n- Gilson's operation surface orientation and protrusions: The July 29, 2016 Office action relied on Gilson for an operation surface/handle at the upper face and for providing surface protrusions (IR4WGVTCRXEAPX1: \"Gilson teaches (figs. 1 and 2) an operation surface (17) positioned at the upper face ... wherein the operation surface (17) faces the upward direction and the second direction ...\"). If applicant believes the cited portions of Gilson were misread and can point to explicit passages in Gilson showing the operation surface faces the insertion/front direction only (with specific col/line or figure detail), applicant may supply those exact Gilson citations and an explanation. To rebut the examiner's combination, applicant must show that Gilson cannot reasonably be read to supply an upper-face operation surface usable to provide the claimed downward/forward release force or that combining Gilson's upper-face operation surface with Gasvoda's cartridge would be precluded by some factual teaching or technical incompatibility.\n- Claim-specific notes: Claim 11 (seal member/pivot center) was supported by Gasvoda's teaching of a compliant septum/seal and a supply opening; the Office action identified those teachings and applied them to the pivot-center claim limitation. Claims 6 and 7 (operation surface with protrusions/elongated protrusions) were rejected in view of Gilson's surface protrusions. Claim 12 (operation surface fixed relative to liquid chamber) was addressed by combining Gasvoda with Gilson's integrated handle teaching.\nConclusion on 103 rejections: Applicant's general traverses do not, on the present record, supply the necessary documentary or factual showing to overcome the prima facie case. The examiner therefore maintains the 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections against the listed claims. If applicant provides: (i) claim amendments that avoid the recognized teachings of the cited references or (ii) persuasive evidence or detailed argument showing the cited references do not, in fact, disclose or render obvious the specific claimed limitations (with precise citations to the prior art and explanation), the examiner will reconsider. Applicant is invited to (and will expedite prosecution if) provide either claim amendments or focused rebuttal evidence/argument.\nAllowable subject matter / dependent claim treatment\n- The July 29, 2016 action indicated that claims 2, 4, 5, and 8–10 were objected to as depending on rejected base claims but would be allowable if rewritten in independent form including the limitations of the base claims. The examiner stands by that position: those dependent claims contain limitations not found in the applied art and would be allowable if the base claim became allowable or if rewritten in independent form incorporating the base claim limitations.\nNew claims (24, 25)\n- Applicant added claims 24 and 25 on October 31, 2016 dependent on claim 1. Those claims are noted but have not been examined substantively at this point. Their entry is not presumed. If applicant wishes the new claims to be entered for examination, applicant should confirm they are to be entered and, if necessary, provide any required fees or a statement under 37 C.F.R. 1.116 regarding late claim entry. The examiner will consider them for entry and examination once they are formally of record.\nAction required (patentability rejections):\n- For 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections: Within the time period for reply applicant may:\n  - Amend the claims to place them in condition for allowance (e.g., add limitations overcoming the cited art), and/or\n  - Present persuasive, claim-specific argument and supporting evidence (prior-art citations, affidavits, or technical data) showing the combination does not teach or suggest the claimed limitations or that there are unexpected results or secondary considerations of non-obviousness; and/or\n  - Request an interview with the examiner to discuss the rejections and claim amendments.\n- For the nonstatutory double-patenting issue: See above — a terminal disclaimer must be filed and entered in the record or applicant must persuasively traverse the rejection.\nProcedural notes\n- If applicant intends to rely on the terminal disclaimer referenced in the remarks, please ensure a copy or proof of filing is promptly placed in the file so the examiner can withdraw the provisional double patenting rejection, if appropriate.\n- If applicant believes any factual assertions in the July 29, 2016 Office action are mistaken (for example, the examiner's reading of Gilson's drawings or Gasvoda's latch placement), applicant should point to the specific passages or figure callouts in those references to support the contention. Mere attorney argument is insufficient without factual support when the prior art references themselves can be cited and shown inconsistent with the examiner's reading.\nConclusion and disposition\n- The non-statutory double-patenting provisional rejection remains outstanding until a terminal disclaimer is of record or applicant's traverse is accepted. The 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections over Gasvoda ± Gilson are maintained on the present record. The examiner is amenable to reconsideration upon receipt of claim amendments or persuasive factual/technical evidence. Applicant is encouraged to respond with either claim amendments putting the claims in condition for allowance, a timely-filed terminal disclaimer (if intended), or specific evidence/argument addressing the points raised above.\nExaminer contact\n- If applicant believes a telephone interview would expedite resolution, request one and suggest times. The examiner contact information is set out in the prior Office actions.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "No terminal disclaimer document is present in the record available to the examiner at cutoff; applicant's remark states a terminal disclaimer \"is being filed,\" but proof of filing or the signed terminal disclaimer itself is not of record. Examiner cannot treat the DP rejection as withdrawn until a terminal disclaimer is entered.",
    "The examiner's 103 rejections are based on the examiner's reading of the cited prior art as summarized in the July 29, 2016 action; the examiner does not have additional prior-art documents beyond those cited in that action in the current record.",
    "Applicant-provided broad traversals must be supported by specific citations or evidence from the prior art to overcome the prima facie case; the record currently lacks such targeted, reference-specific evidence."
  ]
}

