ation. Applicant is reminded of the avenues available when a restriction is final: petition (if appropriate) or pursuing the elected group(s) (here apparatus/method). The record shows Applicant elected Group I previously and continued prosecution in that group (see Applicant’s election documents). The Examiner remains willing to discuss these matters at an interview if Applicant requests one.\n3) Obviousness rejections (pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a)) — claims 21–42\n- The Office has applied Liebeskind (U.S. Pat. No. 2,979,246) as primary prior art and has explained the rationale for rejection. The basis is that Liebeskind discloses a carton/blank handling line in which cushioning/adhesive material is applied at predetermined locations on blanks and the locations/positions for deposit are disclosed; further, converting or modifying a dispensing/deposit station into a discrete-piece loader and adding a movable gripper to pick and place discrete reinforcements at the same locations would have been a routine engineering modification within ordinary skill (see Office action reasoning in I4TVSPHWPXXIFW4). The Office also takes official notice that optical imaging/inspection to monitor machine operations and identify visual markers is well known in the art, so adding cameras/readers to locate printed or incised locators or to monitor placement is an obvious design choice to improve accuracy and control.\n- For the record: the Office relied upon the analysis in the prior action and the statement that “Claims 1–19 ... rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Liebeskind (U.S. Patent No. 2,979,246)” (I4TVSPHWPXXIFW4). The Office also stated: “It would have been obvious ... to have modified Liebeskind’s gripping means to be a moveable means to move between a first and second positions...” and that adding optical viewing means is within routine engineering design choices (I4TVSPHWPXXIFW4). Those rationales are applied to the present new apparatus and method 