  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [1,16],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues that, similar to Example 21 of USPTO guidance, the claims recite a particular inventive combination (right of first refusal, remote betting terminals, computer-based jackpot handling) that solves a real-world, Internet-centric problem and thus are patent eligible.",
      "response": "The Examiner acknowledges Applicant's reliance on the July 2015 USPTO Update Example 21. However, the present claims differ materially from Example 21. Example 21 included a combination of specific computerized steps that solved an Internet-centric problem by activating a client application to receive time-sensitive information when the client becomes online. Here, the claims recite (i) the abstract idea of offering a right of first refusal and allowing a remote second player to place a jackpot bet based on combinations of conventional game cards/dice, and (ii) use of generic computer components (a non-transitory computer readable medium, betting terminals). The amendment to recite a \"non-transitory computer readable medium\" addresses the Examiner's transitory-signal concern but does not supply the 'inventive concept' required by Alice step two because the claims do not set forth specific computer architecture, unconventional programming, or other improvements in computer technology. Merely applying the abstract idea using generic computing devices or storage does not make the claims patent eligible. Applicant may overcome the 101 rejection by amending claims to include specific, non-conventional technical features (for example, particular data structures, algorithms implemented in software that are described in the specification as providing improved computer operation, or a particular novel interaction between components), or by presenting persuasive arguments with supporting record evidence that the claimed ordered combination produces a concrete technological improvement beyond conventional automation of an abstract idea.",
      "support_doc_id": "IHLYW39ZPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Claims 1-16 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 101 because the claimed invention is directed to non-statutory subject matter."
    },
    {
