      "argument_summary": "Applicant requested the double patenting requirement be held in abeyance until allowance of a single claim; terminal disclaimer to be deferred.",
      "response": "The Office may consider abeyance of the double patenting rejection in limited circumstances when a clear, singular claim is proposed that would avoid the double patenting issue. Here, Applicant did not (a) submit a replacement single claim expressly identified as the one to be examined for allowance, (b) file a terminal disclaimer, nor (c) show how the amended claims are patentably distinct from the cited U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564. Consequently, the double patenting rejection is maintained. If Applicant believes that claim amendments have rendered the claims patentably distinct from U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564, Applicant should (1) identify the specific claim(s) in the '564 patent relied upon for the rejection and explain, claim-by-claim, why the pending claims are patentably distinct (pointing to claim language and the record), or (2) file a statutory terminal disclaimer under 37 C.F.R. 1.321 to obviate the nonstatutory double patenting rejection.",
      "support_doc_id": "IMOXC29EPXXIFW1",
      "support_span": "Applicant requests the double patenting requirement be held in abeyance until allowance of a single claim, as claim amendments may obviate the need for the terminal disclaimer."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "DETAILED ACTION\nThis communication responds to Applicant’s Amendment and Response filed April 4, 2016 and to the earlier Office action mailed December 2, 2015. The application file indicates Applicant submitted: (i) claim amendments cancelling claim 15 and amending claims 1 and 16 to recite determination of an outcome “by a non-transitory computer readable medium,” (ii) a corrected ADS, and (iii) a replacement IDS that includes a full copy of WO 2005/065793. The ADS correction and IDS filing are noted and will be entered as file-wrapper items. The remaining substantive issues are addressed below.\nI. Claims affected\nCurrent pending independent claims: 1 and 16 (as amended April 4, 2016). Dependent claims 2–14 remain of record. Claim 15 has been cancelled and is no longer pending.\nII. 35 U.S.C. 101 — subject matter eligibility (maintained)\nSummary of record: The prior Office action asserted that claims 1–16 were directed to non-statutory subject matter and explained that merely reciting generic computer components does not render an abstract idea patent eligible (see Office action mailed 11/25/2015: “Claims 1-16 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 101 because the claimed invention is directed to non-statutory subject matter.”). Applicant amended claims 1 and 16 to add “by a non-transitory computer readable medium” and argued that the claims are analogous to Example 21 of the July 2015 USPTO Update, which the applicant contends supports eligibility.\nExaminer's analysis and conclusion: The insertion of “non-transitory computer readable medium” into claims 1 and 16 addresses the Examiner’s prior transitory-signal concern for claims that could have been read to cover a transitory signal per se. However, addition of that recitation alone does not supply an inventive concept under step two of the Alice/Mayo framework.\nStep 1 (Alice step one): The claims are directed to the abstract idea of organizing and managing wagering rights and outcomes — specifically, granting a right of first refusal to a player who has placed a table-game bet and permitting a remote second player at a separate betting terminal to place a jackpot bet if the first player declines, and determining jackpot outcomes based on combinations of conventional game cards or dice. The claimed subject matter is essentially a scheme for conducting wagering and allocating jackpot awards based on card/dice combinations — an organizational/business-type concept and method of managing a game.\nStep 2 (Alice step two): The claims recite additional elements such as a non-transitory computer readable medium and betting terminals. These elements are described at a high level and are generic computer components and conventional terminals as claimed. There is no claim limitation to a particular novel data structure, specialized hardware arrangement, low-level algorithm, or other unconventional computer implementation that is described in the specification and that provides a technical improvement in the functioning of a computer or the operation of the gaming system itself. The mere use of a “non-transitory computer readable medium” to store instructions that perform the abstract idea is well established as insufficient to supply an inventive concept if the medium is used in its generic sense. The pending claims therefore do not recite additional elements that, taken individually or as an ordered combination, transform the abstract idea into a patent-eligible application. The 101 rejection is accordingly maintained for independent claims 1 and 16 and dependent claims that fall with them.\nWhat Applicant may do to traverse this ground:\n- Amend claims to recite specific, non-conventional computer-implemented features that are described in the specification and that provide a technical solution to a technical problem. Examples: a particular data structure for tracking card sequences that reduces computational complexity; a specific algorithm that improves accuracy or speed in determining jackpot combinations and is supported by the specification; specialized hardware components or unconventional integration of server, betting terminals, and dealing-table control unit that enable new technical functionality; specific communication protocols or dataflows that solve a stated technical problem and are not generic. If such features are present in the specification, point to the specification passages and amend claims to recite them.\n- Alternatively, present persuasive, claim-specific arguments supported by the specification or other evidence showing that the claim limitations, in combination, produce a concrete technological improvement beyond conventional automation of a wagering scheme.\nAbsent such an amendment or persuasive showing, the 101 rejection will be maintained.\nIII. Nonstatutory double patenting (maintained)\nSummary of record: The earlier Office action placed Applicant on notice of a nonstatutory double patenting rejection over claims 1–11 of U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564 (see Office action mailed 11/25/2015: “Claims 1-16 are rejected on the ground of nonstatutory double patenting as being unpatentable over claims 1-11 of U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564.”). Applicant requested that the double patenting requirement be held in abeyance until allowance of a single claim, noting that amendments may obviate the need for a terminal disclaimer (see Applicant remarks filed 04/04/2016: “Applicant requests the double patenting requirement be held in abeyance until allowance of a single claim, as claim amendments may obviate the need for the terminal disclaimer.”).\nExaminer's analysis and conclusion: The Office may grant temporary abeyance of a double patenting rejection in limited situations when there is a clear record showing that the pending claims as amended are no longer patentably indistinct from the earlier patent claims, or when Applicant promptly files a terminal disclaimer. Here, Applicant has not filed a terminal disclaimer and has not submitted a focused, claim-by-claim showing of patentable distinctness from the cited claims of U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564. The mere request for abeyance without either (1) a terminal disclaimer, or (2) a detailed explanation supported by the record showing how the amended claims are patentably distinct from the cited patent is insufficient. Therefore, the nonstatutory double patenting rejection is maintained as to all outstanding claims that were rejected on that ground.\nOptions for Applicant to address the double patenting rejection:\n- File a terminal disclaimer under 37 C.F.R. 1.321 to obviate the double patenting rejection. The terminal disclaimer must meet the statutory and rule requirements, including proper signature and any required showing of common ownership, as outlined in the prior Office action.\n- Alternatively, amend the claims to make them patentably distinct from the claims of U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564 and submit a clear explanation identifying the specific differences and why those differences render the claims patentably distinct. If such amendments and explanations are submitted, the Examiner will reconsider the double patenting rejection.\nIV. 35 U.S.C. 112(d) / dependent claim form\nClaim 15 was previously objected to under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 112, fourth paragraph / 35 U.S.C. 112(d) for being in improper dependent form. Applicant has cancelled claim 15 in the April 4, 2016 amendment. Because claim 15 is no longer pending, the earlier 112(d) objection to claim 15 is moot.\nV. Procedural matters entered in the file\n- ADS correction: The file indicates Applicant filed a corrected ADS; that correction is accepted and entered. (Applicant indicated correction during April 4, 2016 submission). See IMOXC29EPXXIFW1: “Priority Objection Applicant has submitted a corrected Application Data Sheet to correct the typographical error.”\n- IDS: Applicant submitted a replacement IDS containing a full copy of WO 2005/065793. The Examiner acknowledges receipt. See IMOXC29EPXXIFW1: “A new Information Disclosure Statement is submitted herewith with a full copy of WO 2005/065793.”\nVI. Summary of outstanding rejections\n- 35 U.S.C. 101: Claims 1–14 and 16 — maintained (see section II above). Applicant may amend claims to recite specific technical improvements or provide supporting evidence/arguments as noted above.\n- Nonstatutory double patenting (dp) over U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564: Claims 1–14 and 16 — maintained. Applicant may file a terminal disclaimer or demonstrate patentable distinctness as explained above.\nVII. Next steps and response requirements\nApplicant is invited to: (1) amend the claims to present specific technical features (not merely generic computer components) that are supported by the specification and that address the 101 rationale above; and/or (2) file a terminal disclaimer to overcome the double patenting rejection; and/or (3) submit a persuasive claim-by-claim argument, supported by citations to the specification and the record, explaining why the pending claims are patentably distinct from U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564. If Applicant elects to submit claim amendments, please highlight or clearly identify the changes and provide clean and marked claim sets. If Applicant files a terminal disclaimer, the Examiner will withdraw the double patenting rejection upon entry of a proper terminal disclaimer. If Applicant desires further clarification of the 101 reasoning as applied to particular claim language, an interview may be requested. Any response should be timely filed. Failure to reply within the statutory or extended period will result in entry of an appropriate action, which may include final rejection of the outstanding grounds.\nLimitations:\n- The file does not include the full text of U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564 in this application’s file wrapper. The double patenting rejection is based on the cited patent as identified in the prior Office action. If Applicant believes a particular portion of the '564 patent distinguishes the pending claims, applicant should cite those portions and explain differences.\n- Examiner’s characterization of the claims for 101 purposes is based on the broadest reasonable interpretation of the pending claim language in light of the specification and the case law cited in the Office action. If Applicant contends a narrower construction applies, Applicant should amend claims to make that narrowing explicit.\nThis communication is made in accordance with the examiner’s duties and the MPEP. The prior Office action (mailed 11/25/2015) remains of record. If Applicant wishes to discuss any of the above matters, an interview may be scheduled with the examiner.\n/William H. McCulloch/\nPrimary Examiner, Art Unit 3714",
  "limitations": [
    "The record supplied to the Examiner does not include the cited U.S. Patent No. 8,668,564 text in the application file submitted here; the double-patenting ground is taken from the earlier Office action and would benefit from Applicant pointing to specific claim language or filing a terminal disclaimer.",
    "The current determination under 35 U.S.C. 101 is made on the claim language as amended and the specification of record; if Applicant asserts additional specific technical details in the specification support eligibility, Applicant should point to those exact passages or amend to claim them."
  ]
}

