{
  "sample_id": "13587693_t1",
  "next_action_type": "CTNF",
  "claim_ground_evidence": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        17,
        19,
        20
      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "I3TANKNIPXXIFW3"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "Independent claim 17 (as amended) and dependent claims 19 and 20 remain rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 over the cited prior art. The Examiner finds that the combination of the prior art references on the record would have suggested the claimed relationships between the inner cover flexural modulus (M1), the outer cover flexural modulus (M2), and the inner core flexural modulus (where recited). The record contains specific teachings of cover-layer flexural modulus ranges in Mayer and Sullivan that overlap or can be combined to meet the claimed numeric differences, and the Applicant has not supplied evidence sufficient to overcome the articulated obviousness rationale.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "I06NA0RDPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Mayer discloses ... an inner cover layer having a flexural modulus between 70,000 to 120,000 psi (par. [0056]); and an outer cover layer having a flexural modulus between 1,000 to 30,000 (par. [0038]; noting the inner cover can be 80,000 psi and the outer cover can be 10,000 psi, so 70,000 \u00096 10,000 = 60,000 psi which satisfies the limitation).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "I06NA0RDPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Sullivan also makes obvious claims 17, 19 and 20. Regarding claim 17, Sullivan discloses the four piece ball (Fig. 1), the ability to make the inner cover layer a high modulus material of 70,000 to 120,000 psi (par. [0062]) and the outer cover layer a low modulus material of less than 30,000 psi (par. [0009]).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    }
  ],
  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        17
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues that Mayer and Sullivan do not teach or provide motivation to select the claimed numeric difference (e.g., \"at least about 45,000 psi greater\") between M1 and M2, and that Mayer and Sullivan teach a variety of materials and do not link the claimed relationship to the asserted benefits; Applicant contends the references may teach away.",
      "response": "The prior art disclosures on the record identify explicit ranges for flexural modulus for inner and outer cover layers that overlap with values that, when combined, yield the claimed differences. The Examiner's prior action explained that Mayer teaches inner cover flexural modulus values of 70,000 to 120,000 psi and outer cover flexural modulus values of 1,000 to 30,000 psi and noted that selecting a representative inner-cover value (e.g., 80,000 psi) with a representative outer-cover value (e.g., 10,000 psi) results in a numeric difference within the claimed ranges (e.g., 60,000 psi). See Office action (9/15/14) quotation: \"Mayer discloses ... an inner cover layer having a flexural modulus between 70,000 to 120,000 psi (par. [0056]), and an outer cover layer having a flexural modulus between 1,000 to 30,000 (par. [0038]; noting the inner cover can be 80,000 psi and the outer cover can be 10,000 psi, so 70,000 \u00096 10,000 = 60,000 psi which satisfies the limitation).\" Where the prior art discloses overlapping numeric ranges for related parameters, it is proper to conclude that a skilled artisan would have had reason to select values within those ranges for the known purpose of optimizing ball performance (e.g., resilience and feel), absent convincing evidence to the contrary. Applicant's assertion that the references do not explicitly state the claimed numeric difference does not negate the factual basis that the references disclose ranges that would yield the claimed relationship, nor does Applicant point to record evidence showing unpredictability or that the skilled artisan would be discouraged from selecting such values. The Examiner therefore maintains the 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection for claim 17.",
      "support_doc_id": "I06NA0RDPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Mayer discloses ... an inner cover layer having a flexural modulus between 70,000 to 120,000 psi (par. [0056]), and an outer cover layer having a flexural modulus between 1,000 to 30,000 (par. [0038]; noting the inner cover can be 80,000 psi and the outer cover can be 10,000 psi, so 70,000 \u00096 10,000 = 60,000 psi which satisfies the limitation)."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        19,
        20
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant traversed the obviousness rejections over Mayer and Sullivan for dependent claims 19 and 20, raising similar points about lack of motivation and that the references do not teach the claimed numeric differences.",
      "response": "The Examiner's rationale likewise applies to claims 19 and 20. The cited passages in Mayer and Sullivan disclose modulus ranges for inner cover, outer cover, and inner core that overlap the numerical criteria recited in claims 19 and 20. The Examiner has specifically identified combinations of values within those disclosed ranges (e.g., inner cover 80,000 psi; outer cover 10,000 psi; inner core 75,000 psi) that meet the claimed numerical differences. In the absence of evidence showing the selection would be unpredictable, inoperable, or contrary to the teachings of the references, the Examiner maintains that the combination of disclosures would have rendered the claimed numerical differences obvious to a person of ordinary skill in the art.",
      "support_doc_id": "I06NA0RDPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Mayer discloses ... an inner cover layer having a flexural modulus between 70,000 to 120,000 psi (par. [0056]), and an outer cover layer having a flexural modulus between 1,000 to 30,000 (par. [0038]; noting the inner cover can be 80,000 psi and the outer cover can be 10,000 psi, so 70,000 \u00096 10,000 = 60,000 psi which satisfies the limitation)."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner Action: Non-final rejection maintained - 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections remain for claims 17, 19 and 20\nSummary\n- Applicant filed a Reply under 37 C.F.R. § 1.111 (12/17/2014) amending the claims and arguing against the outstanding 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections over Mayer and Sullivan. Claims 1-16 and 18 remain withdrawn (election without traverse). Claim 17 is the sole independent claim under examination, with dependent claims 19 and 20 outstanding under the 103 rejections.\n- After consideration of the Applicant's arguments and the claim amendments (claims 21-23 added), the Examiner maintains the rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 for claims 17, 19 and 20 over the cited prior art on the record (Mayer and Sullivan). This non-final action explains the reasons and points to the record support relied upon.\nDiscussion of the 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections and Applicant's arguments\n1) Rejection maintained: claims 17, 19 and 20 over Mayer (US 2008/0009371 A1) and over Sullivan (US 2004/0214661 A1)\nRecord basis relied upon: The Office action dated 9/15/2014 explains the factual basis for the rejections and identifies specific modulus ranges disclosed in the prior art that overlap with and would lead a person of ordinary skill to the claimed numeric relationships. Representative record language relied upon includes the Examiner's statement that \"Mayer discloses ... an inner cover layer having a flexural modulus between 70,000 to 120,000 psi (par. [0056]), and an outer cover layer having a flexural modulus between 1,000 to 30,000 (par. [0038]; noting the inner cover can be 80,000 psi and the outer cover can be 10,000 psi, so 70,000 \u00096 10,000 = 60,000 psi which satisfies the limitation).\" The record also notes Sullivan's disclosure of inner cover modulus values of 70,000 to 120,000 psi and outer cover modulus values of less than 30,000 psi (see prior Office action and applicant's IDS citing Sullivan).\nExaminer's response to Applicant's arguments\nApplicant's principal argument is that Mayer and Sullivan do not teach or provide motivation to select the claimed numeric differences (e.g., \"at least about 45,000 psi greater\") and that the references supply no link between the numeric differences and the asserted benefits. Applicant further argues that Mayer and Sullivan provide broad material disclosures and that the references may teach away from a particular relationship.\nThe Examiner's position is as follows:\n- The record shows that Mayer and Sullivan disclose specific, overlapping numeric ranges for flexural modulus of inner cover layers and outer cover layers. Where prior art references disclose ranges for related parameters, a skilled artisan would have had reason to select values from those ranges for the known purpose of optimizing ball performance (resilience/feel), absent persuasive evidence demonstrating unpredictability or a teaching away that would have discouraged selection. The Examiner has identified concrete combinations of values drawn from the disclosed ranges that meet the claimed differences (for example, inner cover 80,000 psi and outer cover 10,000 psi yields a 70,000 \u00096 10,000 = 60,000 psi difference, which is within the claimed numeric differences recited by the dependent claims and supports the asserted obviousness). See previous Office action quotation: \"Mayer discloses ... an inner cover layer having a flexural modulus between 70,000 to 120,000 psi (par. [0056]), and an outer cover layer having a flexural modulus between 1,000 to 30,000 (par. [0038]; noting the inner cover can be 80,000 psi and the outer cover can be 10,000 psi, so 70,000 \u00096 10,000 = 60,000 psi which satisfies the limitation).\"\n- Applicant has not presented objective evidence (e.g., experimental data, affidavits from skilled artisans, contemporaneous industry publications) on the record showing that the claimed numeric differences are unpredictable, inoperable, or would have been discouraging to a skilled artisan. Nor has Applicant pointed to language in Mayer or Sullivan that specifically teaches the claimed numeric differences as novel and nonobvious or that would instruct away from the proposed combinations. Generalized assertions that the references are silent on the exact numbers do not rebut the fact that the references disclose ranges from which the claimed numbers can be selected.\n- Where the prior art suggests using a hard inner cover and a soft outer cover for improved feel and play (teachings common to the cited references), selecting particular numerical values within the broadly disclosed ranges to obtain a claimed numerical difference is an application of routine optimization by one skilled in the art. The Examiner views the numerical selections at issue as within the ordinary skill of the artisan given the explicit ranges in the references and the shared objective of improving resilience and feel.\nConclusion and next steps\n- The 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections of claims 17, 19 and 20 over Mayer and over Sullivan are maintained in this non-final action.\n- Applicant may reply by submitting additional substantive evidence and/or claim amendments that overcome the rejections. Examples of responsive submissions that may overcome the current grounds include, but are not limited to:\n  - Evidence (declarations, experimental data) demonstrating unexpected results or criticality of the claimed numeric differences that would not have been predictable from the cited prior art; or\n  - Amendments that add limitations not taught or suggested by the cited art (for example, further structural features, materialidentified properties, or manufacturing steps) so as to distinguish over Mayer and Sullivan.\n- If Applicant intends to traverse the maintained rejections, any traverse should expressly point out alleged errors in the Examiner's factual findings, identify supporting evidence of record, and submit any new evidence of patentability. Bare assertions without supporting evidence are insufficient to overturn a prima facie case of obviousness where the references disclose overlapping numeric ranges whose combination would have been within the skill of the ordinary artisan.\n- The Applicant should also be aware that claims 21-23 (new dependent claims added 12/17/2014) have been received and will be considered in further prosecution only insofar as they depend from claim 17; they do not by themselves overcome the stated 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections because they recite values that fall within the ranges already taught by the art on record (e.g., claim 23's 77,000 psi and 550 psi are examples of values already identified as within Mayer's and/or the specification's ranges). If Applicant wishes to rebut the Examiner's combination rationale, Applicant should present evidence showing that those particular numeric selections yield unexpected properties not suggested by the prior art.\nFormalities and procedural points\n- Claims 1-16 and 18 remain withdrawn as being directed to the non-elected species (applicant elected species B without traverse). Claim 17 remains in examination with the above-standing rejections.\n- Applicant will be given time to respond to this non-final action. If Applicant files a reply claiming allowance, the reply should address all outstanding grounds and include any supporting evidence necessary for the Examiner to consider allowance.\nLimitations and record constraints\n- The Examiner's position is based on the references and statements already of record (see Office action 9/15/2014 and the specification/claims as filed). Full text of Mayer or Sullivan is not reproduced in this action; the Examiner relies on the cited passages already entered in the record and on the prior search results. If Applicant contests the Examiner's characterization of the cited references, Applicant should point specifically to the passages in the references (or submit copies or translations if necessary) and explain why the characterization is incorrect.\nThis is a non-final action. The Examiner is willing to confer with the Applicant or the Applicant's representative to clarify the record and discuss possible claim amendments or evidence that might place the claims in condition for allowance.\n(End of action.)",
  "limitations": [
    "The prior-art references Mayer and Sullivan are represented by examiner characterizations and partial quotations in the record; full copies of those references were not included in the documents supplied here. If Applicant disputes the Examiner's characterization, Applicant should point to specific passages in the actual prior art documents or submit copies.",
    "Applicant's election without traverse resulted in withdrawal of claims 1-16 and 18; the record does not contain a successful traverse to restore those claims.",
    "No new experimental or declaration evidence was submitted by Applicant in the A1 reply that would rebut the Examiner's factual basis for the 103 rejections."
  ]
}

