e without identifying the structural elements that produce that result exacerbate the lack of clarity. The fact that the Applicant cites cases addressing the permissible use of \"configured to\" or \"adapted to\" does not automatically convert the present functional recitations into definite structural claim elements. The precedents cited (e.g., Giannelli) show that context and specification support are decisive: functional phrasing must be supported and reasonably limited by the specification so that PHOSITA can determine the claimed boundaries.\nConclusion under 112(b): For the reasons above and as previously identified in O1, the Examiner finds that the amendments do not resolve the indefinite limitations. Therefore, the 35 U.S.C. 112(b) rejection of claims 1–20 is maintained. Applicant may wish to amend claims to recite the structure that performs the recited functions (for example, replacing ambiguous functional phrases with structural limitations disclosed in the specification and drawings) or provide claim definitions/clarifying language in the specification that clearly tie functional language to specific structure.\nRepresentative record support: The prior Office action sets forth the basis for the 112 rejection: \"Claims 1- 20 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) ... as being indefinite ...\" (O1). Applicant's amended claim language still recites functional phrases such as \"so in such a way that the cover assembly receives the debris\" (J1MNMJKBRXEAPX0).\nIII. 35 U.S.C. 103 — Obviousness (maintained over Karlsson and combinations)\nBackground and prior finding: The Office action dated February 27, 2017 rejected many claims under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over Karlsson (US 4,547,908), and, for claims directed to multi-section covers or reflective material, in view of Irrgang and Stokes respectively (O1). The Examiner relied upon explicit disclosures in Karlsson showing a cap/cover attached to a visor and a helmet with front attachments and an elastic rear ribbon that biases the cap (see record quotations in O1).\nApplicant's arguments: Applicant traversed the rejections, arguing (inter alia) that: (1) Karlsson does not teach the claimed front/rear/first/second/third bracket relationships as claimed; (2) the Examiner improperly applied hindsight and failed to provide adequate motivation to combine references; and (3) the functional terms are structural limitations and should be given weight. Applicant additionally asserts that the amendments and added detail distinguish over the cited art.\nExamination and determination: The Examiner has reconsidered the cited references in light of Applicant's arguments and the amended claims. The record supports the Examiner's original position that Karlsson discloses a cap/cover attached to a helmet and visor, front attachments (clamps/pins), and an elastic ribbon at the rear edge that attaches to helmet-side mountings such that the cap is biased rearwards/upwards (see O1 quoting Karlsson). Those disclosures correspond to many of the claimed features: a cover assembly attached to a visor/face-shield bracket assembly and a rear attachment that biases the cover relative to the helmet. For the pending claims that add multi-section cover structure, Irrgang discloses multi-section connectable cover constructions and sewing arrangements, i.e., the specific arrangement of connectable sections claimed. For reflective material, Stokes discloses reflective portions applied to helmet covers. Combining these teachings to adapt well-known cover sectioning or reflective ornamentation to a visor-mounted cap/cover as in Karlsson would have been within the routine skill of a person having ordinary skill in the art to achieve the predictable result.\nOn the point of motivation to combine and hindsight: The Office's rejection identifies factual teachings in the cited references that provide reason to combine — Karlsson teaches a visor/cover arrangement; Irrgang teaches well-known multi-section cover construction; Stokes teaches reflective ornamentation. The combination proposed is a predictable application of known cover-sectioning or reflective ornamentation to the visor-mounted cover arrangement disclosed in Karlsson. Applicant's broad assertion of impermissible hindsight is not persuasive because the Examiner's prima facie case cites explicit teaching in the prior art, and Applicant has not provided convincing objective evidence (such as unexpected technical effect, commercial success tied to claimed features, or other secondary considerations) that rebuts the prima facie case. Nor has Applicant identified a technical incompatibility that would have deterred a PHOSITA from making the proposed combinations.\nConclusion under 103: For the above reasons, the Examiner maintains the rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 as presented in the February 27, 2017 Office action. Applicant's arguments are acknowledged but do not identify persuasive error in the Examiner's factual findings or legal application.\nIV. Guidance to Applicant — Suggested Claiming and Evidence to Overcome the Rejections\nTo address the 112(b) indefiniteness rejection, consider the following (non-exhaustive) approaches:\n- Amend claims to recite specific structural features disclosed in the specification and drawings that perform the recited functions. For example, instead of \"receives the debris,\" claim language could recite \"a front cover section formed as a sheet extending across and overlying the hat brim so as to capture particulate debris falling towards the brim\" or similar structural detail present in the specification.\n- Replace or further define functional results (e.g., \"for shielding\") with structural limitations (e.g., brim geometry, position relative to front hat section, or a recited shield element attached to the brim).\n- If functional language is retained, provide explicit claim-supporting definitions in the specification that tie \"configured to\" language to corresponding structure and equivalents, and then amend claims to incorporate such structure or to refer to the supporting specification paragraphs by reference (bearing in mind 35 U.S.C. 112 requirements).\nTo address the 103 rejections, consider the following (non-exhaustive) approaches:\n- Amend the claims to recite features that are not taught or suggested by the cited art (e.g., particular bracket attachment arrangements, unique structural details of the connector assembly or connectable sections that are not found in Karlsson/Irrgang/Stokes), and ensure those features are clearly supported in the specification.\n- Submit persuasive evidence of non-obviousness if available (e.g., comparative data showing unexpected results, long-felt but unsolved need, licensing/commercial success tied to the specific claimed features, recognition by others, technical acclaim). Evidence should specifically tie the claimed inventive features to the asserted secondary considerations.\n- If Applicant believes the Examiner misidentified the scope of a cited reference, point to explicit passages or figures in the cited art that support the objection and explain why they do not teach the claimed feature.\nV. Next Steps and Period for Reply\nThis is a non-final action maintaining the previous rejections. Applicant is invited to file a further response within the statutory time period: (a) amend the claims to overcome the 112(b) issues (for example, by reciting clear structural limitations rather than ambiguous functional language); (b) present substantive arguments with supporting evidence to rebut the 103 rejections; and/or (c) present admissible objective evidence of non-obviousness. If Applicant elects to present further amendments, please provide a concise claim listing and indicate any claim(s) to be canceled.\nVI. Representative Record Citations\n- O1 (Office action dated Feb. 27, 2017): \"Claims 1- 20 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) ... as being indefinite ...\" (IZIQABSZRXEAPX4).\n- Karlsson teaching quoted in O1: \"A cap arrangement (generally designated 5) extends between the visor 2 and the helmet and consists in this case of a sheet of water-repellant textile fabric. The front edge 6 of the cap arrangement 5 is attached to the upper edge of the visor 2 by means of attachments 8, each consisting of a clamp provided with a pin, holding the front edge 6 of the cap arrangement.\" (IZIQABSZRXEAPX4 quoting Karlsson).\n- Applicant amended claim language showing continued use of functional phrasing: \"the connector assembly is configured to connect the cover assembly to the face-shield bracket assembly so in such a way that the cover assembly receives the debris ...\" (J1MNMJKBRXEAPX0).\nVII. Examiner Contact\nIf Applicant wishes to discuss the action, telephone contact with the Examiner is allowed. The Examiner's phone number is listed in the file wrapper. Telephone interviews can be an efficient way to narrow issues prior to filing a further response.\nConclusion\nThe rejections under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) and 35 U.S.C. 103 as set forth in the Office action dated February 27, 2017 are maintained for the reasons stated above. Applicant is invited to further amend the claims and/or present evidence and argument responsive to the specific defects identified above. A reply that does not place the application in condition for allowance will result in a subsequent Office action (which may be final).",
  "limitations": [
    "The record does not include the full text of the cited prior-art patents (e.g., Karlsson, Irrgang, Stokes) within the provided documents; the Examiner relied on the prior Office action's summaries and excerpts (IZIQABSZRXEAPX4). If Applicant disputes the Examiner's characterization of those references, Applicant should provide specific prior art citations/paragraphs or file a claim amendment that distinguishes the claimed subject matter over the specific disclosures cited by the Examiner.",
    "Applicant assertions about claim construction/case law are acknowledged, but the record lacks applicant-submitted claim-defining dictionary or specification text that would cure current 112(b) issues; adding such specification support or clearer claim structure would assist prosecution.",
    "This action maintains prior rejections and does not assume subsequent filings or claim entries beyond the amended claims submitted on 2017-04-17; any later-filed amendments or remarks are not considered here."
  ]
}

