  "office_action": "Summary of action\n- Applicant's amendment to independent claim 1 (adding a filament limitation) has been considered. The amendment is entered into the record. The specification provides support for filaments extending from the periphery of the haptic plate (see cited specification language below). The prior 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections over Blake that were applied to claim 1 (and dependent claims 2-6 and 8-9) are withdrawn in light of the entered amendment because the combination of record references relied upon in the earlier Office action did not show the newly added filament limitation.\n- The rejection of claim 7 under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over Blake in view of Richardson is maintained. The amendment to claim 1 does not overcome the earlier-applied combination for claim 7, which recites a thickness relationship between the haptic plate and connector that the prior Office action found would have been obvious to modify using Richardson's teachings. Applicant may respond by argument or by amendment directed to claim 7 if prosecution is to continue.\nDiscussion and reasons for action\nEntry of amendment to claim 1\nApplicant timely filed an amendment on March 4, 2014 that adds the limitation \"wherein at least one filament is coupled to the haptic plate extending from a periphery of the haptic plate\" to independent claim 1 (document HSDODPBCPXXIFW3). The amendment is supported by the specification. See for example: \"the haptic plate has filaments 140a-140d extending from its periphery to help in fixing the haptic plate in a capsular bag\" (pgpub_description). Accordingly, the amendment is entered.\nEffect on prior 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections\nThe prior Non-Final Office action (HMALDM6CPXXIFW4, mailed October 4, 2013) rejected claims 1-6 and 8-9 as obvious over Blake (US 2006/0064161), and rejected claim 7 as obvious over Blake in view of Richardson (US 2008/0021550). The earlier rejection of claim 1 relied on Blake as teaching an optic, a haptic plate surrounding the optic, connectors (arms) between the optic and plate, and various plate/connector configurations. The previously applied combination did not identify filaments coupled to the haptic plate extending from the periphery as being disclosed by Blake. Applicant's amendment to claim 1 adds that filament limitation, and the record does not indicate Blake (as applied in the prior Office action) discloses the filaments recited in the amendment. Therefore the Examiner withdraws the earlier 103 rejection for claim 1 and for dependents that rely only on elements present in the newly amended claim 1 (claims 2-6 and 8-9) contingent on the amendment being entered. These claims are now in condition for further consideration for allowance unless new references or issues are presented.\nRejection maintained for claim 7 (35 U.S.C. 103)\nClaim 7 recites: \"The AIOL of claim 1, wherein the at least one haptic plate has a thickness that is greater than any portion of the at least one connector.\" The prior Office action applied Blake in view of Richardson to reject claim 7 under 35 U.S.C. 103. The prior action asserts Richardson teaches an accommodative lens in which connectors (arms) are thinner than the haptic plate portions (see HMALDM6CPXXIFW4: \"Richardson teaches an accommodative lens ... where the connectors (arms) are thinner than the haptic plate (Fig. 9)\"). The Examiner continues to rely on that combination for claim 7 because the amendment to claim 1 (adding filaments) does not alter the plate-versus-connector thickness relationship and does not negate the rationale for combining the two references with respect to the thickness feature. Thus claim 7 remains rejected as obvious over Blake in view of Richardson.\nApplicant arguments and next steps\nApplicant argued in the remarks accompanying the amendment that Blake fails to teach the filament element now present in claim 1. The Examiner agrees and has entered the amendment. Applicant asserted that dependent claims 2-9 are allowable in view of the amended claim 1; dependent claims 2-6 and 8-9 are withdrawn from rejection contingent upon entry of claim 1. Claim 7, however, remains rejected as explained above.\nTo advance prosecution, Applicant may:\n- File a reply canceling or amending claim 7 to include additional limitations not present in the combination of Blake and Richardson, or\n- Present substantive argument explaining why the combination of Blake and Richardson would not have made claim 7 obvious (point to specific teachings/figures/paragraphs in Blake and Richardson and explain why those teachings cannot be combined to yield the plate/connector thickness relationship), or\n- Present additional evidence (e.g., contemporaneous expert declaration) showing non-obviousness of claim 7 over the applied combination.\nIf Applicant instead wishes to request telephonic interview to discuss the maintained rejection and any proposed amendments, the Examiner will entertain scheduling a conference. The Examiner encourages Applicant to identify specific portions of the prior art (with document citations and precise passages) if arguing that the combination is improper.\nClaims status after entry\n- Claim 1 (as amended): entered; prior 103 rejection withdrawn (see rationale above).\n- Claims 2-6, 8-9: dependent on entered claim 1; prior 103 rejections withdrawn contingent on entry of claim 1.\n- Claim 7: dependent on claim 1; rejection under 35 U.S.C. 103 over Blake in view of Richardson is maintained.\nSupport in the record\n- Applicant amendment (claim 1 amended): HSDODPBCPXXIFW3, support span: \"wherein at least one filament is coupled to the haptic plate extending from a periphery of the haptic plate\".\n- Specification support for filaments: pgpub_description, support span: \"the haptic plate has filaments 140a-140d extending from its periphery to help in fixing the haptic plate in a capsular bag\".\n- Prior Office action asserting rejections and combination: HMALDM6CPXXIFW4, support spans cited in the record of the prior action.\nConclusion\nThe amendment to claim 1 is entered. The Examiner withdraws the prior 103 rejections of claim 1 and of dependent claims 2-6 and 8-9 contingent on entry of the amendment. The rejection of claim 7 under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over Blake in view of Richardson is maintained. Applicant is invited to respond by argument, by amendment directed to claim 7, or by submission of evidence addressing the maintained rejection. If Applicant believes the maintained rejection is in error, identify the specific portion(s) of Blake and/or Richardson relied upon in the prior Office action and explain why that portion does not support the Examiner's combination rationale.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "Blake and Richardson full texts are not reproduced in the file wrapper provided; the Examiner relies on the earlier Office action (HMALDM6CPXXIFW4) which characterized those references. If Applicant disputes the Examiner's characterization of the cited prior art, Applicant should point to precise passages in the cited references.",
    "The record does not include the text of Blake or Richardson; statements about what those references teach are taken from the prior Office action and therefore reflect the Office's prior characterization of the references rather than independent quotations from the prior-art documents in the present record."
  ]
