      "ground": "102",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": ["J1NWFDVRRXEAPX2"],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "Claims 1, 11 and 20 have been amended in A1 to recite that the first plurality includes at least one component not in the second plurality AND at least one other component that is also included in the second plurality. This amendment introduces a limitation of overlap (a memory component that is present in both pluralities). The reference relied on in the prior Office action for the 102 rejection (Lee, US 2007/0271424) was previously applied as anticipating the original claim language. The Lee disclosure, as relied on in the Office action, describes distinct memory groups (MG1, MG2, etc.) and does not disclose a single memory component that is simultaneously a member of two distinct pluralities as now required by the amended claims. Applicant pointed out this absence (A1 remarks). In view of the amendment and the applicant remarks, the previously stated 35 U.S.C. 102(a) anticipation over Lee for these amended independent claims is withdrawn. The amendment is considered to be responsive and adds a limitation not taught by Lee.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "J1NWFDVRRXEAPX2",
          "support_span": "the first plurality of the memory components including at least one other memory component that is also included in the second plurality of the memory components.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "IYD0PCNORXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Claims 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 11, 12, 14, 16, 17, 19 and 20 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102a as being anticipated by Lee.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": ["pgpub_claims","IYD0PCNORXEAPX4"],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The Office action applied Lee together with secondary references (Tsern and Bhakta) to reject multiple dependent and independent claims under 35 U.S.C. 103(a). Applicant's A1 arguments correctly point out that Lee (by itself) does not show the newly-added overlap limitation; however the A1 amendments do not remove other claim language that was the basis for the prior § 103 rejections (for example, routing to DIMMs, use of Flash memory, page-table lookups, virtual-pair copying). Consequently the obviousness rejections based on combination of Lee with Tsern and/or Bhakta remain for the claims to which those combinations were applied. The examiner maintains the § 103 rejections for the claims listed above because, on the record of this application, the cited combination references teach, in the examiner's view, the relevant structural and functional features of those claims (e.g., DIMM environment and page-table lookup (Tsern), virtual-pair data copying (Bhakta)), and a person of ordinary skill would have been motivated to combine them for the stated reasons in the Office action (improving module efficiency, providing alternate storage). Applicant's A1 remarks did not provide persuasive evidence or reasoning to overcome the asserted obviousness combinations; consequently the § 103 rejections remain.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "IYD0PCNORXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Claims 3, 5, 10, 13 and 15 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Lee in view of Tsern.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "IYD0PCNORXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Claims 8 and 18 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Lee in view of Bhakta.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "pgpub_claims",
          "support_span": "The method of claim 1 wherein outputting the first and second memory read commands to the memory module at respective times comprises outputting the first and second memory read commands to first and second pluralities of nonvolatile memory components.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "pgpub_claims",
          "support_span": "The method of claim 4 wherein the nonvolatile memory components comprise Flash memory components.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
