  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [1, 11, 20],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues (A1 remarks) Lee does not disclose that a memory component is included in both of two pluralities; Lee's memory groups (MG1, MG2, etc.) are distinct and do not overlap. Applicant contends that the amendment adding overlap limitation distinguishes the claims from Lee and requests withdrawal of the § 102 rejection.",
      "response": "The amendment adding an explicit overlap limitation (a memory component that is included in both the first and second pluralities) is acknowledged. The earlier § 102(a) anticipation rejection based solely on Lee is withdrawn for the amended independent claims 1, 11 and 20 and for any dependent claims that explicitly rely on the amended independent-claim language (for example claim 6 depends on claim 1). The record supports that Lee, as previously relied upon, presents distinct memory groups and does not disclose a single memory component that is a member of both pluralities as now recited. Accordingly the § 102 rejection over Lee is withdrawn with respect to the amended independent claims and dependents that require the overlap limitation. (See O1: IYD0PCNORXEAPX4; A1 amendment: J1NWFDVRRXEAPX2.)",
      "support_doc_id": "J1NWFDVRRXEAPX2",
      "support_span": "the first plurality of the memory components including at least one other memory component that is also included in the second plurality of the memory components."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [3,5,10,13,15,8,18],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argued generally that the combination rejections under § 103 (Lee + Tsern; Lee + Bhakta) are not persuasive because Lee does not disclose the overlap limitation; applicant requested withdrawal of the § 103 rejections.",
      "response": "The examiner notes that withdrawal of the § 102 anticipation rejection based on Lee for the amended independents does not, by itself, require withdrawal of the § 103 rejections for the claims that were rejected under combinations. On the present record, the examiner finds the applied combinations still present a prima facie case of obviousness for the claims to which they were applied: Tsern provides teaching of a DIMM environment and page-table type mapping, and Bhakta teaches virtual-pair copying. Because applicant amended the independent claims to add the overlap limitation, combinations that would still render other claimed subject matter obvious must be addressed claim-by-claim. Applicant's A1 remarks do not supply persuasive evidence or arguments specific to each asserted combination to show non-obviousness (for example, no evidence was submitted showing a technical incompatibility between Lee and Tsern or between Lee and Bhakta, nor a demonstration that the combination would have been non-routine or lacking motivation). Therefore the examiner maintains the § 103 rejections on the dependent and independent claims previously targeted by those combinations. Applicant may rebut the § 103 rejections by: (i) amending the claims to add further distinguishing limitations not taught by the combination of references, and/or (ii) demonstrating (by argument and/or evidence) why a person of ordinary skill would not have been motivated to combine the cited references or why the combination would not yield the claimed subject matter. In particular, to the extent any dependent claim still incorporates the overlap limitation (by dependency), applicant should indicate whether the applied combinations (Lee+Tsern, Lee+Bhakta) disclose or render obvious that overlap feature; if not, applicant may point this out claim-by-claim.",
      "support_doc_id": "IYD0PCNORXEAPX4",
      "support_span": "Claims 3, 5, 10, 13 and 15 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Lee in view of Tsern."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner Communication (Non-Final)\nSummary\nThis Office action responds to the Applicant's filing dated April 18, 2017 (A1) that amends claims and argues against the rejections set forth in the Office action mailed January 27, 2017 (O1). The record documents available as of April 18, 2017 include the prior art references relied upon in O1 (Lee US 2007/0271424; Tsern US 2007/0088995; Bhakta US 2006/0062047), the published specification and claims (PG pub), and Applicant's amended claims and remarks (A1: J1NWFDVRRXEAPX2, J1NWFDVTRXEAPX2). The examiner has considered the amendments and arguments and provides the following determinations and guidance.\n1) Amendment and withdrawal of the § 102 anticipation rejection (Lee)\n- Applicant amended independent claims 1, 11 and 20 to add a limitation requiring that the first plurality of memory components includes: (a) at least one memory component not included in the second plurality, and (b) at least one other memory component that is also included in the second plurality. This creates a claim requirement that a memory component be a member of both pluralities (an overlap between the pluralities).\n- The prior Office action (O1) had rejected the original claims under 35 U.S.C. 102(a) over Lee. O1 relied on Lee's disclosure of multiple memory groups and concurrent read operations. Applicant correctly pointed out in the A1 remarks that Lee's memory groups (MG1, MG2, etc.) are presented as distinct, non-overlapping groups in the cited portions of Lee's disclosure (see Lee Fig. 11 and associated description). The Examiner agrees that, on the present record, Lee as relied upon in O1 does not disclose that a single memory component is a member of two distinct pluralities at the same time — the specific overlap limitation added in the amendments.\n- Conclusion: The examiner withdraws the § 102(a) anticipation rejection over Lee for the amended independent claims 1, 11 and 20 (and for claims that directly depend from them and thereby require the same overlap limitation, e.g., claim 6 depends from claim 1). The withdrawal follows the Applicant's amendment which adds a limitation not taught by Lee and the Applicant remarks demonstrating that the limitation is not found in Lee.\n2) Status of remaining rejections (35 U.S.C. 103(a) over combinations)\n- O1 applied combinations of Lee with Tsern and Lee with Bhakta to reject several dependent and independent claims under 35 U.S.C. 103(a). Those rejections were not predicated on a pure anticipation of the overlap limitation by Lee alone; rather they are combination-based obviousness rejections asserting that the missing teachings can be supplied by the secondary references (Tsern and Bhakta) and that a person of ordinary skill would have been motivated to combine the references for stated reasons (e.g., efficiency, module architecture).\n- Applicant's A1 remarks argued generally that Lee does not disclose the overlap and requested withdrawal of the § 103 rejections. The examiner acknowledges that Lee alone does not disclose the overlap limitation, and the § 102 rejection was withdrawn for amended independent claims as set forth above. However, the amendments and Applicant remarks do not persuasively show that the asserted combinations (Lee + Tsern; Lee + Bhakta) fail to render the other claim features obvious, or that the combination is improper or would lack motivation. In the examiner's view, the cited secondary references do supply teachings that are relevant to the dependent claim limitations (for example Tsern teaches buffered DIMM-like module structures and page-table mapping; Bhakta discloses virtual-pair copying behavior). On the present record, the examiner maintains the § 103(a) rejections for the claims previously targeted by those combinations (claims 3, 5, 8, 10, 13, 15, 18, and the others indicated in O1) because they remain prima facie unpatentable over the cited combinations.\n- Applicant may respond by (a) presenting claim amendments that add limitations not taught by the applied combination, and/or (b) presenting persuasive argument and/or evidentiary material showing absence of motivation to combine or teaching away, unexpected technical effect, incompatibility between references, or other non-obviousness evidence. The examiner will consider such responses and any supporting evidence you may submit.\n3) Specific examiner guidance to address the maintained § 103 rejections\n- For claims rejected over Lee + Tsern: identify specifically which claim limitation(s) Applicant contends are not taught or would not have been obvious from the combined disclosures of Lee and Tsern. If the claimed limitation pertains to DIMM coupling, page-table mapping or other module structures, explain with citation to the specification and, if available, evidence or affidavit why the cited combination would not teach or render obvious that limitation.\n- For claims rejected over Lee + Bhakta: identify specifically which limitation(s) Applicant contends are not taught by the combination (for example, if the \"virtual pair\" copying structure or its function as claimed is not taught or is claimed to be implemented in a manner differing from Bhakta, explain and show support).\n- If Applicant believes the combination would produce an operability or compatibility problem (e.g., technical incompatibility between the teachings of the references that would have prevented a skilled practitioner from combining them), present argument and supporting evidence (for example, declaration, test data, or authoritative technical literature) showing the incompatibility.\n4) Claim form and entry of amendment\n- The amended claim text in A1 (J1NWFDVRRXEAPX2) appears in the record. If Applicant wishes to have the amendments entered as filed, the revised claims will be entered and the record updated. Entry of the claims will be accompanied by withdrawal of the § 102 anticipation rejection for amended independent claims and related dependent claims as noted above, but not the maintained § 103 rejections.\n- If Applicant wishes to file further amendments, please note that amendment practice and any required disclaimers or remarks should comply with 37 C.F.R. 1.121. After entry of the present amendment, any further amendments to independent claims typically will be treated as a new response and may trigger further reexamination or another Office action.\n5) Procedural matters / next steps\n- The § 102 anticipation rejection over Lee is withdrawn for amended independent claims 1, 11 and 20 and for dependent claims that require the overlap limitation (e.g., claim 6). The examiner will enter the amended claims if Applicant requests entry (A1 has been received and will be placed in the file). If Applicant elects not to pursue additional amendments and provides no further substantive reply to the maintained § 103 rejections within the statutory period, the examiner may issue a final Office action on the maintained grounds.\n- The maintained § 103 rejections are continued in the non-final action. Applicant is invited to reply to the § 103 rejections by: (i) amendment of the rejected claims to include additional limitations not taught by the cited combinations and thereby put the claims in condition for allowance, or (ii) persuasive argument and/or evidence addressing why the combination does not render the claimed invention obvious. Any submitted evidence should be accompanied by an appropriate declaration where required.\n- If Applicant wishes to have an examiner interview to discuss claim scope, the maintained rejections and potential amendments, contact the examiner to arrange a convenient time.\n6) Examiner contact\nIf you believe telephone discussion would clarify issues, call the undersigned Examiner at (571) 272-8625. If you are unable to reach the Examiner, call the Examiner's Supervisor, Henry Tsai, at (571) 272-4176.\nLimits/uncertainties\n- The record does not include materials beyond those explicitly cited in the original Office action and Applicant's A1 submission; if Applicant relies on any evidence not yet of record (for example, declarations, test reports or technical articles), such evidence was not available to the examiner at the time of this action and must be submitted in reply to this action to be considered.\n- The examiner has withdrawn the § 102 anticipation rejection only for the amended claims that include the overlap limitation. This withdrawal does not imply allowance of the claims; the maintained § 103 rejections must still be addressed for allowance of affected claims.\nConclusion/Action requested of Applicant\n- The § 102(a) rejection over Lee for amended independent claims 1, 11 and 20 and dependent claims that require the overlap limitation is withdrawn. The Examiner will enter the amended claims upon Applicant's request (A1 suffices to present amendment; any request for entry is honored by the Office if the amendment is formally compliant). - The § 103(a) rejections over the cited combinations are maintained for the other claims identified in the earlier Office action (O1). Applicant may respond by argument, amendment, and/or evidence directed to the maintained § 103 grounds. - If Applicant intends to pursue allowance, please reply within the statutory time period with either (a) arguments and/or evidence addressing the maintained § 103 rejections, or (b) amended claim language addressing the deficiencies identified in this action.\nDate: April 18, 2017 /Aurangzeb Hassan/\nExaminer, Art Unit 2184\nPrimary Examiner: A. Hassan\n(571) 272-8625",
  "limitations": [
    "Record does not include any new evidence (declarations or test data) beyond Applicant's A1 remarks; any such evidence must be filed to be considered.",
    "Withdrawal of the § 102 rejection applies only to the amended independent claims and dependents that incorporate the overlap limitation; maintained § 103 rejections remain for several claims until rebutted by amendment or persuasive evidence/argument."
  ]
}

