  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant asserts Eerolainen discloses network-side prioritization (NW orders the UE to perform measurements) and does not disclose 'prioritizing, by a user equipment (UE), IRAT measurement scheduling...' as recited in amended claim 1.",
      "response": "The applicant's argument that Eerolainen describes network-side prioritization rather than UE-performed prioritization is noted. The prior Office action (O1) characterized Eerolainen as teaching prioritized IRAT measurement scheduling and used that characterization to reject claims. The current record (O1) does not include the full text of Eerolainen in the file wrapper. Absent an explicit citation to a passage in Eerolainen showing that the prioritization is performed only by the network and not performed or implementable by the UE, the examiner will maintain the 102 rejection for claim 1 as amended. To traverse this rejection, applicant should either (a) point to the exact Eerolainen passage(s) that show the absence of UE-performed prioritization, or (b) amend claim 1 to include additional structural or algorithmic details performed by the UE that are not taught by Eerolainen. If applicant submits a passage from Eerolainen showing network-only control of prioritization, the examiner will reconsider the rejection in view of that citation.",
      "support_doc_id": "I5FSG7WPPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Fig. 2, Para. [0072], [0067], discloses prioritizing IRAT measurement and reporting the measurement data to the NW and executes handover"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        6
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant agrees that claims 6-10 invoke 35 U.S.C. 112(f) and identifies corresponding structure in paragraphs [0050]-[0051] of the specification (communicating module 702, prioritizing module 704, processor 722, memory 726, IRAT measurement module 391, IRAT measurement system 714).",
      "response": "The applicant-identified support in the specification is accepted for the currently-amended means-plus-function limitations in claims 6-10. The 112(f) objection is withdrawn as to these claims because applicant identified corresponding structure with pointer to the specification. The examiner's prior identification of corresponding structure (communicating module, prioritizing module, processor, computer-readable medium) is consistent with applicant's pointer and the specification passages in the record (see SPEC).",
      "support_doc_id": "HMCKN90WPXXIFW3",
      "support_span": "The IRAT measurement system 714 includes a communicating module 702 for transmitting an IRAT measurement report ... The IRAT measurement system 714 also includes a pnontizmg module 704 for pnontizmg IRAT measurement scheduling"
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner Action (Non-Final)\n\nSummary\n- The application has been amended and applicant submitted remarks. Claims 1–20 are pending. Claims 1, 6, 11 and 16 were amended to expressly recite that the prioritizing is performed 'by the user equipment (UE)'. Applicant conceded that claims 6–10 invoke 35 U.S.C. 112(f) and identified corresponding structure in the specification. The examiner has reviewed the amendments and remarks and provides the following determinations and guidance.\n\n35 U.S.C. 112(f) (means-plus-function)\n- Status: Withdrawn for claims 6–10.\n- Reasoning: The applicant agreed that claims 6–10 invoke 35 U.S.C. 112(f) and has identified corresponding structure in the specification including the communicating module, prioritizing module, processor, computer-readable medium, and the IRAT measurement system described in the specification and illustrated in Figure 7. The examiner previously identified corresponding structure in the Office action and the applicant has provided support pointers (see SPEC). The identification of these structures is sufficient for the means-plus-function elements recited in claims 6–10, therefore the 112(f) interpretation objection is withdrawn for those claims. Claim drafting note: if applicant later adds or modifies the means-plus-function language, ensure that corresponding structure remains present in the specification or add explicit structural/algorithmic details to the claims to avoid recurrence of 112(f) issues.\n\n35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) – Anticipation over Eerolainen\n- Status: Maintained for the listed claims (see below).\n- Affected claims: 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 11, 12, 14, 16, 17, 19 (as maintained in the prior Office action).\n- Reasoning: The prior Office action identified Eerolainen (U.S. Pub. No. 2008/0176564) as teaching IRAT measurement reporting and teaching prioritized IRAT measurement scheduling. The examiner's prior characterization (O1) indicates Eerolainen discloses prioritized IRAT measurement and reporting measurement data to the network and executing handover. Applicant amended independent claims to add the express limitation that prioritizing is performed 'by the user equipment (UE)' and argues Eerolainen discloses only network-side prioritization. The examiner notes that the prior rejection relied on the examiner's understanding that Eerolainen teaches prioritized IRAT measurement scheduling broadly enough to meet the claim language. The record currently contains the examiner's characterization of Eerolainen as the basis for the rejection but does not include the full Eerolainen text in this file wrapper. Accordingly, the 102(a)(1) rejections are maintained pending a showing by applicant of either (a) a passage from Eerolainen that clearly and explicitly shows the absence of UE-performed prioritization (i.e., a passage that limits prioritization to the network and prevents the UE from performing prioritization), or (b) an amendment to the claims that adds limiting features (structure, specific UE-executed algorithms, data structures, or other details) not disclosed by Eerolainen.\n- Applicant action advised: To rebut the maintained 102 rejections in light of the amended 'by the user equipment' language, applicant should either (1) submit the specific passage(s) from Eerolainen showing that prioritization is performed exclusively by the network and not by the UE (with document/page/paragraph citations), or (2) propose claim amendments that recite additional UE-specific structure or algorithm steps for performing the prioritization which are supported by the specification and distinguish over the cited Eerolainen disclosures.\n\n35 U.S.C. 103 – Obviousness over Eerolainen in view of Mark\n- Status: Maintained for the listed dependent claims.\n- Affected claims: 3, 5, 8, 10, 13, 15, 18, 20.\n- Reasoning: The prior Office action combined Eerolainen (teaching prioritized IRAT measurement scheduling) with Mark (teaching varying neighbor-cell measurement monitoring rates and frequencies) to reach the dependent claim limitations that recite increasing or decreasing measurement frequency for certain neighbor cells. In the examiner's view the combination would have been obvious to a person of ordinary skill to obtain the recited measurement-frequency modifications for prioritized neighbor cells. Applicant's remarks do not persuasively show that Mark fails to teach the relevant feature or that the combination is improper. Therefore, the 103 rejections are maintained. Applicant may rebut by pointing to specific disclosure in the applied references that does not support the combination, by providing persuasive factual evidence (e.g., technical incompatibility), or by amending the claims to add further limitations that are not taught or suggested by the cited combination.\n\nResponses to Applicant Arguments (selected)\n- Applicant argument (claim 1): Applicant argued Eerolainen discloses network-side prioritization (NW instructs UE measurements) and therefore does not disclose 'prioritizing, by a user equipment (UE), ...' as now claimed. Examiner response: The applicant's argument is noted. The prior rejection relied on the examiner's characterization that Eerolainen discloses prioritized IRAT measurement and scheduling; however, applicant is correct that if Eerolainen only teaches network-side control and does not teach UE-performed prioritization, that factual difference may be material. Because the O1 relied on the examiner's understanding and the full Eerolainen text is not in the record here, applicant is requested to point to the specific Eerolainen passage(s) (document, paragraph, page) that demonstrate that the prioritization is network-only (if applicant wishes to establish that Eerolainen does not read on the amended claim). Alternatively, the applicant may amend claims to include specific, limiting UE-side structure or algorithmic steps for the prioritization that are supported by the specification.\n- Applicant argument (claims 6–10 112(f)): Applicant agreed that 112(f) applies and identified corresponding structure in the spec (paragraphs [0050]-[0051], Fig. 7). Examiner response: The specification passages identified by applicant and the examiner's prior identification are sufficient; the 112(f) objection is withdrawn for claims 6–10.\n\nOpportunity to Amend or Present Evidence/Argument\n- Applicant is invited to: (1) submit specific cited passages from Eerolainen (and Mark, if relevant) demonstrating noninfringement of the amended claims or lack of teaching for particular limitations; (2) amend claims to add further UE-specific structure or algorithmic detail for the 'by the user equipment' prioritizing limitation; or (3) submit persuasive argument or evidence showing why the combination applied in the 103 rejections is improper or would not render the claimed subject matter obvious.\n\nConclusion / Next Steps\n- The 35 U.S.C. 112(f) issue for claims 6–10 is withdrawn based on applicant's acknowledgement and specification pointers.\n- The 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) and 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections identified in the prior Office action are maintained for the listed claims. Applicant should respond with amendments and/or evidence addressing the specific points noted above.\n- This is a non-final action. The examiner will consider applicant's response and any submitted evidence or amendments and will either withdraw the maintained rejections or issue a final rejection or allowance, depending on the outcome of prosecution.\n\nRecord Citations (for examiner positions in this action)\n- O1 (I5FSG7WPPXXIFW4): prior Office action characterizations relied upon for claim rejections.\n- SPEC (HMCKN90WPXXIFW3): paragraphs and Fig. 7 cited by applicant as support for UE modules and measurement scheduling behavior.\n\nLimitations\n- The record in this file wrapper does not currently include the full text of Eerolainen or Mark as filed in the file wrapper. The examiner has relied on the earlier Office action characterizations of those references. If applicant presents the specific passages from the cited references that are said to be dispositive, the examiner will reconsider the maintained rejections in view of those passages. If applicant prefers, applicant may file copies of the relevant pages of the cited references with precise citations so the examiner can directly compare the claim language with the prior-art text.\n\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The prior Office action cited Eerolainen and Mark but the full text passages from those external references are not included in the current file wrapper documents provided here; examiner relied on the O1 characterizations. Applicant may submit explicit passages from Eerolainen/Mark to provoke reconsideration.",
    "The examiner's maintenance of the 102/103 rejections rests on the prior action's characterization that Eerolainen teaches prioritized IRAT measurement scheduling; if applicant shows that Eerolainen teaches only network-side control and not UE-side prioritization with exact citations, the examiner will reconsider.",
    "Record evidence for Eerolainen's exact wording of prioritization (UE vs network) is insufficient in the documents currently present; applicant is invited to supply the precise Eerolainen passages if available."
  ]
}

