{
  "sample_id": "14022347_t1",
  "next_action_type": "CTNF",
  "claim_ground_evidence": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 14, 18, 21, 10],
      "ground": "102",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1",
        "HLHZVAMIPXXIFW2"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The record evidence relied upon in the prior Office action (CTNF HX36BBOVPXXIFW4) identifies Solinski (US 2011/0048867) and Chen (US 2012/0031714) as printed publications that the earlier examiner found to disclose the structural combinations recited by these claims. Applicant's A1 amendments to claim language were filed and are of record (I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1). However, Applicant's remarks do not point to any new specification disclosure that removes the cited teachings from the prior-art references nor supply any claim construction that would render the cited references non-anticipatory. Accordingly, the 35 U.S.C. 102(b) rejections stated in the prior Office action are maintained for the claims identified below for the reasons discussed in the Office Action section of this communication.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "HX36BBOVPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claims 1, 2, 4, 6, 8-9, 12, 14, 18, 21 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Solinski et al. (US 2011/0048867).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "HX36BBOVPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claims 1, 8, and 10 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Chen et al. (US 2012/0031714).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1",
          "support_span": "A brake mechanism for use in an infant stroller apparatus, comprising: two wheel supports adapted to affix with two transversally spaced-apart legs of the infant stroller apparatus; two wheels respectively assembled with the two wheel supports about a rotation axis; wherein each of the two wheels includes a hub portion; two latches respectively assembled with the two wheel supports and operable to rotationally lock the two wheels; and a brake actuating assembly including two brake actuators respectively disposed adjacent to the two wheel supports, and a cable having two opposite ends respectively anchored with the two brake actuators, each of the two brake actuators having a cam surface...",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    }
  ],
  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [1],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues Solinski's shuttle (latch) 'moves up and down to engage and disengage the pockets 50' and therefore differs from claim 1's recitation that 'the two brake actuators are rotatable ... to cause displacements of the two latches parallel to the rotation axis ...', and argues Chen likewise does not disclose the claimed cable-coupled brake actuators.",
      "response": "Applicant's arguments do not overcome the prior-art identification in the record. The earlier Office action explicitly mapped Solinski and Chen to the claimed structural elements (see HX36BBOVPXXIFW4). That action explains that Solinski and Chen disclose two wheel supports, two wheels with hub portions, latches operable to rotationally lock the wheels, and brake actuating assemblies with cam surfaces that drive latch displacement. Applicant's mere contention that a particular element in Solinski 'differs' is not supported by citation of evidence from Solinski or by a persuasive claim construction limiting the claimed 'rotatable ... cam surfaces' to exclude the cited teaching. Likewise, Applicant's assertion that Chen does not disclose the claimed cable coupling is not supported by citation to an absence in Chen; the prior action states that Chen discloses brake assemblies connected via a linking tube and tube linkage (HX36BBOVPXXIFW4). To overcome the 102 grounds the applicant must either amend claims to recite features that are absent from the cited references or show, with record support (citations to the references or to the present specification), that a cited statement of the examiner is factually incorrect. No such showing has been made in the present response. Consequently the rejections are maintained.",
      "support_doc_id": "HX36BBOVPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Claims 1, 2, 4, 6, 8-9, 12, 14, 18, 21 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Solinski et al. (US 2011/0048867)."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [13, 15],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant amended claims 13 and 15 into independent form as suggested in the First Office Action and requested allowance of those claims and related dependent claims.",
      "response": "Claims 13 and 15 as amended have been entered and will be considered. However, entry of amended claims does not by itself require allowance. Each entered claim will be examined in view of the prior art of record. If the examiner determines that the amended independent claims are patentable over the cited references, an allowance may be appropriate. At this time the prior-art rejections maintained in the earlier Office action have not been withdrawn. Examiner analysis of the newly independent claims follows in the Office Action section below.",
      "support_doc_id": "I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1",
      "support_span": "The listing of claims will replace all prior versions and listings of claims in the application."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Summary\nThe A1 filed October 1, 2014 (I0R0EH7OPXXIFW3; I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1; I0TKAOG9PXXIFW1) has been considered. The amendments to the claims set forth in the A1 have been afforded entry and are of record for examination. The previously-issued detailed Office action (CTNF; HX36BBOVPXXIFW4) remains the controlling prior action. After consideration of the applicant's remarks and amended claims, the examiner maintains the rejections under 35 U.S.C. 102(b) identified in the prior Office action for the claims listed below. This communication explains the reasons for maintaining those rejections and addresses the applicant's arguments.\nClaims entered\nThe claim set submitted with the A1 (I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1) has been entered and will be examined. New claims 22–28 have been noted and will be considered where indicated in this action.\nDisposition of prior rejections and response to remarks\n1) Maintenance of 35 U.S.C. 102(b) rejections\n- The prior Office action (HX36BBOVPXXIFW4) rejected claims as anticipated by Solinski (US 2011/0048867) and Chen (US 2012/0031714). The examiner's prior identification mapped the structural elements of the challenged claims to features described in those printed publications. Representative record language identifying the grounds is: \"Claims 1, 2, 4, 6, 8-9, 12, 14, 18, 21 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Solinski et al. (US 2011/0048867).\" (HX36BBOVPXXIFW4).\n- Applicant's A1 filing argues that Solinski's shuttle/latch behavior differs from the claimed 'rotatable cam surfaces' causing latch displacements, and that Chen does not show the cable coupling the two actuators as claimed. Those arguments were considered. Applicant has not cited, and the record does not contain, any passage of Solinski or Chen showing the absence of the claimed features relied upon in the prior action, nor has Applicant demonstrated that the prior characterization of those references is factually erroneous. The burden to rebut a prior-art anticipation is to show with record evidence (for example, a citation to the particular teaching in the prior art that does or does not teach the disputed feature, or an amendment that excludes the cited teaching) that the reference does not in fact disclose the claimed limitation. Applicant's assertions in the remarks are not accompanied by such record citations and therefore do not establish error in the prior action. Accordingly, the anticipation rejections are maintained as to the claims listed below.\n2) Claims currently maintained under 35 U.S.C. 102(b)\n- Solinski-based rejections maintained for claims: 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 14, 18, 21. See HX36BBOVPXXIFW4: \"Claims 1, 2, 4, 6, 8-9, 12, 14, 18, 21 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Solinski et al. (US 2011/0048867).\"\n- Chen-based rejections maintained for claims: 1, 8, 10. See HX36BBOVPXXIFW4: \"Claims 1, 8, and 10 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Chen et al. (US 2012/0031714).\"\nExamination notes applying to maintained rejections\n- Claim scope: The entered claim language (I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1) has been read in light of the specification as filed (pgpub_description; HLHZVAMIPXXIFW2) using ordinary claim construction consistent with 35 U.S.C. 282 and relevant case law. The prior Office action set forth specific mappings from claim elements to the cited disclosures; those mappings remain the basis for the anticipatory findings absent a persuasive showing otherwise.\n- Applicant argument regarding Solinski shuttle: Applicant contends the shuttle behavior differs. The earlier Office action identified Solinski features that the examiner construed to correspond to the claimed latches and cam-driven operation. Applicant has the opportunity to: (a) submit specific citation(s) to the prior art showing a mischaracterization; (b) amend the claims to add limitations that are not present in the cited references; or (c) provide persuasive argument supported by record evidence that construction of the claim element should exclude the cited teaching. None of these corrective steps were taken in the A1 response.\n- Applicant argument regarding the cable/linkage in Chen: The prior Office action cites passages characterizing Chen's linking tube and cable linkage. If Applicant believes the prior action misread Chen or that Chen lacks the cited feature, Applicant must point to the exact passages in Chen or otherwise demonstrate the prior action's factual inaccuracy. A bare assertion of difference is insufficient.\nAction on allowable matters\n- The prior Office action indicated that certain claims (3, 7, 11, 13, 15-17, 19-20) were objected to as dependent on rejected base claims but could be allowable if re-written in independent form. Applicant rewrote claims 13 and 15 into independent form and those amended claims have been entered for examination. They will be examined on the merits with respect to the cited references. Presently, the record does not establish that the newly independent claims are patentable over the same references already relied upon; examination of those claims will continue and the examiner will determine whether the references of record anticipate or render obvious the claimed subject matter.\nNext steps available to Applicant\n- To advance prosecution toward allowance, Applicant may: (1) amend claims to include further limitations not found in the cited references (supported by the current specification); (2) submit persuasive, evidence-backed arguments showing that a cited reference does not in fact disclose a claimed limitation (for example, cite specific figures/paragraphs of the reference and explain why the citation does not anticipate the claim element); or (3) file remarks with new evidence such as a declaration that presents factual differences not of record (e.g., demonstrating a structural or functional distinction in the prior art). Any amendment should comply with 37 C.F.R. 1.121 and not introduce new matter.\nConclusion and disposition\n- The 102(b) rejections set forth in the prior Office action (HX36BBOVPXXIFW4) are maintained for the claims listed above. Claims amended and entered in A1 are of record and will be examined. This is a non-final action; prosecution remains open. The applicant may respond by amendment and/or arguments as described above.\nRepresentative record citations (supporting prior action conclusions)\n- HX36BBOVPXXIFW4: \"Claims 1, 2, 4, 6, 8-9, 12, 14, 18, 21 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Solinski et al. (US 2011/0048867).\"\n- HX36BBOVPXXIFW4: \"Claims 1, 8, and 10 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as being anticipated by Chen et al. (US 2012/0031714).\"\n- I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1 (entered claim 1) shows the claim language now of record.\nTime period for reply\n- Applicant is permitted to respond to this Office action. If Applicant chooses to file an amendment, persuasive argument, or evidence, such response should be filed timely. This is a non-final action; normal shortened statutory periods do not apply to an applicant's election to respond following a non-final action, but prosecution is kept open.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The examiner relies on the examiner's prior mappings in HX36BBOVPXXIFW4 to Solinski and Chen; the full text and figures of those prior art publications are not reproduced here and the examiner's characterizations in the prior action are accepted as the record basis for the rejections unless applicant points to contrary passages in those publications.",
    "Applicant's argument in the A1 (I0TKAOF3PXXIFW1; I0TKAOG9PXXIFW1) did not include documentary citations to Solinski or Chen showing factual error in the prior mappings; absence of such pointed evidence limits the examiner's obligation to revoke the prior rejections.",
    "Entry of the amended claim set from the A1 is recorded; any uncertainty in OCR-corrupted text of submitted claims is noted — if applicant believes any entered claim text is corrupted, applicant should promptly file a clean corrected replacement showing the intended text and identifying the problem."
  ]
}

