  "claim_ground_evidence": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [1,3,4,5,8,9,10,11,12,13,15],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": ["I5K2NDP9PXXIFW3"],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The amendment to claim 1 does not introduce a patentable distinction over the applied prior art as explained below. The references of record teach (i) serving GUI pages and links to networked client devices (Niwamoto), (ii) storing appliance information and XML-style tag data for appliance control and state (Arling), and (iii) use of HTTP/file-name requests and server-side files in a relay/commanding context (Kopf, Yuh). It would have been within ordinary skill to combine these teachings to provide execution of a tag file (XML) identified by a GUI link and to use the tag file contents to format and send the appliance-specific command over a protocol appropriate for the appliance.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claims 1-3, 5, 8-13 and 15 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Niwamoto et al. in view of Arling et al.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "H4A8BNB5PXXIFW3",
          "support_span": "causing a graphical user interface page to be provided to the personal communication device",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "H4A8BNB5PXXIFW3",
          "support_span": "tag files 1012 may specify the actions to be performed by a slave relay device when an HTML tag is activated on the client device",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Arling: Paragraph 30; tag file capability",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [4],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": ["I5K2NDP9PXXIFW3"],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "Applicant's amendment specifying an HTTP request including a file name is addressed by the prior art which teaches HTTP GET/HTTP-like interactions and use of file-name-identifiable resources in such transactions (see Kopf cited in the Office action). Combining Kopf's teaching of HTTP/file-name requests with Niwamoto/Arling would have been an obvious implementation choice to one of ordinary skill.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claim 4 is rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103(a) ... in further view of Kopf.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Kopf ... uses HTTP request protocols and XML file (Paragraphs 37 and 41)",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [6,7,14],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": ["I5K2NDP9PXXIFW3"],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "Claims reciting GUI pages or protocol information being supplied from/obtained from a server or remote repository are shown in Niwamoto and in the cited secondary references. The combination would have been obvious to provide remote storage/retrieval of GUI/page/protocol data for flexibility and resource reasons.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claims 6, 7 and 14 are rejected ... in further view of Yuh et al.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "H4A8BNAYPXXIFW3",
          "support_span": "the data files which comprise the GUI pages ... may be stored on a local (e.g., computer 202) or remote (e.g., computer 210) server system and accessed on an as needed basis by slave relay devices",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    }
  ],
  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [1],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues Niwamoto routes commands via a communication adaptor coupled to the appliance and thus does not disclose an executable tag file at the gateway that generates and transmits appliance-specific commands; they contend the proposed combination changes Niwamoto's operating principle and Arling's XML is only a saved 'state' not an executable mapping.",
      "response": "The Examiner has considered these points. The record references show the claimed features in combination are prima facie suggested. Niwamoto teaches serving GUI pages and receiving link-based requests from personal devices (specification: \"causing a graphical user interface page to be provided to the personal communication device\"). Arling teaches XML/tag files that associate device definitions and command data (Office Action cites Arling ¶30). It is a routine design choice to have a gateway or server execute or interpret an XML/tag file (i.e., to read a stored tag file or resource identified by a GUI link and use it to format and send commands) so that the gateway can send appliance-appropriate commands over an appropriate protocol — this is the combination the Office previously articulated and maintains as rendering the claims obvious. The fact that Niwamoto describes an architecture where a communication adaptor is directly coupled to an appliance does not preclude designing an implementation in which the gateway/server holds or references executable tag data to produce the appliance commands; such an implementation is a predictable engineering modification. Therefore the Examiner maintains the obviousness rejections for claim 1.",
      "support_doc_id": "I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Niwamoto discloses ... causing a graphical user interface page to be provided to the personal communication device; Arling ... tag file capability"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [3,7],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant amended claims but some claim text contains typographical/antecedent errors (e.g., \"claim 2 1\" and \"claim 7 1\").",
      "response": "The Examiner objects to the form of claims containing incorrect antecedent or internal references. For example, claim 3 as presented reads \"The slave relay station as recited in claim 2 1,\" which is indefinite as to antecedent basis and must be corrected. Claim 7 likewise contains an improper dependency reference \"claim 7 1.\" Please correct these formal defects (typos and improper claim dependencies) in the next submission. A clean listing of claims with correct antecedent references is required before entering any further substantive allowance consideration.",
      "support_doc_id": "I5K2NDP9PXXIFW3",
      "support_span": "The slave relay station as recited in claim 2 1, ... ; The slave relay station as recited in claim 7 1, ..."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [1,6,14],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant contends Arling's XML is only for state saving and thus does not teach executing a tag file to transmit commands directly to appliances; also argues modification would change Niwamoto's principle.",
      "response": "The Examiner acknowledges Arling's paragraphs describe use of XML for state but notes the Office action considered Arling as teaching XML/tag files for linking commands and device data (see O1: \"Arling ... discloses the linking of commands ... which are found in XML files (tag files)(Paragraph 30)\"). Even if Arling's examples emphasize state, interpreting or processing XML-formatted data to derive commands is a routine implementation step. The combination of Niwamoto (GUI/link serving and network gateway functionality) and Arling (XML/tag descriptions of device data and commands) renders the claimed executing/using of a tag file to produce an appliance-protocol-based transmission obvious. Additionally, forward-cited references (Kopf, Yuh) demonstrate using HTTP and servers for file-name-based requests and for serving GUI pages or command data, supporting the Examiner's position that these are predictable, known techniques that one of ordinary skill would apply.",
      "support_doc_id": "I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Arling: Paragraph 30; tag file capability"
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Action on Applicant's January 30, 2015 response and amendments\n\nSummary\nThis is a non-final action in response to the applicant's A1 filing (filed 2015-01-30) which amended the claims and argued against the outstanding 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections. The prior Office action (November 3, 2014) set forth rejections of the original claims over Niwamoto in view of Arling, with further citations of Kopf and Yuh for certain dependent claims. The applicant cancelled claim 2 and amended claim language. The record has been reviewed. The Examiner maintains the prior 103 rejections for the reasons below, and addresses claim form defects that must be corrected before further processing toward potential allowance.\n\n1) Status of claims\n- Claims pending: 1, 3–15 (per applicant filing). Claim 2 is canceled. The Examiner acknowledges the amendments but observes remaining substantive rejections as indicated below. Several dependent claims contain improper or unclear dependency language that must be corrected (see paragraph 4).\n\n2) Rejection maintained — obviousness under 35 U.S.C. 103\nClaims 1 and the indicated dependent claims remain rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over the combinations stated in the November 3, 2014 Office action:\n- Claims 1–3, 5, 8–13, 15: Niwamoto (US 7,631,197 B2) in view of Arling (US 2005/0097618 A1).\n- Claim 4: Niwamoto + Arling, further in view of Kopf (US 2008/0294667 A1) for HTTP/file-name request teaching.\n- Claims 6, 7, 14: Niwamoto + Arling, further in view of Yuh (US 7,093,003 B2) for server GUI/provisioning teaching.\n\nReasons (concise):\n- The specification and claim amendments continue to recite known elements: serving GUI pages to networked personal devices, activatable links, tag files/XML representing device definitions and command lists, selection of pages tailored to device capability, and retrieving/storing GUI and protocol data on local or remote servers. Niwamoto discloses serving GUI pages and link-based interaction between a user device and gateway/gateway-stored resources (specification supporting \"causing a graphical user interface page to be provided to the personal communication device\"). Arling discloses XML/tag files and associating device information and command elements in tag-like structures (Office action cites Arling ¶30). Kopf and Yuh teach use of HTTP/file-name resource requests and server-based GUI/command provisioning respectively. Combining these teachings would have been an ordinary and predictable design choice for a person having ordinary skill in the art to provide the claimed functionality (per the rationale already stated in the November 2014 action). See the Office action summary (Nov. 3, 2014) for specific mappings of claim elements to the references of record.\n- Applicant's arguments that Niwamoto's architecture routes via an adaptor and that adding an executable tag-file capability would 'change the principle' of Niwamoto do not establish nonobviousness. The asserted modification (interpreting/using stored tag/XML data at the gateway or server to form/send appliance-appropriate commands, and using GUI links/file-name-identifiable resources to select those tag files) is a routine architectural option and is supported by the cited references that teach network-served resources, XML/tag data, and HTTP/URI-based requests. The Examiner therefore finds the record combination still presents a prima facie case of obviousness for the claims currently pending.\n\n3) Claim form objections (requires correction)\n- Several claims contain unclear antecedent/dependency language likely due to typographical errors in the submitted claim set. Examples: claim 3 reads in the record as \"The slave relay station as recited in claim 2 1, wherein...\" and claim 7 reads \"The slave relay station as recited in claim 7 1, wherein...\". Such internal references are indefinite or nonsensical and must be corrected. Provide a corrected claim listing that: (a) uses proper antecedent identifiers (e.g., \"The slave relay station as recited in claim 1...\" or \"The slave relay station as recited in claim 2...\"), (b) removes duplicate/malformed dependency numbering, and (c) presents the claims in clean form. The Examiner cannot treat unclear dependencies as allowable language; please submit an amended claim set that fixes these formal defects.\n\n4) Examiner response to applicant remarks\n- Applicant argued Arling's XML is merely state data and not an executable tag file that causes direct appliance transmissions. The Examiner notes that Arling describes XML/tag constructs and that interpreting or processing XML-formatted tag data to derive commands is a routine programming step. The Office's combination of references shows the elements claimed—GUI links, resources identifiable by file name or tag reference, XML/tag storage of device/command data, and server/gateway execution or interpretation to format/send appliance commands—are all taught separately and would be combined by a person of ordinary skill for predictable reasons (interoperability, use of known web protocols, flexibility of server-side provisioning). Consequently, the 103 rejections are maintained.\n- Applicant's statement reserving right to argue dependent claims is noted. After correction of the claim dependency/form issues, the Examiner will review any argument or amendment specific to particular dependent claims.\n\n5) Next steps / requirements to applicant\n- File a corrected set of claims with clean antecedent language and no typographical dependency errors. If applicant wishes to press substantive arguments against the maintained rejections, present (1) persuasive explanation why the combination of references is unsound as a matter of fact or law (for example, pointing to explicit teachings in the references that would teach away from combining), or (2) amendments that clearly set forth limitations not suggested by the cited art. If amendments are made, provide clear support in the specification for any newly added limitations.\n- If applicant believes any cited reference has been mischaracterized, specify the exact passages relied upon and explain why the Examiner's characterization is incorrect, with precise record citations.\n\n6) Conclusion / action on record\n- The Examiner maintains the rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 for the pending claims as described above. This non-final action gives applicant an opportunity to submit corrected claim form and/or substantive response addressing the maintained obviousness grounds. Absent appropriate response, the Examiner may continue prosecution which could include an eventual final rejection if the rejections remain unresolved.\n\nLimitations / uncertainties\n- The Examiner relied on the cited Office action (I1WD6Z6LPXXIFW4, 11-03-2014), the applicant's claim amendments (I5K2NDP9PXXIFW3, 01-30-2015), and the specification as filed (H4A8BNAYPXXIFW3 / pgpub_description). If applicant submits further evidence or arguments that materially change the evidentiary record, the Examiner will reconsider the findings.\n\nRequired formal correction\n- Submit a corrected claim set removing typographical/dependency errors (claims 3 and 7 examples) and indicating which claims applicant wishes to have entered (if different from the submitted set). The Examiner will not enter claims with unclear antecedent basis.\n\nExaminer contact\n- For any questions on this action, contact the examiner per the prior Office action contact information.\n\n(End of action.)",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant's A1 claim amendments contain typographical/antecedent errors (e.g., 'claim 2 1', 'claim 7 1') that prevent clear interpretation; Examiner objects and requires corrected claim formatting.",
    "The Examiner's maintenance of the 103 rejections is based on the record references cited in the November 3, 2014 Office action and the applicant's January 30, 2015 claim file; if applicant submits further evidence or arguments, the Examiner will reconsider.",
    "Examiner relied on the Office action characterizations of Arling and Niwamoto as presented in the record; characterization is not independent authentication of all underlying reference content beyond the cited passages."
  ]
}

