      "argument_summary": "Applicant's A1 REMARKS state: \"A Terminal Disclaimer is submitted herewith and it is therefore respectfully requested that the rejection of claims 1-20 be withdrawn.\"",
      "response": "The examiner acknowledges receipt of the Applicant's statement in the reply (HYHHWFFNPXXIFW3) that a terminal disclaimer was \"submitted herewith.\" However, there is no terminal disclaimer document in the application file wrapper accessible to the examiner as of the present record. Absent an entered terminal disclaimer meeting the requirements of 37 C.F.R. § 1.321, the nonstatutory double patenting rejection must be maintained. To overcome the rejection, Applicant must either: (1) file and have entered a valid terminal disclaimer that (a) identifies U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255 as the reference patent, (b) is signed in accordance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.321(b), and (c) meets the common-ownership or joint-research agreement condition of 37 C.F.R. § 1.321(c)/(d); or (2) provide documentary evidence in the record showing common ownership (e.g., an assignment record or a copy of an earlier-filed terminal disclaimer already of record) or a joint research agreement meeting the statutory requirements. If a terminal disclaimer has in fact been filed via EFS-Web, please (A) identify the EFS confirmation number and the exact submission date/time, and (B) submit a copy of the terminal disclaimer PDF as a reply paper so it may be associated with the application file. Absent such documentary evidence, the double patenting rejection is continued.",
      "support_doc_id": "HYHHWFFNPXXIFW3",
      "support_span": "A Terminal Disclaimer is submitted herewith and it is therefore respectfully requested that the rejection of claims 1-20 be withdrawn."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "DETAILED ACTION\n\nBackground\nThe application is being examined under the pre-AIA first-to-invent provisions. An earlier Office action dated May 8, 2014 (document HUVLV8HBPXXIFW4) placed claims 1–20 under a nonstatutory (obviousness-type) double patenting rejection over claims 1–33 of U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255. Applicant's reply filed August 5, 2014 (A1) includes remarks stating that \"A Terminal Disclaimer is submitted herewith\" and requests withdrawal of the double-patenting rejection.\n\nDisposition and Reasons for Continued Rejection\nDouble Patenting (nonstatutory) — maintained (claims 1–20)\nClaims 1–20 remain subject to the nonstatutory obviousness-type double patenting rejection over U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255 (claims 1–33) as set forth in the May 8, 2014 Office action. The Office action explained that, although the claims in the instant application are not identical to those of the patent, they are not patentably distinct and thus are unpatentable under double-patenting principles. Applicant's A1 filing requests withdrawal of the rejection and states that \"A Terminal Disclaimer is submitted herewith.\" The examiner has reviewed the application file record available as of the date of this communication and finds no terminal disclaimer document that has been entered in the file wrapper which overcomes the rejection.\nUnder the Office practice, a nonstatutory double patenting rejection may be overcome by filing a terminal disclaimer in accordance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.321 when the required common ownership (or qualifying joint research agreement) condition is met. A terminal disclaimer must be properly signed and must comply with the regulatory requirements. If a terminal disclaimer is not of record, the rejection must be maintained.\nAccordingly, the rejection of claims 1–20 over U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255 is continued for the following reason(s):\n- The May 8, 2014 Office action made the double patenting ground of rejection of record (HUVLV8HBPXXIFW4: \"Claims 1-20 are rejected on the ground of nonstatutory double patenting as being unpatentable over claims 1-33 of U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255.\").\n- Applicant's August 5, 2014 response (HYHHWFFNPXXIFW3) asserts that a terminal disclaimer was submitted. The examiner finds no terminal disclaimer in the file that has been entered and that meets the requirements of 37 C.F.R. § 1.321. The mere statement that a terminal disclaimer was submitted in the reply paper is not sufficient to establish that a compliant terminal disclaimer is of record.\n\nWhat Applicant Must Do to Overcome the Double Patenting Rejection\nTo place the application in condition for further consideration with respect to the double patenting rejection, Applicant should take one of the following actions:\n1) If a terminal disclaimer was actually submitted via EFS-Web or another filing route but not yet associated with the file, provide documentation that identifies the submission (for example, the EFS confirmation number and the exact submission date/time), and file a copy of the terminal disclaimer (PDF) as a reply paper so that it can be associated with the application record. The terminal disclaimer must meet the formal and substantive requirements of 37 C.F.R. § 1.321, including a proper signature and identification of the patent (U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255) as the patent over which the disclaimer is made. The terminal disclaimer must also comply with the common-ownership or joint-research agreement requirements (37 C.F.R. § 1.321(c) or (d)), or the disclaimer will not be effective to overcome the rejection.\nOR\n2) If no terminal disclaimer has been filed, file a terminal disclaimer that complies with 37 C.F.R. § 1.321. The terminal disclaimer must: (a) identify U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255 as the patent over which the disclaimer is made; (b) be signed in accordance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.321(b) by a person authorized to sign; and (c) include the statement of common ownership or show that the statutory joint research agreement exception applies (37 C.F.R. § 1.321(c) or (d)). If the terminal disclaimer meets the requirements, and is entered into the file, the double patenting rejection will be reconsidered in view of the entered terminal disclaimer.\nOR\n3) If the double patenting rejection is to be opposed on the ground that claims are patentably distinct, submit persuasive argument and, where appropriate, claim amendments and evidence demonstrating patentable distinction over the claims of U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255. Note that absent an entered terminal disclaimer, argument alone must persuasively show patentable distinction over the identified patent claims.\n\nIf Applicant files a terminal disclaimer meeting the statutory and regulatory requirements and it is entered in the file, the examiner will withdraw the nonstatutory double patenting rejection as to the claims covered by the terminal disclaimer, subject to any other outstanding substantive rejections or formal objections.\n\nTiming and Procedural Matters\n- If Applicant intends to rely on a terminal disclaimer that is already filed but not yet of record, identifying the submission (EFS confirmation number and submission timestamp) and providing a copy of the filed terminal disclaimer as a reply paper will expedite correction of the file and reconsideration of the rejection. Failure to provide an entered terminal disclaimer or other appropriate response will result in maintenance of the double patenting rejection and further action.\n- The applicant's August 5, 2014 claim set has been noted and will be treated as the pending claims. This Office action is limited to the double patenting issue described above. Any other outstanding examination matters remain unaffected and may be addressed in subsequent communications if present.\n\nConclusion and Next Steps\nThe double patenting rejection of claims 1–20 is continued because there is no terminal disclaimer of record that overcomes the rejection. Applicant should either (A) submit a compliant terminal disclaimer that identifies U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255 and meets the requirements of 37 C.F.R. § 1.321 (and ensure it is entered into the file), or (B) provide documentary evidence that satisfies the common-ownership or joint-research agreement requirement, or (C) present persuasive argument and/or claim amendments demonstrating patentable distinction from the cited patent claims.\n\nThe examiner is available for telephone conference if Applicant wishes to discuss the specific requirements for a terminal disclaimer or evidence appropriate to show common ownership. If Applicant has already filed the terminal disclaimer, please identify the EFS confirmation and provide a copy so this office can correct the file and withdraw the double patenting rejection upon entry of a compliant terminal disclaimer.\n\n(Administrative note) The examiner's prior guidance in the May 8, 2014 communication (HUVLV8HBPXXIFW4) and the present action should be read together. The May 8, 2014 action set forth the double patenting grounds; Applicant's reply asserts a terminal disclaimer but no terminal disclaimer document is of record. The present action explains what is required to place the application in condition for allowance with respect to the double patenting ground.\n\nLimitations\n- The record available to the examiner does not include a terminal disclaimer document that has been entered in the application file wrapper. If the applicant can show by documentary evidence that a compliant terminal disclaimer has in fact been filed and entered, this communication will be superseded accordingly. The examiner does not decline to recognize a terminal disclaimer that was in fact filed; rather, the examiner must act on what is of record.\n- The examiner has not performed an independent reexamination of the entire scope of claims 1–20 against U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255 beyond the double patenting concern previously made of record. The double patenting rejection is maintained solely because it is not yet overcome by an entered terminal disclaimer or other appropriate evidence or argument.\n\nIf Applicant elects to file a terminal disclaimer, please ensure that the terminal disclaimer (1) explicitly identifies U.S. Patent No. 9,213,255 as the patent over which the terminal disclaimer is made, (2) includes a statement that the terminal disclaimer is being filed to obviate the nonstatutory double patenting rejection with respect to the identified patent, and (3) otherwise complies with 37 C.F.R. § 1.321 in form and signature. If Applicant believes a terminal disclaimer was already filed, identify the EFS confirmation number and submit a copy so the file can be corrected and the rejection reconsidered.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant's A1 states a terminal disclaimer was \"submitted herewith,\" but no terminal disclaimer document is present in the available file; examiner continued the double-patenting rejection for that reason.",
    "If a terminal disclaimer was indeed filed outside the record available to the examiner (for example, a filing error or e-filing not yet associated), the applicant must provide documentary proof (EFS confirmation number and copy) so the file can be corrected; absent that evidence the examiner cannot treat the rejection as withdrawn."
  ]
