  "office_action": "Examiner Action — Non-Final\nSummary\n- The application remains under active examination. Claims 1–16 were considered in the Office Action mailed February 9, 2017. Applicant filed amendments and remarks received May 4, 2017 (A1). The A1 proposed several claim changes including amendment of claims 1 and 16 to add that the relaxed abduction problem is \"determined by a piece of description logic,\" cancellation of claim 15, and addition of apparatus claims 17–19 (proposed). The prior Office Action made rejections under 35 U.S.C. 102 and 103 based on Hubauer (and combinations of Hubauer with Roshchin, Eiter, and Vasseur). Applicant argues the cited references do not teach certain limitations and that allowable subject matter was indicated for claims 9–15 in the OA. The record does not provide a basis for withdrawal of the rejections at this time. This action maintains the prior rejections and explains the deficiencies in Applicant's reply.\nIssues Addressed\n1) 35 U.S.C. 102 rejection (Claims 1, 3, 16) — maintained\n- Background: In the Office Action of Feb. 9, 2017 the Examiner identified Hubauer as disclosing an abduction problem and steps of determining and solving it to yield explanations (and cited the Hubauer portions in support). The Office characterized Hubauer as teaching the methods claimed in claims 1 and 16 and as teaching claim 3's use of preference/orders in producing explanations.\n- Applicant response: Applicant amended claims 1 and 16 to add the limitation \"wherein the relaxed abduction problem is determined by a piece of description logic.\" Applicant argues Hubauer does not teach \"relaxed abduction\" and that Examiner allowed claim 15 dependent on claim 1 in the previous action (applicant cites allowance of claim 15 in the OA as a basis to dispute the rejection).\n- Examiner analysis and findings: The February 9, 2017 Office Action explicitly cited Hubauer in rejecting claims 1, 3 and 16 (see IYW27NTZRXEAPX0). The applicant's amendment narrows the claim by adding a description-logic limitation. Applicant has not submitted evidence (e.g., a specific printed publication excerpt, affidavit, or demonstrative claim construction supported by the specification and pointing to any record prior-art deficiency) showing that the cited Hubauer disclosure fails to anticipate the amended claim limitation. The Office's characterization of Hubauer in the prior action stands on the record of that action. Additionally, the mere fact that a particular dependent claim (15) was identified by the previous action as allowable does not by itself show the prior-art teaching does not anticipate the independent claims as amended; if applicant contends the Examiner misapplied a reference to dependent claim 15, applicant must point to precise text in the reference/record or propose a specific claim amendment that ensures patentable distinction. On the current record the 102 rejection of claims 1, 3 and 16 is maintained.\n- Action: Claims 1, 3, and 16 remain rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) over Hubauer. Applicant is invited to (a) present claim amendment(s) that clearly traverse the cited Hubauer disclosure (and provide the specific support in the specification for the amendment), (b) submit persuasive documentary evidence (e.g., the Hubauer publication itself with highlighted passages demonstrating absence of the claimed feature; applicant-submitted declaration showing non-enablement in Hubauer; or other prior-art evidence) that the combination relied upon in the prior action does not teach the amended claim language, or (c) provide cogent argument mapping the claim limitations to specific passages of the record demonstrating non-anticipation.\n2) 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections (Claims 2, 4–8, 6) — maintained\n- Claim 2: The Examiner combined Hubauer and Roshchin to teach the use of the claimed method in turbines. Applicant's A1 arguments against the §102 rejections (discussion above) are not accompanied by documentary evidence distinguishing the applied combination. On the record, Hubauer supplies the abduction approach and Roshchin supplies application to turbines. The combination therefore renders claim 2 obvious. The 103 rejection is maintained.\n- Claims 4, 5, 7, 8: The Examiner relied on Hubauer for the general abduction solution and on Eiter for the formal RAP formulation and hypergraph encoding. Applicant argues Eiter does not contain the parameters but has not submitted a specific citation showing the absence of the recited RAP structure in Eiter or other evidence demonstrating non-obviousness. The Examiner therefore maintains these rejections. If applicant believes Eiter has been mischaracterized, applicant should identify the exact Eiter passages and explain why those passages do not correspond to the limitations recited in claims 4, 5, 7, and 8.\n- Claim 6: The Examiner added Vasseur to the Hubauer+Eiter combination to show label-based pruning to determine Pareto-optimal paths. Applicant contends Vasseur is inapposite; applicant has not provided evidence to rebut that labels or label-correction techniques for multi-criteria path selection were known in the art as applied by the Examiner. The 103 rejection of claim 6 is maintained.\n- Action: The rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 for claims 2, 4–8, and 6 are maintained. Applicant may traverse these rejections by submitting amended claims with limiting features not taught or suggested by the applied references, or by submitting evidence (publications, declarations, or specific passages of the applied references) that the combination relied on does not teach the claimed limitations.\n3) Allowable subject matter and dependent-claim rewrite guidance\n- The prior Office Action indicated that certain dependent claims (claims 9–15 as noted in the OA) were objected to as dependent on rejected base claims, but would be allowable if presented in independent form including the limitations of the base claims and any intervening claims. Applicant amended claims 1 and 16 by adding the limitation previously in claim 15. Applicant canceled claim 15. Applicant should note that adding previously-identified allowable limitations to an independent claim does not automatically overcome every prior-art ground; it may, however, change the obviousness/anticipation analysis. If applicant elects to rewrite dependent claims 9–15 into independent form (per the Office's guidance), include all necessary antecedent and structural limitations and provide support in the specification for the rewritten independent claim language.\nConclusion / Next steps\n- The rejections and rationale from the Office Action mailed February 9, 2017 are maintained for the reasons discussed above. This is a non-final action. The applicant may respond by: (A) filing claim amendments with support in the specification that distinguish over the applied references; (B) submitting evidence (publications, declarations) or pointing to specific portions of the applied references demonstrating lack of teaching for the claimed limitations; and/or (C) providing persuasive, claim-specific arguments that map claim limitations to the record to show patentable distinction.\n- If applicant files amendments that introduce new claim scope, the Examiner will consider whether any newly presented claim language is supported by the specification and whether any new prosecution history raises additional issues (e.g., new prior art, new rejections under 35 U.S.C. 112). If applicant's next filing does not traverse the outstanding rejections, the Examiner may issue a final rejection after appropriate procedural steps.\nRequired applicant action\n- Reply to this non-final action under 37 C.F.R. § 1.111 within the statutory time period. If applicant intends to amend the claims, provide the complete claim set and point to the specification support for any added limitations. If applicant intends to rely on argument/evidence, provide it with the reply and show where in the applied references or specification the distinctions are found.\nRepresentative contact\n- If applicant wishes to discuss the rejections by telephone prior to amendment, counsel may arrange a call with the Examiner. The telephone number listed in the prior Office Action remains available.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The record does not include the full text of the Hubauer, Eiter, Roshchin, or Vasseur references; the examiner relied on the prior OA characterization of those references (IYW27NTZRXEAPX0). If applicant desires withdrawal of a rejection based on lack of specific disclosure, applicant must cite specific passages of the applied reference(s) or submit supporting evidence.",
    "Claim-entry status for new/ amended claims in A1 was recorded as 'proposed' in the file wrapper; actual entry into the application requires formal entry processing. This communication addresses the amendments as presented in A1 but does not treat any amendment as having been admitted beyond what the record shows.",
    "Some support_spans in the record are from the Examiner's prior Office Action (IYW27NTZRXEAPX0) which paraphrased or characterized external references; the paraphrase is being used as record evidence of the Examiner's prior positions. The actual text of the external references has not been provided in the record."
  ]
}

