      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "HNEG9OW2PXXIFW2",
        "I6HVFCOJPXXIFW4",
        "I9TTIG25PXXIFW4"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The earlier Office action applied 35 U.S.C. 103 over Giglio to claims that require stages located in the same or more than one building (multiplication/duplication of Giglio's facility). The applicant's amendments add a \"walled off\"/sound isolation limitation to independent claim 1 (and to the single-show independent claim 9/7 in various submissions), and applicant argues that this distinguishes over Giglio. However, the Examiner continues to find that duplicating/partitioning the facility and incorporating sound‑isolating partitions or glass barriers would have been obvious to a person of ordinary skill in view of Giglio's disclosure of stage sections and known architectural partitioning; the dependent claims' added limitations remain within the scope of routine architectural/structural design choices and therefore unpatentable as obvious over the cited prior art unless applicant shows a non‑obvious functional effect or that Giglio necessarily excludes such arrangements.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "I6HVFCOJPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claims 4-6 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as being unpatentable over Giglio et al.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "I9TTIG25PXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claim 2 (new): An attraction facility in accordance with claim 1, wherein the stages are walled off from the visitors in line to a stage by partitioning walls.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    }
  ],
  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant amended claim 1 to add that stages are \"walled off\" so visitors in line cannot hear what is said on stage unless provided broadcast sound, and traversed the Giglio-based 102 rejection asserting Giglio's stages are part of a panoramic production and are not the same as the invention's per-section recreated stages.",
      "response": "The amendment adds a structural/arrangement limitation. The prior Office action considered the television-game-show language to be an intended use; Giglio was found to disclose stages and sectioned stage areas capable of being used for different performances. A traverse stating that the objects/subject matter differ between the disclosures does not, by itself, identify a clear factual error in the Examiner's reliance on Giglio. To overcome the 102 rejection applicant must either: (1) point to express disclosure in the record that Giglio does not disclose or teach partitioning/walling/sound isolation of stages from visitors in line (by citing specific passages of Giglio), or (2) amend the claims to recite a definite structural feature not taught or enabled by Giglio (for example, a specific construction or arrangement not mere partitioning—e.g., an active soundproofing system with defined components and operation—or other novel structural details disclosed in the specification that are not present in Giglio). The current amendment is broadly stated (\"walled off ... in such way that the said visitors in line cannot hear...\") and on the present record can be read as encompassing ordinary architectural partitions or glass barriers. The applicant's remarks that Giglio's stages are used in a 360° panoramic production does not, without more, establish that Giglio lacks the capability (or disclosure of arrangements) to isolate stage sound; therefore the 102 rejection is maintained. If applicant believes a particular passage of Giglio contradicts the Examiner's understanding, submit that passage for consideration.",
      "support_doc_id": "I6HVFCOJPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Claims 1-3 and 7 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) as being anticipated by Giglio et al U.S. Patent 5,765,314."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        4,
        5,
        6
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argued that dependent claims 4-6 (now numbered variably in amendments) incorporate the new limitation of claim 1 and therefore distinguish over Giglio; applicant requested allowance.",
      "response": "Dependent claims that merely add conventional locational limitations (same building or more than one building) to an independent claim that is otherwise not shown to be structurally distinct from the prior art will not render the claim nonobvious when the prior art discloses the same facility and a person of ordinary skill would have been motivated to replicate or multiply stages to serve more visitors. The prior Office action explained duplication of Giglio's facility to accommodate additional viewers/participants is within ordinary skill. The applicant must show either (a) that the combination produces an unexpected result or non‑routine effect, or (b) point to record disclosure in Giglio that would teach away from duplication/partitioning and sound isolation. Absent such showing, the 103 rejection for the dependent claims is maintained.",
      "support_doc_id": "I6HVFCOJPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Claims 4-6 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as being unpatentable over Giglio et al."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Section A — Status of claims and amendments\nThe application is being continued under examination. I have reviewed the applicant's May 14, 2015 and September 30, 2015 amendment papers (I9TTIG25PXXIFW4; IFFHQE4PPXXIFW4) and the earlier non-final Office action dated February 23, 2015 (I6HVFCOJPXXIFW4). The following claim set (as presently before the Office in the record) is treated for purposes of this action as the claimed scope to be examined: independent claim 1 as amended to add that stages are walled off so visitors in line cannot hear what is being said on stage unless provided broadcast sound (I9TTIG25PXXIFW4); dependent claims adding partitioning walls or glass barriers and the location-based dependent claims; and an independent claim directed to multiple copies of a single game-show stage with the same walled‑off limitation (I9TTIG25PXXIFW4). If the applicant intends different claim text to be entered, submit a clean replacement set and explicitly state which prior claim versions are to be withdrawn.\nSection B — Rejections maintained and reasoning\n1) Rejection under 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) — Claims 1–3 and 7 (as amended and/or pending)\nThe February 23, 2015 Office action rejected original claims 1–3 and 7 as anticipated by Giglio et al. (see O1: \"Claims 1-3 and 7 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) as being anticipated by Giglio et al U.S. Patent 5,765,314.\"). The applicant amended independent claim 1 to add that the stages are \"walled off\" from visitors in line so those visitors cannot hear what is said on stage unless provided broadcast sound (I9TTIG25PXXIFW4). The applicant's remarks state that this amendment was intended to provide a structural distinction over Giglio and that Giglio's disclosure is of a 360° panoramic production (I9TTIG2DPXXIFW4; IFFKCIDHPXXIFW4).\nA claim is anticipated only if a single prior art reference discloses each and every limitation of the claimed invention. The earlier Office action found that Giglio discloses an attraction facility comprising a plurality of sections, each including a stage, and that nothing in the claims' television-show language imposed structural limitations that Giglio's stages could not satisfy — i.e., the Examiner treated the television-game-show aspects as an intended use and concluded Giglio's structures are capable of being used in the claimed manner (O1). The present amendment adds an isolation/\"walled off\" limitation, but on the record as it stands this limitation is broad (e.g., partitioning walls, glass barrier, or other conventional architectural partitions) and does not recite a particular construction, active sound attenuation system, materials, or otherwise-specific hardware that Giglio demonstrably lacks. Absent a showing that the cited Giglio reference expressly and necessarily excludes the claimed partitioning/sound isolation arrangement, or absent a showing in the record that the applicant's added limitation is a novel structural limitation not present in Giglio, the Examiner maintains the anticipation rejection because Giglio was previously found to disclose stage sections and there is no persuasive evidence in the applicant's remarks establishing that Giglio cannot, or does not, disclose partitioned stages or arrangements that would prevent nearby queued visitors from hearing stage audio.\nWhat is required to overturn this rejection: to overcome the 102 rejection the applicant should either (A) identify precise, supporting passages in Giglio showing the absence of any partitioning or sound‑isolation structure (i.e., a passage in Giglio that indicates the lack of walls, barriers, or sound control) such that Giglio does not disclose all claim limitations, or (B) further amend the claims to recite specific structural features or active components (e.g., defined sound‑attenuating wall construction, specified gap tolerances, active noise‑cancelling system with recited components and operation, or other tangible elements disclosed in the specification) that are not found or suggested in Giglio. A conclusory traverse that the objects/subject matters differ is not sufficient to establish non‑anticipation.\n2) Rejection under 35 U.S.C. 103 — Claims 4–6 (dependent claims directed to location/duplication)\nThe February 23, 2015 Office action rejected claims 4–6 as unpatentable over Giglio, reasoning that duplicating the facility or providing more than one building would have been an obvious modification to Giglio's facility to accommodate additional visitors. The applicant states that dependent claims incorporate the new limitation of claim 1 and therefore distinguish over Giglio (I9TTIG2DPXXIFW4; IFFKCIDHPXXIFW4). However, the dependent claims as presented add conventional locational limitations (e.g., \"all stages located in the same building\" or \"located in more than one building\") that, on the present record, are within routine architectural/operational choices. The Examiner continues to find these dependent claims unpatentable as obvious since Giglio discloses stage sections and one of ordinary skill would have been motivated to duplicate/partition or arrange stages across multiple buildings for capacity or operational reasons.\nWhat is required to overturn this rejection: to overcome the 103 rejection the applicant must provide either (A) persuasive factual evidence that the claimed combination produces a non‑obvious technical effect beyond mere duplication/relocation (for example, a demonstrated unexpected improvement in acoustic isolation achieved by a claimed wall construction that is not taught by Giglio), or (B) point to explicit language in Giglio that teaches away from duplicating the facility or arranging stages across multiple buildings and thereby refutes the Examiner's factual premise. Alternatively, amending the claims to recite specific structural details or functional mechanisms not found in Giglio would alter the obviousness analysis.\nSection C — Response to applicant remarks\nThe applicant's remarks (May 14 and Sept 30, 2015) state the intent behind the amendments and assert that no new matter was introduced (I9TTIG2DPXXIFW4; IFFKCIDHPXXIFW4). The Examiner acknowledges the applicant's position and has considered the amendments. The applicant's statement that Giglio's stages are for a 360° panoramic production and therefore different is noted, but on the current record that difference has not been shown to be a factual basis to negate the Examiner's citations or conclusions. The applicant is invited to more specifically identify passages in Giglio (the full text of Giglio was cited in O1) that bear on partitioning or sound isolation, or to submit supporting evidence (affidavit, declaration, or technical references) demonstrating that the claimed structural limitation is not disclosed or would not have been obvious in view of Giglio.\nSection D — Opportunity to amend/submit evidence\nThe applicant may: (1) file remarks pointing to specific text from Giglio that shows Giglio lacks the newly-added limitation(s); (2) further amend claims to recite specific structural elements or configurations (for example, identified structural elements of the wall/barrier or an active sound control system with defined components) that are neither disclosed nor suggested by Giglio; or (3) submit declarations, expert evidence, or other documentary evidence demonstrating that the claimed arrangement yields unexpected results or that Giglio teaches away. If the applicant files an amendment that overcomes all outstanding rejections, the Examiner will consider allowance. Alternatively, if the applicant chooses to contest the rejections without further amendment, provide specific and persuasive evidence or argument addressing the points identified above.\nConclusion / Next action\nThe outstanding rejections under 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) for independent claims (as amended) and 35 U.S.C. 103 for the dependent claims are maintained for the reasons stated above. This action is continued non‑final to give the applicant opportunity to respond with the evidence or claim changes described above. If the applicant replies with convincing argument and/or evidence that overcomes the grounds of rejection, prosecution may be advanced toward allowance. If the applicant files no reply within the statutory period, the application will remain rejected as set forth in this action.\n\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The record does not include the full text of the cited Giglio reference (only the Examiner's identification of Giglio as the basis for the rejections is in the file). Therefore the Examiner's characterization of Giglio in the earlier Office action is relied upon as the basis for the rejections; if the applicant submits or points to specific Giglio passages that contradict that characterization, the Examiner will re-evaluate.",
    "It is not yet established on the record whether the applicant's May 14 and September 30, 2015 amended claim texts were formally entered as the application's claim set at the time of this action; this Office action addresses the amendments as presented in the file wrappers cited (I9TTIG25PXXIFW4, IFFHQE4PPXXIFW4) but the applicant should confirm claim entry or submit a clean claim set if a different set is desired."
  ]
}

