      "claim_ids": [1],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues Tregub lists amyl (pentyl) octanoate as an example vehicle and that amyl octanoate has a boiling point of about 260 °C (NIST), so Tregub does not teach a solvent system with boiling point >=300 °C or a solvent system comprising at least 90 wt% ester and therefore cannot render claim 1 obvious.",
      "response": "The Examiner has considered the point. Tregub teaches ester-based vehicles (including amyl octanoate as an example) and provides working evaporation/boiling-temperature and surface-tension values that overlap the claimed ranges. Where the claimed ranges overlap prior-art ranges, a prima facie case of obviousness exists. The fact that a specific example in Tregub (amyl octanoate) has a lower boiling point than the claim does not negate the prima facie case because Tregub's disclosure of vehicle blends and solvent properties would have led a skilled artisan to select an ester (or mixture of esters) having a higher boiling point to meet the recited operational/evaporation conditions. Applicant’s submission of an external boiling-point data point for amyl octanoate (NIST) is noted; however that data point does not show that other esters with higher boiling points and similar solvent functionality would not have been obvious choices to meet the claimed numerical values. Absent prior-art evidence showing that the claimed numerical ranges are unobtainable or produce unexpected results, the Examiner maintains the prima facie case. If applicant submits comparative data showing unexpected properties or showing that substituting other esters to reach >=300 °C would fail, the Examiner will consider it.",
