  "office_action": "Examiner Action (Continuation of Record / Non-Final)\n\nSummary of Action\n- The application has been examined in view of the record, the prior office action (CTNF mailed 03 Feb 2016), and the Applicant's Amendment and Reply filed 25 Mar 2016 (A1). The Applicant's amendments have been entered for consideration where indicated in the file. Claims 1-19 and newly added claims 34-48 are pending. Claims 3 and 4 remain withdrawn per the restriction/election practice noted in the record. This action maintains the earlier rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 for certain groups of claims and indicates allowability for other claims consistent with the prior Examiner finding; it also explains the Examiner's response to the Applicant's A1 arguments.\n\nCurrent Claim Scope (claims of record after A1 entry)\n- Independent method claims recite methods of forming EML and HTL layers using ester-based solvent systems with specified boiling/evaporation and surface-tension ranges and with solvent-system composition limitations (e.g., at least 90 wt% ester, and in new claims 34/38 at least 99 wt%). Several claims now specifically recite octyl octanoate and mixtures of diethyl/octyl octanoate (new claims 42-48).\n\nIssues Requiring Further Prosecution\n1) Rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 (maintained):\n   - Claims 1, 2, 5, and 10: Rejected as obvious over Tregub (WO 2013/040468).\n     Rationale: Tregub teaches formation of OLED layers using ester-containing vehicle(s) and provides working evaporation/boiling temperature and surface-tension ranges that overlap the claimed ranges. Because the claimed numerical ranges overlap the prior-art ranges and because Tregub teaches selecting ink vehicle/vehicle blends for printing OLED layers, a prima facie case of obviousness is established that the claimed method ranges and solvent-system concept would have been arrived at by a person of ordinary skill with reasonable expectation of success.\n     Response to Applicant's A1: Applicant correctly notes that one specific example cited in Tregub (amyl/pentyl octanoate) has a boiling point lower than 300 °C. That single data point does not, however, overcome the prima facie case because Tregub discloses vehicle blends and broad workable properties (evaporation temperature/boiling point and surface tension) for inks; these teachings would have led a skilled artisan to choose an ester or ester blend having higher boiling/evaporation temperature while still functioning as an ink vehicle—i.e., substitution of one ester for another is within the ordinary skill of the art. Absent compelling evidence in the record that such substitution would have been unsuccessful or produces unexpected properties, the Examiner is not persuaded to withdraw the rejection. If Applicant believes that substituting higher boiling esters is not routine, Applicant should submit experimental evidence demonstrating unexpected properties or that inks meeting the claimed parameters would not have been predictable or enabled by the prior art.\n\n   - Claims 11-13, 16-18: Rejected as obvious over Tregub in view of Yamauchi (US 2012/0211729).\n     Rationale: Tregub supplies the general teaching for forming HTLs with ester vehicles and overlapping solvent-property ranges; Yamauchi teaches the inclusion of crosslinking polymers in HTLs. The combination provides motivation to include crosslinking polymers while using ester-based solvent systems within the recited ranges. The Examiner finds that one of ordinary skill would have been prompted by these teachings to form HTLs with crosslinking polymers and to choose suitable ester solvent systems to achieve the taught processing/evaporation properties.\n     Response to Applicant's A1: Applicant's point about amyl octanoate's boiling point and the absence of a specific teaching to use it at 90 wt% is noted. The Examiner's combination does not rely on using amyl octanoate at 90 wt% to reach 300 °C; instead it relies on the general disclosure of ester vehicles, solvent-property ranges, and the teachable option to include crosslinkable polymers in HTLs. As with the EML claims, evidence of unexpected properties or unexpected criticality is needed to overcome the prima facie case. Absent such evidence, the rejection is maintained.\n\n2) Allowable Subject Matter (as earlier acknowledged):\n   - Claims previously found allowable in the record (claims directed explicitly to octyl octanoate and certain dependent species) are accorded allowance status subject to the absence of newly cited art. Specifically, the earlier office action indicated claims 6-9, 14-15, and 19 were allowable because the cited prior art did not disclose octyl octanoate and no motivation was found in the then-available search record to modify the prior art to include octyl octanoate. Applicant has redrafted and added claims 42-45 (EML with octyl octanoate) and 47-48 (HTL diethyl/octyl octanoate mixtures) that correspond to the previously-acknowledged-allowable claims. Accordingly, claims 42-45 and 47-48 are in condition for allowance based on the present record and absent new art that overcomes the prior determination. Note: claim 46 (two or more alkyl octanoates) and claim 19 (diethyl octanoate/octyl octanoate combination) were previously found allowable (19 was allowed earlier); however, further search for prior art specific to the diethyl/octyl octanoate mixture may be conducted to confirm allowance for claims reciting those mixtures (claims 46-48). If the Office locates relevant art directed specifically to octyl octanoate or the diethyl/octyl octanoate mixtures, applicant will be notified.\n\nResponse to Applicant's Specific Submissions in A1\n- Applicant submitted argument (A1 Remarks) asserting that Tregub's example (amyl octanoate) has a boiling point of ~260 °C and thus cannot supply the claimed >=300 °C teaching. The Examiner acknowledges the data point but reiterates that Tregub's disclosure of vehicle blends and overlapping temperature ranges supports a prima facie case that selecting other ester solvents to meet the claimed numerical values would have been within routine skill. The mere presence of a lower-boiling example in Tregub does not negate the combination rationale, absent evidence of non-analogous behavior or non-functionality of other esters.\n- Applicant indicated submission of an external NIST page (addendum) for amyl octanoate boiling point. That factual datum (boiling point of a specific ester) is noted, but does not by itself rebut the Examiner's prima facie case. If Applicant prefers, experimental evidence that inks composed of the claimed ester-rich solvent-systems produce unexpected properties (e.g., improved uniformity, nozzle compatibility, or CV% improvements beyond predictable results) should be submitted with verifiable data and experimental details for consideration.\n\nActions/Next Steps\n- The rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 are maintained for claims 1, 2, 5, 10, 11-13, 16-18 based on the cited references and rationale set forth above.\n- Claims 42-45 and 47-48 (and previously allowed corresponding claim numbers) are in condition for allowance on the present record. If applicant wishes allowance of those claims, applicant may indicate acceptance of allowance and pay the necessary issue fees after any formalities are satisfied; or applicant may request allowance be withheld pending resolution of outstanding dependent claims—please indicate preference.\n- Applicant may traverse the maintained rejections by one or more of the following (each with supporting evidence where appropriate):\n   a) Submit persuasive factual evidence and explanation showing unexpected results or criticality of the claimed solvent system compositions and/or numerical ranges compared to the prior art. Experimental data must include comparative samples, measurement methods, and reproducible results.\n   b) Amend claims to add limiting features not suggested in the cited art (for example, specific ester identities with structural or functional limitations not taught or suggested by Tregub/Yamauchi), or to add process conditions/steps that are not derivable from the cited art.\n   c) Provide persuasive legal argument demonstrating that the cited references do not in fact teach or suggest the claimed limitations or that the combination would not have been made by one of ordinary skill in the art with a reasonable expectation of success.\n\nEvidence and Procedural Notes / Limitations\n- The record includes the prior office action (IK4156HXRXEAPX1) and the Applicant's A1 submission (IMDJS3ETPXXIFW2, IMDJS3F5PXXIFW2). Applicant indicated an addendum (NIST amyl octanoate data) in the A1 but that document is not present among the USPTO documents made available for this action. If Applicant submitted such evidence to the file, ensure it is entered into the file; otherwise resubmit the evidence or include it with a future reply. The Examiner cannot rely on documentary evidence that is not of record.\n- The Examiner's earlier restriction/election determination and acknowledgment of applicant's election remain part of the prosecution record; claims withdrawn under that election remain withdrawn unless rejoinder occurs per applicable practice.\n\nConclusion\n- Maintained: 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections for claims 1, 2, 5, 10, 11-13, 16-18.\n- Allowable (on present record): claims directed specifically to octyl octanoate-based solvent systems (claims 42-45) and claims corresponding to previously-allowed claims (47-48) are in condition for allowance absent newly-cited art.\n\nOffice Interview\n- The Applicant is invited to request an interview to discuss the issues set forth in this action and to present any experimental data or arguments. An interview may expedite resolution if Applicant can present persuasive new evidence or amendments.\n\nExaminer: Nathan T. Leong\nArt Unit: 1715\n\n",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant asserted a NIST boiling-point datum for amyl (pentyl) octanoate in the A1 addendum, but that addendum file is not present among the documents supplied to the examiner in this record; the Examiner cannot rely on documents not of record — if applicant provided such a document, it must be entered into the prosecution record or resubmitted.",
    "The Examiner's prima facie obviousness determinations rest on Tregub's broad teaching of ester vehicles and overlapping numerical ranges; if applicant provides persuasive experimental evidence of unexpected results or non-routine effects, the Examiner will reconsider the rejections.",
    "Search availability for art specifically teaching octyl octanoate and diethyl/octyl octanoate mixtures is limited to the cited record; prior allowance determinations for octyl-octanoate claims were based on the then-available search. If new prior art directed to these specific esters is located, the allowance status may be reconsidered."
  ]
}

