  "office_action": "Examiner Communication\nSummary\nThis communication follows the Office action mailed June 5, 2014 (HVZIDU1VPXXIFW4) and is in response to applicant's Amendment and Response filed August 18, 2014 (HZ07CPXXPXXIFW3; HZ2PPVOZPXXIFW2; HZ2PPVQVPXXIFW2; HZ2PPVRHPXXIFW2). Applicant submitted replacement drawings and repeated arguments that Figure 3 (provisionally filed) supports operability. The application remains under pre-AIA, first-to-invent provisions. Claims under examination are 1, 5-12, 15-22, 24-28, and 31-33, the elected species per the restriction/election action.\nDrawings\nThe replacement drawing sheets filed 8/18/2014 (HZ2PPVOZPXXIFW2) are acknowledged and will be entered into the record. Entering corrected artwork addresses the Examiner's earlier formal objections to figure clarity and letter sizing. The Examiner will consider the replacement figures for substantive evaluation. However, mere replacement of a figure file does not by itself resolve the substantive factual bases for prior rejections. If applicant asserts that the replacement figures correct an error in the prosecution-image rendering that materially affects the experimental interpretation, applicant must accompany the replacement figure with explanatory evidence (see below under Evidence Required).\nRestriction/Election Status\nThe restriction/election made during prior prosecution remains in effect. Claims withdrawn as nonelected (claims 2-4, 13, 14, 23, 29, 30) remain withdrawn from consideration. The claims presently under examination are those elected (listed above). No change to election status is made by this communication.\nStatus of Rejections and Required Response\nThe following substantive rejections stand on the record and are maintained at this time:\n1) 35 U.S.C. 112(a) — Written Description / Enablement (first paragraph): Claims 1, 5-12, 15-22, 24-28, and 31-33. Rationale: The Examiner previously relied upon Example 3 and Figure 3 as they appeared in the application file to conclude that the experimental data in the record do not demonstrate possession or enablement of the elected species (porcine ECM covalently bound to IDA and functionalized with Zn) for the asserted antimicrobial function. The Examiner noted that the Figure 3 data, as then in the record, indicated that the IDA-Zn combination had higher CFU than other comparison groups, contradicting the claimed antimicrobial effect. This discrepancy raises a written-description/enablement issue because applicant's specification and exemplified data, as in the prosecution record, must reasonably convey that the inventors possessed and enabled the claimed species.\n2) 35 U.S.C. 101 — Utility / Operability: Claims 1, 5-12, 15-22, 24-28, and 31-33. Rationale: Because the Examiner determined that the experimental data in the prosecution record fail to demonstrate the claimed antimicrobial effect for the elected species, the Examiner concluded that the claims are directed to an inoperative species for the asserted purpose and thus lack the particular utility claimed. This is a factual determination based on the application record as examined.\nWhat Applicant Argued and Why the Rejections Are Maintained\nApplicant argues that (i) the replacement Figure 3 and the provisional application Figure 3 show that IDA-Zn functionalization does provide antimicrobial activity, and that (ii) the appearance in the originally-filed Figure 3 was altered by an image conversion issue during electronic filing. Applicant requests withdrawal of the §112 and §101 rejections, and states corrected drawings were filed.\nThe Examiner has entered the corrected drawings. Nevertheless, the Examiner maintains the §112(a) and §101 rejections for the elected claims because the Examiner's prior rejections were based on the prosecution record as it existed when the June 5, 2014 Office action was issued and on the factual interpretation of Example 3 and Figure 3 contained in that record. Replacement drawings alone (without corroborating explanatory evidence) do not automatically negate the factual basis for the prior rejections. If applicant contends that the earlier figure in the record was erroneous or misleading due to conversion or upload error and that the corrected figure (or the provisional figure) accurately reflects the experimental results, applicant must place competent evidence into the record to demonstrate that the corrected figure is an accurate representation of the original experimental data and that those data show operability for the elected species.\nEvidence Required to Overcome the 112/101 Findings\nTo permit reconsideration and possible withdrawal of the rejections, applicant should, within the response period, submit one or more of the following items addressing the Examiner's specific factual concern about Example 3 and Figure 3:\n- A declaration under 37 C.F.R. § 1.132 by an individual with direct knowledge (for example, the experimenter or an author of the Example), identifying the experiment(s) of Example 3, explaining the experimental methods and controls in sufficient detail, and stating that the corrected Figure 3 (entered 8/18/2014) is an accurate representation of the experimental results (identify the figure by the filing date and figure number). The declaration should specifically address the elements the Examiner relied upon (i.e., whether the IDA-Zn bar demonstrates lower CFU than comparators for the same experimental conditions and wash/no-wash groups). If the experimenter used specific concentrations, incubation times, and wash protocols, these should be set forth in the declaration and, if appropriate, appended as a short methods description or table. The declaration should be signed and dated as required by 37 C.F.R. § 1.132.\nOR\n- A certified copy of U.S. Provisional Application No. 61/637,413 including the prototype Figure 3 and any lab notebooks, raw data, or contemporaneous records that substantiate the results claimed for the IDA-Zn condition. If applicant relies on the provisional to show possession and enablement, submit a certified copy of the provisional (or supply the provisional serial number evidence as appropriate) and highlight where the pertinent data appear in the provisional. Note that merely asserting that the provisional contains supportive data without providing the provisional or a certified copy will be insufficient to rebut the Examiner's factual finding.\nOR\n- New experimental data added to the record (with an appropriate enabling written description and reproducible methods), accompanied by a § 1.132 declaration attesting to the accuracy of the new data. New data should be described sufficiently in the submission (methods, concentrations, incubation times, CFU counts, controls). The Examiner will consider such new matter but it is subject to rules regarding new matter and may require amendment of the specification if new matter is added; applicant should take care to observe the rules and to present data as confirmation of what was in the application at the filing date rather than as new matter that extends beyond the originally filed disclosure.\nIf applicant timely files one or more of the above items and the evidence persuasively demonstrates that the corrected Figure 3 (or provisional Figure 3) accurately reflects operable, reproducible antimicrobial activity for the examined species, the Examiner will withdraw the written description/enablement and utility rejections and continue prosecution on the merits (including any other outstanding issues). If applicant does not provide such evidence, the Examiner will maintain the rejections and an appropriate next action (which may include a final rejection under 35 U.S.C. 112 and/or 101 or an examiner's amendment/call for further amendment) will be mailed.\nOther Matters\n- Double patenting: The Examiner's prior note that application 13/777,153 was considered is for the record and does not presently alter the grounds above.\n- Restriction/election: As noted above, the election without traverse stands and the nonelected claims remain withdrawn from consideration.\nAction Required\n1) If applicant desires reconsideration of the 35 U.S.C. 112(a) (written description/enablement) and/or 35 U.S.C. 101 grounds, submit within the statutory/reply period: a § 1.132 declaration addressing the Figure 3/Example 3 factual issue and/or a certified copy of the provisional showing the claimed data and/or new experimental data with methods and results. If relying on the provisional, submit it as evidence by certified copy and identify where the data appear. If relying on the replacement figure alone, supply a § 1.132 declaration explaining the discrepancy and affirming the corrected figure's accuracy. The Examiner will consider the submission and respond accordingly.\n2) If applicant does not timely provide the requested evidence, the Examiner will continue to maintain the current rejections and proceed to a subsequent action which may be final in nature with respect to the rejections on record.\nLimitations and Procedural Notes\n- The Examiner entered applicant's replacement drawings; however, the Examiner's earlier factual conclusions were based on the prosecution record as it existed at the time of the June 5, 2014 Office action. Replacement figures need supporting explanatory evidence to rebut the factual conclusions.\n- The provisional application (61/637,413) is not automatically part of the patent application record unless provided or relied upon with supporting certified material; applicant offered a statement referencing the provisional but did not provide a certified provisional copy in the A1 submission. If applicant wishes the provisional to be considered as evidence of possession/operability, include a certified copy or other appropriate proof in the reply.\n- The Examiner will accept a telephone contact regarding submission content and procedural questions. Contact information remains as stated in the record.\nConclusion\nThe asserted 35 U.S.C. 112(a) (written description/enablement) and 35 U.S.C. 101 (utility) rejections for the elected claims are maintained at this time. The replacement drawings have been entered but the Examiner requires affirmative evidence (declaration and/or certified provisional copy and/or additional experimental data with methods) placed into the record to rebut the factual basis for the rejections. Upon timely submission of such evidence the Examiner will reconsider the rejections. If no such evidence is submitted, the Examiner will proceed with prosecution preserving the current rejections.",
  "limitations": [
    "The record does not contain a certified copy of the cited provisional (61/637,413); applicant referenced it but did not supply a certified copy in the A1 response — so the Examiner cannot rely on its content until it is placed in the record.",
    "The Examiner's factual conclusions in the June 5, 2014 action were based on the prosecution-file versions of Figure 3; replacement drawings alone are insufficient to rebut those factual conclusions without supporting declaration or corroborating data.",
    "If applicant supplies new experimental data, care must be taken regarding new matter rules; the Examiner cannot treat post‑filing matter as proof of original possession without appropriate procedural handling (declaration, certification, or amendment)."
  ]
}

