  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        1
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues Benveniste does not teach numbers of fundamental channels used for RTS and CTS, and Fontaine discloses only frequency bands for physical channels (2.4/5 GHz and 60 GHz) and not explicit counts of minimum-bandwidth fundamental channels or the stated relation 'second number is smaller than or equal to the first number.' Applicant contends the combination does not supply the claimed numeric relationship.",
      "response": "The Examiner has considered the applicant's arguments and the amended claim language. The Examiner acknowledges that Benveniste focuses on RTS/CTS-style reservation and does not explicitly recite 'first number' or 'second number' of fundamental channels in the applicant’s numeric form. However, Fontaine teaches sending RTS on a first physical channel (2.4/5 GHz) where respective channel widths may be 10/20/40 MHz and sending CTS on a second physical channel (60 GHz) with different channel widths. A person of ordinary skill would understand that a physical channel of a given total bandwidth can be described equivalently as composed of a number of minimum-bandwidth fundamental channels (e.g., 20 MHz fundamental channels commonly used in IEEE 802.11 family), and would have been motivated to map Fontaine’s disclosed channel widths to numbers of such fundamental channels for implementation. Further, the Examiner relies on the combination of teachings to reasonably infer the claimed relation that the 'second number is smaller than or equal to the first number' in situations where the physical-channel/bandwidth mapping taught in Fontaine (and the primary-channel teaching in Fischer where invoked) would lead the skilled artisan to select the number of constituent minimum-bandwidth channels accordingly. The Examiner finds no persuasive technical distinction in the amendment that renders the claimed subject matter non-obvious over the cited combination. This position is supported by the Office action (HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4) where Fontaine is cited for teaching first/second physical channels and channel widths.",
      "support_doc_id": "HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Fontaine teaches on a first number of fundamental channels, wherein the first number is an integer greater than or equal to 1 ... RTS sent over first physical channel ... 5 GHz physical channel with channel width of 10, 20 or 40 MHz"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        9
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant amended system claim 9 similarly to include 'second number is smaller than or equal to the first number' and argues Benveniste and Fontaine do not teach the numeric counts or relation.",
      "response": "For the system claim, the same reasoning applies. Benveniste teaches modules that perform RTS/CTS exchanges on channels and Fontaine supplies the teaching that the RTS and CTS may be transmitted on different physical channels and widths. The combination renders the recited system modules that send/receive on respective collections of fundamental channels obvious, including the numerical relationship when a skilled artisan implements Fontaine’s described channel-width arrangement using standard minimum-bandwidth channels. Therefore the rejection under 35 U.S.C. 103 is maintained for the reasons stated in the prior Office action and recited above.",
      "support_doc_id": "HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "As to claim 9 Benveniste teaches a system for channel reservation ... wherein the sending station comprises: a first sending module ... a first receiving module ... a channel reservation module ..."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "REJECTION MAINTAINED — Continue Non-Final Action\nIntroduction\nThis communication responds to Applicant's Amendment/Response filed 12 November 2014 (I2EYPR2OPXXIFW3, I2HWLTL6PXXIFW2, I2HWLTM4PXXIFW2). The claims presently of record include the set filed 12 February 2013 (HD4O0SVBPXXIFW2) and the claim amendments proposed 12 November 2014. The prior Office action mailed 12 August 2014 (HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4) made rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 over combinations of Benveniste, Fontaine and other secondary references. Applicant's arguments and the amended claims have been considered, and the record is updated as follows.\nSummary of Examiner's Position\n- The Examiner has reviewed Applicant's Amendment adding the limitation \"wherein the second number is smaller than or equal to the first number\" to independent claims 1 and 9 and Applicant's accompanying remarks. - For the reasons set forth below, the prior 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections are maintained. The combination of Benveniste and Fontaine (with Fischer/Ghaboosi/Maltsev as to particular dependent claims) continues to render the claimed subject matter prima facie obvious. Applicant's arguments do not persuasively show the amended claims are patentable over the cited art.\nDiscussion — Substantive Points\n1) Benveniste (primary reference) and the generic reservation exchange\nAs stated in the prior Office action and supported by Benveniste's description, Benveniste discloses a method and apparatus for coordinating adjacent channel transmissions by exchanging reservation signaling akin to RTS/CTS, where a sending node issues a reservation request and the receiving node responds to accept or decline. The Examiner relies on Benveniste for the general sequence of: sending a reservation request, receiving an acknowledgement/clear-to-send, and thereby completing reservation. See HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4: \"Benveniste teaches a method for channel reservation ... a sending station sending a channel reservation request message ... the receiving station sending a channel reservation acknowledgement message ... the sending station receiving the channel reservation acknowledgement message, and completing channel reservation with the receiving station.\" This maps to the broad recitation of claim 1's core reservation steps.\n2) Fontaine (secondary reference) and the multi-channel / different-physical-channel teaching\nFontaine discloses use of two physical channels for contention/RTS/CTS operations and expressly describes that the first physical channel may be in 2.4/5 GHz bands with particular channel widths (10/20/40 MHz), while the second physical channel may be in 60 GHz bands with different absolute channel characteristics. The Office action cites Fontaine to supply the teaching that RTS may be sent over a first physical channel and CTS over a second physical channel and that physical channels have particular bandwidths. See HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4: \"Fontaine teaches ... RTS sent over first physical channel ... 5 GHz physical channel with channel width of 10, 20 or 40 MHz\" and \"CTS sent over a second physical channel/60GHz physical channel with width of 2160 MHz\".\nA person of ordinary skill in the art would understand and implement physical channels of specified total width as being composed of constituent minimum-bandwidth 'fundamental channels' (for example, 20 MHz units in IEEE 802.11 practice). Consequently, mapping Fontaine's described channel widths to numbers of such fundamental channels would have been an obvious and routine design choice to implement Fontaine's teaching in a system that uses minimum-bandwidth channels as building blocks. The Examiner therefore finds that Fontaine supplies the skilled artisan with the necessary teaching to express the physical-channel disclosure as a number of fundamental channels and supports the Examiner's combination with Benveniste.\n3) Applicant's argument that Fontaine only discloses frequency bands, not counts of fundamental channels, is considered but not persuasive\nApplicant asserts that Fontaine discloses frequency bands for the two physical channels but not explicit counts of minimum-bandwidth channels, and that therefore the Examiner's reliance on Fontaine to supply the numeric 'first number' and 'second number' (and their relationship) is improper. Applicant also argues the frequency-band teaching does not establish the claimed relation \"second number is smaller than or equal to the first number.\"\nThe Examiner acknowledges the distinction between 'frequency band' and 'bandwidth' but finds the combination of Fontaine's bandwidth/physical-channel teachings and the commonplace implementation practice of decomposing wider channels into minimum-bandwidth units makes the claimed numerical characterization an ordinary design choice. Additionally, the claimed relationship \"second number is smaller than or equal to the first number\" is not a non-routine, narrow numerical requirement that would necessarily render the claimed subject matter non-obvious — rather, it is a routine selection consistent with the cited teachings where the artisan would choose channel widths (thus numbers of fundamental channels) to achieve the desired data-rate / compatibility trade-offs. For these reasons, Applicant's arguments do not overcome the Examiner's prima facie case of obviousness.\n4) Fischer, Ghaboosi, Maltsev — dependent claim support\nThe earlier Office action relied on Fischer to supply teaching about primary-channel transmission/reception for RTS/CTS and on Ghaboosi for channel pre-negotiation between source and destination, and on Maltsev for frame structure/modulation/rate details. Applicant's amendment and arguments did not present persuasive reason to withdraw reliance on these references for the dependent limitations they are cited against. The combination of Benveniste/Fontaine with the respective secondary references continues to provide the teachings relied upon for dependent claim rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103.\n5) The amendment 'wherein the second number is smaller than or equal to the first number' does not overcome the Examiner's prima facie case\nThe newly-added limitation has been considered. The Examiner finds that the cited references, taken in combination, would have provided the skilled artisan with rationale to select numbers of minimum-bandwidth channels for the RTS and CTS transmissions and to select them so that the second number would be less than or equal to (or equal to) the first number, for example in order to preserve backward-compatibility on a primary channel or to optimize throughput on the sending side. Applicant's rebuttal does not show that this limitation imparts a non-obvious structural or functional improvement over the cited art.\nConclusion and Requirement for Further Prosecution\nBecause the record supports the combination of references and because Applicant's arguments and claim amendments therefore do not establish patentability, the rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 for the claims identified in the prior Office action are maintained. This action is therefore a Continue Non-Final (CTNF) rejection; prosecution is continued. Applicant is invited to respond by either: (1) submitting substantive arguments and/or evidence (e.g., declaration, technical comparisons, or claim amendments narrowing to distinguish over the cited combination) that specifically rebut the Examiner's reasons for combining the references and demonstrate patentable distinction, or (2) proposing further claim amendments to more clearly recite structural/functional limitations that are not suggested by the cited art. If an Examiner interview is desired to discuss the rejections and claim amendments, please contact the Examiner to schedule.\nIf Applicant wishes to traverse the maintained rejections, Applicant should specifically identify where in the cited references the asserted distinguishing feature(s) are lacking and explain why the cited combinations would not have rendered the claimed subject matter obvious to one of ordinary skill in the art. General statements of non-teaching or conclusory assertions without pointing to specific differences will not overcome a prima facie case of obviousness.\nCurrent Claim Status\n- The following rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103 are maintained: claims 1-20 as listed and grouped in the prior Office action (HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4). The maintained groupings and secondary references are summarized in the claim_ground_evidence section above.\nLimitations\n- The record available to the Examiner consists of the prior Office action (HYRAEBJ6PXXIFW4), the originally-filed specification/claims (HD4O0SVBPXXIFW2 and HD3L35TNPXXIFW3 / HD3L35U5PXXIFW3), and Applicant's Amendment/Response (I2EYPR2OPXXIFW3, I2HWLTL6PXXIFW2, I2HWLTM4PXXIFW2). - If Applicant relies on an interpretation of any cited reference that departs from the Examiner's understanding, Applicant should identify exact passages in the cited references and explain the appropriate interpretation. - If Applicant believes any cited reference was mis-cited or that a factual error appears in the prior Office action, Applicant should point to the exact text in the cited reference and provide persuasive explanation why the Examiner's reading is incorrect.\nNext Steps\nApplicant may: (1) submit a substantive reply addressing the maintained rejections (including amendments supported by explanation), (2) request an interview with the Examiner, or (3) file a reply traversing the maintained rejections with supporting evidence. A reply that does not overcome the maintained rejections will result in a subsequent Office action or eventual final action consistent with prosecution practice.\nExaminer Note: The Examiner has considered all arguments presented in Applicant's reply papers. Only those arguments pertinent to the maintained rejections are discussed above. The Applicant's attention is directed to the claim_ground_evidence and applicant_argument_responses sections of this communication for the recorded bases for the maintained rejections and the Examiner's responses to Applicant's arguments.",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant's A1 arguments rely on interpretations of reference text — if Applicant contends specific quoted passages exclude the Examiner's interpretation, cite exact passages from the prior-art documents.",
    "The record does not include any submitted declaration or technical evidence showing unexpected results, commercial success, or long-felt need which could rebut obviousness; if Applicant has such evidence, it must be submitted.",
    "The Examiner's mapping of physical-channel widths to numbers of minimum-bandwidth 'fundamental channels' is based on ordinary design practice and the passages in Fontaine; if Applicant disputes that mapping, identify exact contrary text in the cited references."
  ]
}

