  "office_action": "Office Action (Non-Final) - Response to Applicant's Amendment dated 22 Dec 2014\nIntroduction\nApplicant’s amendment filed 22 Dec 2014 (document I40JLR5YPXXIFW3) amends, cancels, and adds claims. The amendment is acknowledged and is entered to the extent consistent with statute and regulation. The following sets forth the examiner’s disposition of outstanding grounds of rejection in view of the amendment and the prosecution history of record (including the restriction/election history). The status of prior rejections is explained and remaining rejections are maintained where appropriate. Applicant is advised of the action to be taken and options for reply.\n1) Entry of Amendment\n- Applicant’s amendment to claims was received on 22 Dec 2014 and is hereby entered for consideration. The newly amended independent claims are 1, 7, 16 and 19. New dependent claims 20–33 have been added, and multiple claims were cancelled as indicated in the amendment. The amendment supplies antecedent basis and new structural limitations (pair of side members defining an opening positioned between the rail portion of the upper extension/upper portion and the top rail of the upper receiver in assembled configuration).\n2) Withdrawal of 35 U.S.C. 112(b) Rejection for Amended Claims\n- Background: In the last action (I08CCH5TPXXIFW4), claims 1, 2, 6, 8 and 9 were rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) for indefiniteness due to insufficient antecedent basis (quote: \"Claims 1, 2, 6, 8 and 9 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112(b)\"). The rejection arose from claim language using \"upper portion\" and \"upper rail extension\" inconsistently, rendering metes and bounds unclear.\n- Examiner determination: The amendment to claims 1, 7, 16 and 19 supplies clear antecedent language and defines the claimed components (e.g., \"upper rail extension\" in claim 1 and explicit recitation of a \"rail portion and a pair of side members defining an opening\"). Accordingly, the 35 U.S.C. 112(b) rejection is withdrawn as to the entered amended claims (1, 7, 16, 19) because the indefinite antecedent problem has been cured by the amendment. The withdrawal is based on the amendment text (I40JLR5YPXXIFW3): \"wherein the upper rail extension has a rail portion and a pair of side members defining an opening proximate to an end of the rail portion ...\".\n- Note: Claims not amended or claims cancelled by the amendment are not impacted by this withdrawal (canceled claims are not pending).\n3) Reconsideration of Prior Art 102/103 Rejections in View of the Amendment\n- Background: Prior to amendment, the examiner applied several prior art references (Swan US 8,201,353; Troy US 2012/0324775; Daniel US 2010/0126054; E'Nama US 5,198,600) and rejected many claims under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102 (anticipation) and applied 103 rejections for certain dependent claims in combination with Brown US 2012/0036756. The prior rejections (I08CCH5TPXXIFW4) asserted that the cited references disclosed upper rail extensions/openings proximate a distal end or sight and the modular removable structures.\n- Applicant’s amendment adds the limitation that the upper rail extension/upper portion \"has a rail portion and a pair of side members defining an opening proximate to an end of the rail portion ... located between the rail portion ... and the top rail of the upper receiver when ... secured.\" This defines the opening as being bounded by side members and explicitly located between the extension’s rail portion and the upper receiver’s top rail in the assembled configuration.\n- Examiner determination re: anticipation (35 U.S.C. 102): After review of the cited references on the record, the prior art as applied in the September 22, 2014 Office action does not explicitly teach the combination of features now recited in amended independent claims 1, 7, 16 and 19 — specifically, a pair of side members that define an opening and the spatial limitation that the opening is located between the rail portion of the extension/upper portion and the top rail of the upper receiver when secured. The references previously cited disclose various openings proximate to a distal end or proximate to a sight (the Office action cited passages such as Swan’s disclosure of opening 31 and Troy/Daniel’s openings), but the record does not show an express teaching of the claimed pair-of-side-members structural limitation together with the explicit assembled-location limitation as newly claimed. Therefore the pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(e)/(b) anticipation rejections as to amended claims 1, 7, 16 and 19 are withdrawn.\n- Examiner determination re: obviousness (35 U.S.C. 103): For the newly amended independent claims (1, 7, 16, 19), no combination of the currently cited references of record has been shown to render the exact combination of added limitations obvious. Accordingly, the previously-applied 102 rejections are withdrawn for these amended independent claims. However, this determination is based on the current record and the specific prior art applied previously; additional prior art may be cited in subsequent Office action if located that discloses or renders obvious the newly-claimed limitations.\n4) Reconsideration and Maintenance of 35 U.S.C. 103 Rejection for Claim 6\n- Background: Claim 6 (dependent on claim 1) remains pending and recites that \"the release mechanism further comprises a pair of members pivotally mounted to the bottom portion wherein each of the pair of members have a portion configured to be received within a feature of the upper rail extension.\" In the September 22, 2014 Office action the examiner rejected the corresponding limitation as obvious over Brown in view of Swan/Troy/Daniel/E'Nama (quote: \"Claims 6 and 14 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) ... in view of US 2012/0036756 to Brown\"). Brown discloses a pivotal member received in a feature and the other references disclose modular rail/upper extension structures. The examiner found it would have been within routine skill to duplicate or reverse the location of such pivotal members.\n- Applicant’s amendment did not alter claim 6 and did not submit arguments distinguishing the combination of Brown with the other references with respect to the pivotally-mounted pair of members limitation.\n- Examiner determination: The 103 rejection of claim 6 is maintained. The record supports combining Brown’s teaching of a pivotal member received in a mating feature with the modular rail structures of Swan/Troy/Daniel/E'Nama to arrive at the claimed arrangement (see prior discussion in I08CCH5TPXXIFW4 and supporting citation in the Notice of References Cited PTO-892). Applicant may traverse this 103 rejection by (a) amending claim 6 to add a limiting feature not suggested by the cited combination, or (b) presenting persuasive arguments or evidence that the combination would not have been made or that Brown’s teaching would not have been applied to the modular rail structures for some teaching-away or technical incompatibility reason. Mere attorney argument unsupported by facts or evidence will not be sufficient.\n5) Status of New Dependent Claims (20–33)\n- Applicant added dependent claims 20–33 directed to positional relationships (opening located forward of a sight opening, pair of side members securing/abutting forward end, gas-block/piston removal) and application of those features to the modular rail system, upper receiver and method claims. Some of these new dependent claim limitations closely track disclosed features in the specification/figures (the specification describes embodiments with an opening 80 providing access to the sight/gas block and removal of piston sleeve). However, the prior art of record (e.g., Swan, Daniel, Troy, E'Nama) disclosed openings proximate a sight or distal end, and in many cases disclosed structures enabling access to or removal of gas-block components. Because the new dependent claims are narrow and derive support from the specification, they are entered but will be examined for novelty and obviousness in view of the entire prior art of record. At present, no affirmative allowance of these new dependent claims is made. The examiner finds that the prior art of record may be pertinent to certain of these dependents; a full examination finding as to each new dependent will be made after review and comparison to the entire cited art and any art submitted in reply. Applicant should be prepared to present arguments or amendments if prosecution of these dependents is contested.\n6) Action Taken and Options to Applicant\n- The following rejections are withdrawn as to the entered amended independent claims (1, 7, 16, 19): the 35 U.S.C. 112(b) rejection and the anticipatory 35 U.S.C. 102 rejections previously applied in the September 22, 2014 action based on the record references cited therein.\n- The 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection to claim 6 (in view of Brown combined with Swan/Troy/Daniel/E'Nama) is maintained.\n- New dependent claims 20–33 are entered and will be examined for novelty and obviousness in light of the prior art of record.\n- This action is non-final. Applicant is invited to reply to the maintained rejection of claim 6 and to address any prior art the examiner may cite in a subsequent action. To advance prosecution, Applicant may: (a) reply with substantive arguments as to why Brown (and any combination with the other references) does not render claim 6 obvious; (b) amend claim 6 to add distinguishing structure or limitations not suggested by the cited combination; (c) cancel claim 6; and/or (d) submit evidence (e.g., declarations) addressing unexpected results, teaching away, or other pertinent factual issues.\n7) Examiner's Guidance and Suggested Reply Topics\n- For claim 6, to overcome the 103 rejection the applicant should either amend claim 6 to recite a structural or functional limitation not taught or suggested by Brown in combination with the other references, or present technical argument/evidence why Brown would not have been applied to the modular rail systems of Swan/Troy/Daniel/E'Nama (for example, incompatibility of attachment configurations, difference in pivot mounting orientation that would prevent the claimed interaction, or other factual differences). Citations in the record indicate Brown’s pivotal member is mounted to the upper portion in Brown’s disclosure; Applicant may argue that Brown’s member location, function, or mating geometry would not be reasonably applied to the bottom portion members of the other references, but such argument should be supported by record evidence or technical reasoning.\n- For the newly entered dependent claims (20–33), Applicant should be prepared to identify specifically where support in the specification/drawings exists for each newly-claimed limitation (applicant has asserted the specification provides support). If prosecution continues with further rejections based on art, Applicant may amend further or submit argument/evidence as appropriate.\n8) References of Record Cited in Prior Actions (representative)\n- US 8,201,353 (Swan) — cited in prior action for openings and modular rail structure (I08CCH5TPXXIFW4). See earlier office action statement: \"Swan discloses ... upper portion has an opening, 31, proximate to a distal end ...\".\n- US 2012/0324775 (Troy) — cited for modular rail/upper portion with opening (I08CCH5TPXXIFW4). See earlier office action statement: \"Troy discloses ... upper portion has an opening ...\".\n- US 2010/0126054 (Daniel) — cited for modular rail/upper portion with opening (I08CCH5TPXXIFW4).\n- US 5,198,600 (E'Nama) — older patent with removable bottom/upper portion and opening proximate sight (I08CCH5TPXXIFW4).\n- US 2012/0036756 (Brown) — cited for pivotal member teaching combined with other disclosures (I08CCH5TPXXIFW4).\n9) Procedural Notes\n- This is a non-final action. Applicant’s responsive reply should address the maintained 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection of claim 6 and any other issues raised by the examiner, and may include amendment(s), argument(s), or evidence as appropriate. Applicant may also present a request for interview to discuss claim 6 and the cited combination. If Applicant elects to cancel or amend claim 6 in a manner to avoid the maintained rejection, prosecution on the merits of the remaining claims may be advanced more rapidly.\nLimitations\n- The examiner’s withdrawal of earlier 35 U.S.C. 112(b) and 102 rejections is based on the current record and the specific prior-art references previously relied upon. If additional prior art is located that discloses the newly-claimed combination of limitations (pair of side members and the explicit assembled-location limitation), such art may be applied in a subsequent Office action.\n- Examiner’s assessment of obviousness for claim 6 is based on the record references of 22 Dec 2014 and prior Office actions; the maintained rejection reflects the examiner’s view that Brown’s teaching reasonably would have been combined with the modular rail disclosures absent persuasive arguments or evidence to the contrary.\nConclusion\n- Entered: Applicant’s amendments (I40JLR5YPXXIFW3) are entered. The 35 U.S.C. 112(b) rejection is withdrawn as to the entered amended independent claims. The earlier 35 U.S.C. 102 anticipatory rejections applied to the now-amended independent claims are withdrawn. The 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection of claim 6 (Brown combined with other references) is maintained. New dependent claims 20–33 are entered and will be examined in due course.\n- Applicant is given the opportunity to reply to the maintained 103 rejection of claim 6 by amendment, argument, or evidence. A reply to this action must be timely filed. The shortened statutory period for reply set in the non-final action cover letter applies.\nExaminer: Bret C. Hayes\nArt Unit: 3641",
  "limitations": [
    "Withdrawal of prior rejections is based on the current record and the specific prior-art references currently of record; other prior art not yet cited could lead to renewed rejections.",
    "The maintained 35 U.S.C. 103 rejection for claim 6 rests on the combination of Brown with the modular rail references of record; Applicant did not amend claim 6 or present distinguishing evidence in the A1 response."
  ]
}

