  "applicant_argument_responses": [
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        3
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant traversed the §103 rejection arguing Walter teaches batch (not continuous) processing and therefore does not teach the claimed continuous method; applicant also argued Hayes actually discloses a single tank divided by a weir and that the references do not suggest combining a micro-still into a cleaning system.",
      "response": "The examiner has considered the traversal and the cited portions of Walter, Hayes, Donnelly, and Tonkovich. The rejection is maintained. The examiner relies on the references as combined in the prior action: Walter provides the general cleaning/rinsing and solvent recovery context and identification of disposing/recycling rinse solvent; Hayes provides the teaching of separating cleaning and rinse zones to reduce rinse contamination; Donnelly provides the teaching of distillation to recover and recycle contaminated rinsing solvent; and Tonkovich specifically discloses a micro-still (including condenser and bottoms withdrawal). The examiner finds it would have been within the ordinary skill of the artisan to combine these known elements (distillation/micro-still, pump, level control, condenser) to obtain the predictable result of continuously separating and returning purified rinse solvent while directing concentrated contaminants to waste. Differences between batch and continuous embodiments in an art of solvent recovery are not dispositive where the combination teaches and would motivate adapting distillation and pumps/level controls for continuous replenishment; applicant has not submitted evidence showing unobvious technical advantage tied to the combination that overcomes the prima facie case of obviousness.",
