      "claim_ids": [
        10,
        14
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argued dependent claims 10 and 14 (distill then condense back to rinse tank) are nonobvious only if independent claims 3/13 are nonobvious; applicant asserts the combination improperly uses hindsight.",
      "response": "As independent claim 3 (and claim 13) are found unpatentable over the combination, the added limitation of distillation plus condensation as recited in claims 10 and 14 is taught by Donnelly (distillation and condenser returning cleaned solvent) and Tonkovich (micro-still with condenser). The examiner continues to find a motivation to combine these teachings to recover rinse solvent for reuse. Applicant's hindsight assertion is noted but not persuasive without objective evidence showing the claimed combination yields unexpected results or solving a long-felt need in a nonobvious way.",
      "support_doc_id": "HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "The micro-still of Tonkovich includes a condenser to obtain a distillate. (para 0051 of Tonkovich) ... Donnelly ... a condenser eventually returns the cleaned solvent (col 4 lines 35-65) to be reused as stated by Walter (col 10 lines 45-55)."
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        11,
        12,
        15,
        16
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant contested rejections of claims reciting pumping and liquid level control activation, asserting these are routine automation features and dependent on nonobvious independent claims.",
      "response": "The cited references (Donnelly in particular) teach routing contaminated solvent by pump to a distillation tank and controlling feed responsive to tank level; Tonkovich and Donnelly show bottoms removal and liquid level related controls. Use of pumps and level controls to feed a still and control fill/empty cycles is a known expedient; combining these known controls with a solvent recovery/distillation system to achieve continuous recovery is within ordinary skill. These dependent claim limitations do not impart patentability in view of the applied prior art absent additional technical effect or evidence.",
      "support_doc_id": "HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4",
      "support_span": "Donnelly ... contaminated solvents are manually pumped ... to tank 12 ... distillation tank 14, condenser 16, and clean solvent tank 18 ... (col 3 lines 35-40; col 6 lines 30-60)."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner Action (Non‑Final rejection maintained and confirmed as final):\nSummary\nClaims pending in the application consist of elected method claims 3 and 10–16. The prior final restriction (CTNF) and examiner’s rejections under pre‑AIA 35 U.S.C. 103 were considered together with applicant’s July 8, 2014 response (traverse and correction to claim 16). Applicant’s amendments cure the formal antecedent objection to claim 16; however, the substantive obviousness rejections are maintained and are hereby confirmed (final). The examiner explains the reasons for maintaining the rejection and provides the examiner’s rationale and citations relied upon.\nClaims under consideration and current scope\n- Claim 3 (elected): a method for continuously separating contaminants from rinse solvent: (a) treating a contaminated substrate previously treated with a solvating agent with a rinsing solvent in a separate rinse tank such that contaminants collect in the rinse tank; and (b) removing contaminated rinsing solvent from the rinse tank to a micro‑still to separate contaminants from rinsing solvent, directing rinsing solvent back to the rinse tank, and directing residual solvating agent and contaminants to waste disposal.\n- Claims 10–12: dependent on claim 3 and recite distillation/condensation (10), pumping feed to the micro‑still (11), and feeding responsive to still liquid level control (12).\n- Claims 13–16: alternative independent method (13) with dependents (14–16) reciting distillation/condensation, pumping, and pump activation by level, respectively.\nGrounds of rejection and reasoning\n1) Obviousness — pre‑AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) (Claims 3, 10–16):\nThe claims are rejected as obvious over the combination of Walter (U.S. 6,348,101 B1), Hayes (U.S. 5,716,457), Donnelly (U.S. 6,159,345), and Tonkovich (US 2006/0016215). The examiner’s position is supported by the record and summarized as follows:\n- Walter provides a method of cleaning electronic components using washing and rinsing fluids and contemplates disposal, recycling, and reuse of rinse solvent (examiner cited portions: col. 2–3; col. 10). The cited examiner record states: “Walter teaches a method for continuously separating contaminants from rinse solvent ... treating a contaminated substrate ... with a rinsing solvent ... and removing contaminated rinsing solvent from said rinse tank (col 10 lines 10-55).” (HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4).\n- Hayes teaches separating cleaning and rinsing zones (use of different solvent zones/sections) to minimize contamination of the rinsing liquid (examiner cited: Hayes col. 9–10). The record notes: “Hayes teaches that it is known to apply solvating agents in one tank and the rinse fluid in another tank ... in order to achieve the predictable result of removing contaminants and residual solvating agents ...” (HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4).\n- Donnelly discloses distillation recovery systems for contaminated solvent and returning cleaned solvent to service (examiner cited: Donnelly col. 3 and col. 4 lines describing tank, distillation and condenser feed/return). The record states: “Donnelly teaches a method that utilizes distillation ... for recovering and recycling solvents ... prepare them to be reused again.” (HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4).\n- Tonkovich discloses a micro‑still assembly with distillation, condenser, and bottoms withdrawal (examiner cited: abstract and paras 0051–0052). The record notes: “Tonkovich teaches that it is well known to use a micro‑still for a distillation process ... and for withdrawing a bottoms product from the system.” (HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4).\nRationale for combination: The cited references supply complementary teachings: Walter/Hayes provide the cleaning/rinse context and the practice of separating cleaning and rinsing to reduce contamination; Donnelly provides the teaching of distillation recovery and routing of contaminated solvent to a distillation apparatus and returning cleaned distillate to service; Tonkovich supplies a specific micro‑still architecture (including condenser and bottoms removal). It would have been within the routine skill of the artisan to adapt known pumps, level controls and a micro‑still into a rinse system to achieve continuous recovery and reuse of rinse solvent. Combining these known elements yields the claimed method steps (continuous removal of contaminated rinsing solvent to a micro‑still, distillation/condensation to return rinse solvent to rinse tank, and removal of concentrated residual solvating agent/contaminants to waste).\nAddressing applicant arguments\n- Batch versus continuous: Applicant argues Walter shows batch embodiments and thus cannot teach continuous operation. The examiner recognizes multiple embodiments in the art (batch and continuous) but finds the differences do not impart patentability because the art (Walter, Donnelly, Tonkovich) teaches and would motivate adapting distillation and feed controls (pumps, liquid level) for continuous replenishment. The claimed result (continuous separation and return of rinsing solvent) is a predictable outcome of combining the cited teachings.\n- Hayes teaching: Applicant argues Hayes discloses a single tank divided by a weir. The examiner relied on Hayes’ teaching that separating cleaning and rinse regions reduces contamination of rinse liquid; whether implemented as sections of a single tank or discrete tanks, Hayes teaches the conceptual separation that motivates using a separate rinse reservoir and practices to minimize contamination — a motivation to use distinct rinse handling and hence to route contaminated rinse for recovery.\n- Micro‑still combination: Applicant asserts no suggestion to add a micro‑still to a Walter/Hayes system. The examiner finds Tonkovich supplies the micro‑still teaching and Donnelly supplies the distillation recovery concept; combining these known art teachings with Walter/Hayes is a predictable application of well‑known distillation technology to the known problem of rinse solvent contamination and recovery.\nDependent claims (10–12, 14–16): The dependent limitations (distillation + condenser, pumping, level control activation) are taught or suggested by Donnelly and Tonkovich (feeding contaminated solvent to a distillation tank by pump, level‑responsive control, condenser returning liquid). These are routine/known expedients in solvent recovery; applicant has not presented objective evidence of unexpected results or other facts overcoming the prima facie case of obviousness.\nConclusion and next steps\n- The improper antecedent objection to claim 16 has been cured by applicant’s amendment; the formal objection is therefore withdrawn. (Claim 16 now reads with proper antecedent: \"... in said micro‑still.\")\n- The obviousness rejection under 35 U.S.C. 103 for claims 3 and 10–16 is maintained and is hereby made final. The examiner finds applicant’s traversal unpersuasive because the combination of prior art teachings reasonably renders the claimed combination obvious to a person of ordinary skill in the art.\n- Applicant may file a Reply to Final Rejection (R. 1.116) with amendments placing the claims in better form to distinguish the prior art (for example, by adding specific structurally or functionally limiting features not suggested by the cited references and supported by the specification, or by presenting objective evidence of nonobviousness such as unexpected results, long‑felt need, commercial success, or failure of others). Any proposed amendment should be responsive to the specific prior art findings set out above and should be commensurate with the original disclosure.\n- If applicant wishes to preserve other subject matter, divisional filings may be pursued for withdrawn/non‑elected apparatus or other claims.\nCited examiner support (record): HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4 (Examiner detailed rejection) as the record source for the combination rationale; specification paragraphs and examples remain available as disclosure support for claim limitations (see SPEC / HDHIWM50PXXIFW3 and amended SPEC HSHWT0BZPXXIFW3).",
  "limitations": [
    "This action relies on examiner characterizations and specific quote spans from the examiner’s April 8, 2014 Office Action document HTR7LS2ZPXXIFW4. The exact full text and column/line context of the external prior art references (Walter, Hayes, Donnelly, Tonkovich) are not provided in full in the record available here; the examiner’s statements about those references are treated as the examination record evidence.",
    "Applicant’s traversal and arguments were considered, but no new experimental data or objective evidence of unexpected results was submitted in the record to rebut the prima facie obviousness finding."
  ]
}

