  "office_action": "Examiner Response to Applicant’s February 24, 2015 Submission\nIntroduction\nThis communication responds to the applicant’s Reply filed February 24, 2015 (A1 documents I6JQ7G0APXXIFW3, I6JQ7G1DPXXIFW3, I6JQ7G1RPXXIFW3) addressing the Office action mailed December 15, 2014 (O1 document I3PX7NH5PXXIFW4). The December 15, 2014 Office action rejected claims 1-7, 9, 12-16, and 18-20 under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over Lee in view of Kennedy, further in view of Homan, and indicated claim 8 as containing allowable subject matter. The applicant has argued against those rejections. After consideration of applicant’s arguments and the record, the Examiner maintains the rejections for the identified claims for the reasons set forth below, and confirms claim 8 remains allowable as previously indicated.\nRepresentative basis for maintained rejections (35 U.S.C. 103)\n1) Claims rejected and primary rationale\nClaims 1-7, 9, 12-16, and 18-20 remain rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as unpatentable over Lee in view of Kennedy, further in view of Homan. The prior Office action identified the combination rationale and specific teachings relied upon (I3PX7NH5PXXIFW4: \"Claims 1-7, 9, 12-16, and 18-20 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103(a) ... over US 2006/0124360 (Lee) in view of US 2006/0028321 (Kennedy), further in view of US 2010/0071794 (Homan)\"). The combination as explained in the Office action is summarized here to respond to applicant's arguments.\n- Lee discloses modeling and simulation of a bottom hole assembly (BHA) and downhole operations, including explicit references to BHA modeling and simulating downhole conditions (see Office action quoting Lee: \"BHA Modeling\" at para [0432] and \"to simulate downhole conditions as closely as possible, and conduct a mock intersection test at surface\" (Lee: para [0368])). These teachings are suitable for predicting BHA response and possible contact behavior of a rotating drillstring.\n- Kennedy teaches placement an