  "office_action": "Office Action — Non-Final (follow-up to A1)\nSummary\nThis non-final action responds to Applicant’s Amendment under 37 C.F.R. § 1.111 filed Sep. 2, 2015 (IE3AJE9GPXXIFW3 et seq.) and applicant remarks (IE3AJEA8PXXIFW3). The prior Office action dated Jun. 4, 2015 (IAFRWV4BPXXIFW4) is incorporated by reference and considered in full. The examining attorney has considered the amended specification and proposed claims. The following determinations and actions are made at this time.\n1) Claim status and entry of amendments\n- The examiner has entered the originally-pending claim set (claims 1–14) as previously examined (HJIRVRJ9PXXIFW3). Applicant submitted an amended claim listing with the A1 (IE3AJE9GPXXIFW3). The entry status of the amended claim set filed Sep. 2, 2015 is currently under consideration because formal entry requires compliance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 and other entry requirements (e.g., clean marked-up replacement sheets or an applicant’s signed request to enter). Accordingly, the presently-examined claims in this Office action are the originally filed claims 1–14 as set forth in the record (HJIRVRJ9PXXIFW3). The examiner has treated applicant’s proposed amended claims as persuasive arguments and potential amendments; applicant is encouraged to ensure formal entry of the amended claim set per 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 to have the amended claims considered as the active claim set.\n2) 35 U.S.C. 112 — informalities and clarity\n- The earlier formal objection for claim 4 (missing word 'temperature') is considered addressed by applicant’s A1 amendment; that objection is therefore withdrawn. (See IE3AJE9GPXXIFW3, claim 4 amended to recite compression raising \"a temperature of the steam\").\n- The prior 35 U.S.C. 112(b) (pre-AIA second paragraph) rejections of claims 7 and 14 as indefinite have been reviewed in view of the A1 amendments. Applicant’s clarifying language for these claims now specifies that the heat exchanger supplies part of the steam heat to the absorbing liquid in the regeneration column 'by means of heat exchange with the absorbing liquid in the regeneration column.' This amendment overcomes the Examiner’s earlier indefiniteness concern for those claims. The 112(b) rejections for claims 7 and 14 are therefore withdrawn.\n3) 35 U.S.C. 102(b) — Anticipation by Chao (maintained)\n- The examiner has reviewed Applicant’s arguments that Chao differs in working fluid (e.g., potassium carbonate vs. amine solutions), that Chao’s device 186 is a steam jet/ejector rather than a mechanical compressor, and that Chao does not teach compressively-heated steam raised above the regeneration temperature. Applicant’s arguments have been considered but are not, at this time, persuasive to overcome the anticipation rejection applied in the prior action.\n- The prior action mapped claim limitations to Chao’s Fig. 2 and Example 2 streams and explained how Chao discloses production of vapor from solution and delivery of heated steam to the regenerator (IAFRWV4BPXXIFW4). Where Chao explicitly teaches generating vapor from solution and delivering steam-derived heat to the regenerator, those functional teachings correspond to the primary claim limitations for independent claims 1 and 8 and dependent claims that recite similar features. The 102(b) rejections for the previously-examined claims (listed in the prior action) are therefore maintained.\n- Applicant may distinguish Chao by: (a) amending claims to recite specific structural limitations not taught in Chao (for example, explicitly reciting a mechanical compressor of specified type, or structural detail of the steam-generator arrangement), and/or (b) submitting persuasive evidence or technical declaration showing Chao’s disclosed devices and operating conditions cannot perform the claimed compression/temperature-raising limitation as properly construed. If narrowing claim language is submitted, applicant should provide support in the specification for the narrowed language.\n4) 35 U.S.C. 103(a) — Obviousness (maintained)\n- The prior action found certain dependent claims (3, 5–6, 10, 12–13) unpatentable as obvious over Chao in view of common design choices (e.g., providing a second generator, arranging multiple compressors in series). Applicant’s A1 arguments do not provide persuasive factual differences or non-obvious technical advantages sufficient to overcome the Examiner’s findings. The 103 rejections for those dependent claims are therefore maintained.\n- If applicant believes an actual teaching-away, unexpected technical effect, or other objective indicia of non-obviousness apply, submit such evidence (e.g., declarations, comparative data, or contemporaneous technical literature) and specify claim limitations to which the evidence pertains.\n5) Drawings objection (still outstanding)\n- The June 4, 2015 action objected to the drawings because reference character “12” was used to designate both filling materials and pump, and required corrected replacement drawing sheets (IAFRWV4BPXXIFW4). Applicant has not submitted corrected replacement drawing sheets complying with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121(d) in the present A1. The drawing objection therefore remains outstanding. To avoid abandonment, submit corrected Replacement Sheet(s) that include all figures on the replaced sheet(s) and an Annotated Sheet showing changes from the prior drawing version, labeled per 37 C.F.R. § 1.121(d)(1). The objection will not be held in abeyance.\n6) Newly added claims (15–16)\n- Claims 15–16 were newly added in A1 and recite that the absorbing liquid 'includes an amine absorbant.' These claims were not part of the claim set earlier acted on and have not been examined for novelty/obviousness by the prior action. The patentability of these claims is unresolved. Formal entry of the amended claim set is necessary before those claims can be fully examined. If applicant desires entry of these claims for examination, ensure the Sep. 2, 2015 claim set is formally entered in the record per 37 C.F.R. § 1.121.\n7) Examiner response to Applicant arguments regarding Chao and 'compression device'\n- Applicant argues Chao’s 'steam jet 186' acts as a vacuum-inducing device and not as a compression device that raises steam temperature by compression work. The characterization of Chao’s device affects how the claim limitation should be construed. If Applicant contends the claim term 'compression device' requires a specific type of compressor (e.g., a mechanical compressor producing temperature increase by compression), Applicant should either: (a) amend the claims to expressly define the required compressor structure (preferred to remove ambiguity), or (b) provide argument supported by evidence that Chao’s disclosure cannot meet that structural limitation. Without such narrowing or evidence, the Examiner construes the limitation to encompass conventional means for producing heated steam from generated vapor where the claimed structural or functional result is met (i.e., steam generated from solution and delivered to regenerator at sufficient temperature), which Chao reasonably teaches.\nAction required of Applicant\n- If Applicant wishes prosecution to continue, respond within the time set by 37 C.F.R. § 1.134 with one or more of the following: (1) formal entry of the amended claim set submitted Sep. 2, 2015 (IE3AJE9GPXXIFW3) by providing properly-labeled replacement sheets and an annotated marked-up copy, per 37 C.F.R. § 1.121(d); (2) substantive argument and/or evidence (e.g., declaration, technical comparison) showing why Chao does not anticipate or render obvious the claims as properly construed; and/or (3) narrowing claim amendments to recite structural or functional limitations not taught by Chao (for example, a specified mechanical compressor type, explicit wording that excludes ejectors/ejector-type steam jets, or other structural features supported by the specification). Also submit corrected replacement drawing sheets and the required Annotated Sheets to resolve the outstanding drawing objection.\n- If Applicant elects to amend the claims, be mindful that claim amendments may raise new issues of written description or new matter; provide support in the specification for any narrowing language.\nConclusion\n- The examiner has withdrawn the informal/formal objection to claim 4 and the indefiniteness rejections under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) for claims 7 and 14 based on the A1 amendments. The remaining 102(b) and 103 rejections for the previously-examined claims are maintained. The drawing objection remains outstanding. Newly added claims 15–16 are not yet examined and are unresolved pending formal entry of the amended claim set. Applicant is advised to respond promptly and to provide any evidence or amendments necessary to place the application in condition for allowance.\nLimitations\n- The record does not include a formal replacement drawing sheet responsive to the prior drawing objection; therefore the drawing objection remains outstanding.\n- Applicant submitted amended claims in A1 but did not complete formal entry per 37 C.F.R. § 1.121; the examiner has treated those amendments as arguments and possible amendments but has not formally entered them as the active claim set. If Applicant believes the amended claim set should be the active set, submit the required formal entry materials.\n- The examiner's application of Chao is based on the mappings and stream-table interpretation of Chao as set forth in the Jun. 4, 2015 Office action (IAFRWV4BPXXIFW4). The examiner relied on the text and figures of Chao as recorded in the prosecution file; a claim amendment specifically excluding device types (e.g., ejectors) or requiring a particular compressor structure would narrow the claim and may alter the application of Chao.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant’s Sep. 2, 2015 amended claim set was not formally entered per 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 at the time of this action; examiner treated those proposed claims as arguments and potential amendments but maintained rejections against the previously-entered claim set.",
    "No corrected replacement drawing sheets complying with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121(d) were submitted to resolve the prior drawing objection; the drawing objection therefore remains outstanding.",
    "Examiner relied on Chao as set forth in the prior Office action (IAFRWV4BPXXIFW4); if Applicant submits technical evidence or a persuasive claim construction that narrowly defines 'compression device' to exclude devices disclosed in Chao, rejections may be revisited."
  ]
}

