  "office_action": "Examiner Response (Non-Final) - Summary and Action\nIntroduction\nThe Office has considered the applicant's Amendment and Remarks submitted December 22, 2016. This communication responds to the arguments and claim amendments and continues the prosecution of U.S. App. Ser. No. 14/935,107.\nStatus of Claims\nThe claims currently of record are those filed December 22, 2016 (document IX8WCVSYPXXIFW1). The following rejections remain outstanding for the reasons stated below: (1) 35 U.S.C. §101 rejection of independent claims 1, 9 and 14 (and their dependents); (2) 35 U.S.C. §103 rejections based on Li et al. and Park et al. for the indicated groups of claims; and (3) 35 U.S.C. §112 issues for claims 14-20 related to the \"RF module\"/functional limitations. Claim 8 remains objected-to as noted previously and may be allowable if rewritten in independent form including intervening limitations.\nI. 35 U.S.C. §101 — Abstract Idea Rejection (Claims 1, 9, 14 and dependents) — MAINTAINED\nBackground: The prior Office action (Sept. 22, 2016; ITBY8A5XRXEAPX0) rejected claims 1, 9 and 14 under 35 U.S.C. §101 as directed to an abstract idea (mathematical relationships and information organization) and lacking \"significantly more.\" The applicant amended the independent claims to recite that the recited steps are performed by \"one or more processors\" executing \"computer executable ... program code.\" Applicant argues these additions make the claims statutory.\nExamination and Analysis: Under the current subject-matter eligibility framework, the claims must be examined under the two-step Alice/Mayo test. The claimed subject matter, even as amended, calls for receiving a reference signal, calculating numeric ratios (signal-to-noise, interference-to-noise), comparing the ratios to a threshold, generating bit information (an interference flag), and feeding back values to a base station so the base station can perform MU-MIMO scheduling. These steps are mathematical relationships and information processing steps — an abstract idea. The mere recital that these steps are performed by generic processors executing \"program code\" does not transform the abstract idea into patent-eligible subject matter because the claims do not recite a specific improvement in computer technology, specialized hardware, or an unconventional programming technique that meaningfully limits the claim to significantly more than the abstract idea. The Examiner has considered the specification; while hardware elements such as processor 210 and RF module 230 are described, the claims as amended specify only generic \"program code\" and generic processors, and thus the limitation is a conventional computer implementation of the abstract idea. The applicant has not identified a claim feature that meaningfully confines the claim to a specific, inventive application of the abstract idea.\nConclusion: The 35 U.S.C. §101 rejections of independent claims 1, 9 and 14 (and their dependent claims) are maintained. If the applicant wishes to overcome the §101 rejection, suggested options include: (a) amending the claims to recite the particular structural elements described in the specification (for example, reciting the specific RF module 230, processor 210 and their interconnections and unique configurations) and any non-conventional, specific data structures or steps that amount to an improvement in the functioning of the computer or network; or (b) presenting persuasive argument with record support that the claimed combination of elements provides a concrete improvement in the functioning of the device or network beyond abstract data manipulation. Merely appending ‘‘program code’’ executed by a generic processor will not suffice.\nII. 35 U.S.C. §103 — Obviousness Rejections (Claims 1-7, 9-13, 14-20) — MAINTAINED\nReferences relied upon in the prior action (Li et al. US 8,953,475 and Park et al. US 2012/0218913) were made of record and the Office set forth detailed mappings of claim limitations to the teachings of those references. The applicant argues Li does not disclose the second value (interference-to-noise ratio, i.e., MUIb) and that the combination therefore fails to teach the claims.\nAnalysis: The cited references are directed to related wireless terminal/base-station feedback for MU-MIMO. Li discloses estimating SINR/CQI values from reference signals and reporting channel-state-related measures to a base station. Park discloses generating an interference-reflecting CQI and an MUI flag (one-bit MUI) and reporting CQI/MUI for MU scheduling. Combining the references to implement reduced-feedback MU scheduling — where terminals report a primary channel-quality metric and a simplified interference indicator — would have been within the level of skill of the ordinary artisan and would have had a reasonable expectation of success. Park provides the teaching of flagging an interference condition and reporting an MUI flag; Li provides techniques for computing and reporting reference-signal-based measures such as SINR. An ordinarily skilled artisan would have been motivated to combine these teachings to reduce feedback overhead while enabling MU scheduling. The applicant's argument that Li's step 503 is not identical terminology is acknowledged, but the claimed second value (ratio of interference to noise) is an inherent and obvious modification of the SINR/CQI computations taught by Li when combined with Park's MUI teachings.\nConclusion: The §103 rejections over Li in view of Park (and Park in view of Li for claims 9-13) are maintained. If applicant wishes to rebut the rejections, applicant should: (1) amend the claims to recite specific features that distinguish over the applied combination (for example, non-conventional steps, particular structures, or numerical relationships not taught or suggested by the combination); or (2) structure an argument that identifies persuasive factual differences and evidence (e.g., technical advantages shown in the specification) that would have made the cited combination non-obvious to a person of ordinary skill. Unsupported attorney argument is insufficient.\nIII. 35 U.S.C. §112 Issues — RF Module / Means-Plus-Function (Claims 14-20) — MAINTAINED, REQUIREMENT FOR CLARIFICATION/AMENDMENT\nBackground: The earlier Office action interpreted the \"RF module ... receiving\" and \"RF module ... feeds\" limitations under 35 U.S.C. §112(f) (pre-AIA §112, sixth paragraph) because they use a generic \"module\" placeholder with functional language without reciting sufficient structure. The Office identified RF module 230 from the specification as possible corresponding structure (see specification paragraph noting \"The RF module 230 is connected to the antenna ... and transmits or receives a radio signal;\").\nApplicant's response: The applicant contends that skilled artisans would understand necessary hardware and algorithmic implementations and that the specification and cited references supply the understanding.\nAnalysis and Action: If a claim limitation invokes §112(f), the claim is construed to cover the corresponding structure, material, or acts described in the specification and equivalents. The mere assertion that artisans would know how to implement the function does not satisfy the requirement — the applicant must either (A) amend the claims to recite the specific structural elements from the specification (for example, by expressly reciting \"RF module 230\" and/or \"processor 210\" and/or other structure disclosed in the specification), or (B) identify, in the next submission, the precise support in the specification (by paragraph and line or figure reference) that shows the corresponding structure for the functional RF module/processor limitations and explain why that disclosure provides sufficient structure to perform the claimed functions and to avoid invocation of §112(f). The Office previously pointed to paragraph [0095] and FIG. 5; if applicant intends that these are the corresponding structures, the applicant should expressly state that identification in the next filing and explain why the disclosure supports the scope of the claims under §112(f). Absent such amendment or explicit identification, the §112(b) indefiniteness and §112(f) concerns remain.\nIV. Allowable Subject Matter / Claim 8\nAs noted in the prior Office action, dependent claim 8 was objected to as dependent on a rejected base claim; claim 8 may be allowable if rewritten in independent form including all limitations of its base claim(s) and intervening claims.\nProcedural Action / Requirements\n1) The examiner maintains the rejections under 35 U.S.C. §§ 101, 103 and the 35 U.S.C. §112 issues for the claims as currently amended. The present action is non-final and the applicant is given an opportunity to respond.\n2) To address the §101 rejection, applicant may (a) amend claims to recite specific structural or technological improvements (not generic processors/program code) that are described in the specification and that demonstrate a specific technological improvement in the functioning of the device or network, or (b) provide persuasive argument and evidence that the claimed combination produces a technological improvement beyond mere data manipulation.\n3) To address the §103 rejections, applicant may amend claims to include limitations not taught or suggested by the cited references, or provide persuasive evidence/arguments that the combination of references does not teach or suggest the claimed limitations or lacks a motivation to combine and reasonable expectation of success.\n4) To resolve the §112 issues, the applicant must either: (A) amend the claims to recite the specific structure from the specification (for example, expressly recite ‘‘RF module 230’’ and/or ‘‘processor 210’’ and any other structural features disclosed and intended to be claimed), or (B) expressly identify in the specification the corresponding structure that performs the claimed function (cite paragraph(s)/line(s) and figure(s)) and explain why that disclosure supplies the required structure. If the applicant does not intend the claim to be treated under §112(f), the claims must be rewritten to recite sufficient structure to perform the claimed function without invoking §112(f).\n5) If applicant believes the Office has erred in applying the cited references, applicant should: (a) point to specific claim language and explain why the reference(s) do not disclose or suggest the limitation (with record citations), or (b) submit amendments that place the claims in a patentable posture over the cited art.\n6) The examiner is available for an interview (telephone or video) to discuss the outstanding issues; the applicant is encouraged to contact the examiner to arrange an interview.\nConclusion\nThe prior rejections as noted above are maintained. This is a non-final action. The applicant is invited to reply with amendments and/or arguments directed to the points identified above.\nExaminer Note: The record documents relied on in this action include the prior Office action (ITBY8A5XRXEAPX0), the originally-filed specification and claims (IGTEXY7CPXXIFW2; IGOZJZM7PXXIFW3; pgpub_description), and the applicant's December 22, 2016 amendments/remarks (IX8WCVSYPXXIFW1; IX8WCVURPXXIFW1).",
  "limitations": [
    "The record does not include the full text of Li et al. and Park et al. opinions or all cited passages; the examiner relied on the prior Office action's characterization of those references (ITBY8A5XRXEAPX0).",
    "Applicant submitted amended claim wording using \"program code\" and processor/RF-module phrasing. If applicant intends particular disclosed structure (e.g., RF module 230, processor 210) to be the corresponding structure for §112(f), the Office requires explicit identification by specification paragraph/figure or amended claim language; absence of such explicit identification leaves §112 issues unresolved."
  ]
}

