ical advantages shown in the specification) that would have made the cited combination non-obvious to a person of ordinary skill. Unsupported attorney argument is insufficient.\nIII. 35 U.S.C. §112 Issues — RF Module / Means-Plus-Function (Claims 14-20) — MAINTAINED, REQUIREMENT FOR CLARIFICATION/AMENDMENT\nBackground: The earlier Office action interpreted the \"RF module ... receiving\" and \"RF module ... feeds\" limitations under 35 U.S.C. §112(f) (pre-AIA §112, sixth paragraph) because they use a generic \"module\" placeholder with functional language without reciting sufficient structure. The Office identified RF module 230 from the specification as possible corresponding structure (see specification paragraph noting \"The RF module 230 is connected to the antenna ... and transmits or receives a radio signal;\").\nApplicant's response: The applicant contends that skilled artisans would understand necessary hardware and algorithmic implementations and that the specification and cited references supply the understanding.\nAnalysis and Action: If a claim limitation invokes §112(f), the claim is construed to cover the corresponding structure, material, or acts described in the specification and equivalents. The mere assertion that artisans would know how to implement the function does not satisfy the requirement — the applicant must either (A) amend the claims to recite the specific structural elements from the specification (for example, by expressly reciting \"RF module 230\" and/or \"processor 210\" and/or other structure disclosed in the specification), or (B) identify, in the next submission, the precise support in the specification (by paragraph and line or figure reference) that shows the corresponding structure for the functional RF module/processor limitations and explain why that disclosure provides sufficient structure to perform the claimed functions and to avoid invocation of §112(f). The Office previously pointed to paragraph [0095] and FIG. 5; if applicant intends that these are the corresponding structures, the applicant should expressly state that identification in the next filing and explain why the disclosure supports the scope of the claims under §112(f). Absent such amendment or explicit identification, the §112(b) indefiniteness and §112(f) concerns remain.\nIV. Allowable Subject Matter / Claim 8\nAs noted in the prior Office action, dependent claim 8 was objected to as dependent on a rejected base claim; claim 8 may be allowable if rewritten in independent form including all limitations of its base claim(s) and intervening claims.\nProcedural Action / Requirements\n1) The examiner maintains the rejections under 35 U.S.C. §§ 101, 103 and the 35 U.S.C. §112 issues for the claims as currently amended. The present action is non-final and the applicant is given an opportunity to respond.\n2) To address the §101 rejection, applicant may (a) amend claims to recite specific structural or technological improvements (not generic processors/program code) that are described in the specification and that demonstrate a specific technological improvement in the functioning of the device or network, or (b) provide persuasive argument and evidence that the claimed combination produces a technological improvement beyond mere data manipulation.\n3) To address the §103 rejections, applicant may amend claims to include limitations not taught or suggested by the cited references, or provide persuasive evidence/arguments that the combination of references does not teach or suggest the claimed limitations or lacks a motivation to combine and reasonable expectation of success.\n4) To resolve the §112 issues, the applicant must either: (A) amend the claims to recite the specific structure from the specification (for example, expressly recite ‘‘RF module 230’’ and/or ‘‘processor 210’’ and any other structural features disclosed and intended to be claimed), or (B) expressly identify in the specification the corresponding structure that performs the claimed function (cite paragraph(s)/line(s) and figure(s)) and explain why that disclosure supplies the required structure. If the applicant does not intend the claim to be treated under §112(f), the claims must be rewritten to recite sufficient structure to perform the claimed function without invoking §112(f).\n5) If applicant believes the Office has erred in applying the cited references, applicant should: (a) point to specific claim language and explain why the reference(s) do not disclose or suggest the limitation (with record citations), or (b) submit amendments that place the claims in a patentable posture over the cited art.\n6) The examiner is available for an interview (telephone or video) to discuss the outstanding issues; the applicant is encouraged to contact the examiner to arrange an interview.\nConclusion\nThe prior rejections as noted above are maintained. This is a non-final action. The applicant is invited to reply with amendments and/or arguments directed to the points identified above.\nExaminer Note: The record documents relied on in this action include the prior Office action (ITBY8A5XRXEAPX0), the originally-filed specification and claims (IGTEXY7CPXXIFW2; IGOZJZM7PXXIFW3; pgpub_description), and the applicant's December 22, 2016 amendments/remarks (IX8WCVSYPXXIFW1; IX8WCVURPXXIFW1).",
  "limitations": [
    "The record does not include the full text of Li et al. and Park et al. opinions or all cited passages; the examiner relied on the prior Office action's characterization of those references (ITBY8A5XRXEAPX0).",
    "Applicant submitted amended claim wording using \"program code\" and processor/RF-module phrasing. If applicant intends particular disclosed structure (e.g., RF module 230, processor 210) to be the corresponding structure for §112(f), the Office requires explicit identification by specification paragraph/figure or amended claim language; absence of such explicit identification leaves §112 issues unresolved."
  ]
}

