      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The combination of Inoue with Kajino and Kato, as relied upon in the earlier Office action, continues to provide a prima facie basis to reject the claimed combination of (a) a laser module and fluorescent members producing red/green/blue beams, (b) separate collimators for the R/G/B beams, (c) a prism receiving the collimated R/G/B beams, (d) a scanning optical system that projects beams from the prism onto an image surface, and (e) use of a single-wavelength UV excitation source. The amendments to claim 1 add explicit dichroic mirror elements (first, second, third dichroic mirrors) and recite that the beams reflected by those dichroic mirrors are combined to form the image. Fujisawa (as noted in the prior Office action) discloses dichroic mirrors that separate/route R/G/B components and relay mirrors to guide blue light to a prism; Kajino and Inoue teach collimation and scanning optical projection receiving beams from a prism; Kato teaches a single-wavelength (UV) excitation of phosphor/fluorescent elements. Combining these teachings gives rise to the claimed arrangement as an obvious design choice to one of ordinary skill in the art.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "HNQ6UMIGPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Claims 1, 3, and 7 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Inoue in view of Kajino and Kato.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "HNQ6UMIGPXXIFW4",
          "support_span": "Fujisawa discloses, in Fig. 1, a projector ... a first dichroic mirror (dichroic mirror 31) ... to pass the red beam therethrough and reflect the green beam and the blue beam ... a second dichroic mirror (dichroic mirror 32) ... to pass the blue beam therethrough and reflect the green beam ... reflecting mirrors 37, 39 reflect the blue component of the beam.",
