  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner's Non-Final Action\nAction on Applicant's Amendment and Arguments (filed 02/10/2014)\nSummary\n- The applicant's amendments to the specification title and the claims have been considered. The informalities and the 112(b) indefiniteness issues noted in the prior Office action (11/12/2013) are addressed by entry of the amendments for the pending claims as discussed below. - The prior 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections over the applied prior art remain. Continued rejection(s) for obviousness are maintained for the reasons set forth.\nEntry of Amendment\n- The title amendment (replacement title: LASER PROJECTION SYSTEM HAVING SCAN SYSTEM AND FLUORESCENT MEMBERS) is entered. - The applicant's claim amendments presented on 02/10/2014 are entered as follows: claims 1-3 and 7 are now the pending claims; claims 4-6 are canceled. The claim set entry is recorded and will be the basis for further examination.\n35 U.S.C. 112(b) (indefiniteness) — Status\n- The earlier Office action (11/12/2013) rejected claims 1 and 3-6 under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) for indefiniteness, primarily due to ambiguous use of the term 'lens' and inconsistent claim/specification terminology. Applicant amended claims 1 and 3 to replace ambiguous language (e.g., 'scan system lens' / 'first lens' / 'second lens') with clearer terminology (e.g., 'scanning optical system', 'first dichroic mirror', 'second dichroic mirror', 'third dichroic mirror') and canceled claims 4-6. The examiner finds these amendments responsive and withdraws the prior 112(b) rejection as to the currently-pending claims (1 and 3). If any remaining claim term later is found ambiguous, a specific explanation will be provided.\n35 U.S.C. 103 — Obviousness (maintained)\nGrounds maintained: Claims 1, 3, and 7 (as amended) — rejected over Inoue (US 8,038,299 B2) in view of Kajino (US 2009/0067033 A1) and Kato (JP 2004-341105 A). Claim 2 — rejected over Inoue in view of Kajino and Kato, and further in view of Kurosaki (US 2010/0328632 A1). Claims 4-6 were canceled and thus are no longer before the Office.\nReasons for maintaining rejection:\n1) The prior Office action explained how Inoue teaches a laser projection concept using fluorescent regions excited by a laser source and contemplates use of a scanning optical module. The action also explained that Kajino discloses collimating lenses for red, green, and blue illumination, a dichroic prism receiving collimated beams, and a scanning mirror section that scans the received beams onto a screen. Kato teaches excitation using a single-wavelength ultraviolet source for phosphor excitation. The combination of these teachings addresses the major functional limitations recited in the amended claim 1: separate fluorescent members producing R/G/B beams, collimation of those beams, a prism/dichroic combining arrangement, and a scanning optical system projecting the combined beams to form an image. (See prior OA citations recorded in the file.)\n2) The applicant argues Fujisawa's mirrors differ because Fujisawa's arrangement begins with white light from an arc lamp. While the application notes differences in source type, the examiner relies on the fact that Fujisawa nonetheless discloses dichroic/splitting and relay mirrors that perform the claimed pass/reflect behavior for R/G/B components; Kajino discloses collimation and scanning projection; and Kato supplies the teaching of single-wavelength UV excitation. Combining the teachings reflects routine design substitution and combination to a person of ordinary skill for the reasons previously given (predictable results; routine engineering choices). The examiner finds that applicant's arguments do not persuasively establish that the claimed combination as a whole would have been nonobvious in view of the cited art.\n3) For claim 2, Kurosaki explicitly teaches placement of collimator lenses between a laser module and a fluorescent wheel to collimate excitation light; inclusion of such a collimator is a predictable design choice and therefore claim 2 remains rejected for obviousness.\nOutcome\n- The 35 U.S.C. 112(b) rejection is withdrawn for the pending claims as noted above. - The 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections are maintained for claims 1-3 and 7 (as appropriate) for the reasons set forth. This action is non-final. Applicant is allowed to reply by amendment and/or by providing persuasive argument or evidence (for example, affidavits, declarations, or prior art evidence) that would overcome the obviousness findings. If applicant elects to amend, any amendment must comply with applicable rules and avoid introduction of new matter.\nNext steps for Applicant (suggested)\n- If applicant wishes to prosecute further, submit claim amendments that more clearly distinguish over the cited art with support in the specification, or present argument/evidence showing nonobviousness (for example, unexpected results, teaching away in the cited art, or a showing that the combination would not operate as claimed). - If applicant contends that any cited reference does not teach a particular limitation, identify the precise limitation and provide supporting citations to the intrinsic record or additional evidence where possible.\nConclusion\nThe application remains under consideration. The applicant's responsiveness to the prior 112(b) objection is noted and the amendments are entered. The previously-stated obviousness conclusions are maintained. Applicant is invited to respond to this Non-Final Action to advance prosecution.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The examiner's reliance and reasoning for the obviousness rejections are taken from the prior Office action (HNQ6UMIGPXXIFW4). The actual full text of the cited prior art references (Inoue, Kajino, Kato, Fujisawa, Kurosaki) are not reproduced in the record provided here; the examiner's characterization in the earlier OA is used as the evidentiary support.",
    "It is not established in the supplied record whether the applicant's amended claims have been formally entered into the application file beyond the applicant's submission; the examiner's entry statement above presumes the amendments are in proper form and enters them for examination. If formal defects exist in the amendment papers (e.g., signature, fees, form), they are not evident in the provided record and would be raised separately if discovered."
  ]
}

