    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Office Action — Non-Final (Response to Applicant’s A1 dated 2016-07-20 and 2016-08-26)\nAction summary\n- Applicant’s amendments to the specification and the addition of claims 2–9 are acknowledged and entered where compliant. Claims 1–9 are currently of record and pending. (See Applicant submissions dated 07/20/2016 and 08/26/2016.)\n- The following rejections are maintained at this time for claim 1: 35 U.S.C. § 101 (subject-matter eligibility), nonstatutory double patenting (provisional) over U.S. Pat. No. 9,201,956, and pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. § 103(a) (Chung in view of Tran). The double patenting ground remains outstanding because no terminal disclaimer or evidence of common ownership is of record.\nDiscussion of amendments and entry\n- Specification amendment: Applicant amended the RELATED APPLICATIONS paragraph to add U.S. Pat. No. 9,201,956. The amendment is entered and will be incorporated into the file record. (See Applicant submission, IQVENMBMRXEAPX0 and ISCBWSX8RXEAPX4.)\n- Claims: Applicant added claims 2–9 concomitant with the response. The newly added claims are of record (ISCBWSX9RXEAPX4). The Examiner will consider these claims during continued examination; however, the primary rejections addressed below concern claim 1 as independent and representative.\n35 U.S.C. § 101 — subject matter eligibility (claim 1)\nApplicant requested clarification because the prior Office Action contains language that appears to both find and deny eligibility. The record does contain inconsistent language. The Examiner reviewed the entirety of the prior action and the present record and now provides the following clarification and reasoning.\n- Basis for continued 101 rejection: Claim 1 is directed to the abstract idea of classifying and filtering information (assembling a set of concepts/claims, designating concepts as irrelevant, and removing patents mapped only to those concepts). The Office previously characterized the claim in different places; however, the explicit analysis concluding that the claim \"describes a method for identifying patents ... mapping the patent and removing the patents from a patent pool with a relevancy determinations (all is abstract idea) and the claimed steps as a whole fails to add 'significantly more' to judicial exceptions\" remains the controlling statement of rationales in the record. Accordingly, the Examiner is maintaining the 35 U.S.C. § 101 rejection for claim 1.\n- What must Applicant show to overcome the rejection: Applicant may overcome the 101 rejection by amending the claims to recite additional features that create an inventive concept or transform the abstract idea into a practical application (i.e., elements that amount to significantly more than the judicial exception), or by providing persuasive argument and supporting evidence demonstrating that the claim elements, individually and as an ordered combination, do supply such significantly more. Mere recitation of generic computer components or conventional data storage without more will not suffice. The Applicant is invited to respond with claim amendments and/or evidence and argument.\nNonstatutory double patenting (provisional) over U.S. Pat. No. 9,201,956\n- The record contains a provisional double patenting rejection over claim 1 of U.S. Pat. No. 9,201,956 (see IN64MD6MPXXIFW4). Applicant asserts that a terminal disclaimer is enclosed. The Examiner finds no terminal disclaimer document or other evidence of common ownership in the prosecution file available to the Examiner at this time. If Applicant timely files a compliant terminal disclaimer (e.g., in accordance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.321) that satisfies the formal requirements and identifies the referenced patent, the provisional double patenting ground may be obviated. Absent such a filing, the double patenting ground remains outstanding and requires resolution before allowance.\n- Applicant should file the terminal disclaimer as a separate submission with proper signatures and any required statement of common ownership or otherwise provide documentary evidence of common ownership (or amend claims to be patentably distinct). If Applicant has already filed a terminal disclaimer, please indicate the submission date and receipt details; otherwise submit the terminal disclaimer if Applicant intends to traverse this ground.\nPre-AIA 35 U.S.C. § 103(a) — Chung (US 2003/0217335) in view of Tran (US 2005/0210008) (claim 1)\nApplicant traversed the rejection arguing that the cited passages of Chung address editing concepts in a concept pool but do not teach using concepts as negative-filters to remove patents mapped to concepts designated irrelevant; Applicant contends Tran is not asserted for that purpose and hence is deficient for the combination.\nExaminer response and rationale for maintaining the rejection:\n- Chung discloses automated concept extraction and generation/organization of concept hierarchies and further discloses interfaces for manipulating concepts within a hierarchy or pool (e.g., adding or deleting concepts). (See IN64MD6MPXXIFW4: \"concept hierarchy ... GUI for user to manipulating the concept in the hierarchy or pool such as adding, deleting ([0181] and [0193])\").\n- Tran discloses an IP/patent server and techniques for presenting patent search results and allowing user interaction with the result set, including selecting patents from results and modifying the result set by annotating, rating, or deleting patents from the result set (see IN64MD6MPXXIFW4: \"Tran teaches ... IP Patent Server ... return a list of patent ID number ... UI will allow the user to select a set of patents ... the user will modify the search result set by annotating patents, rating, or deleting patents from the result set ([0053], [0054], [0055])\").\n- A person of ordinary skill in the art would have been motivated to combine these teachings: applying Chung’s concept-extraction/concept-hierarchy mechanisms to patent documents and providing user-facing manipulation of concepts together with Tran’s taught user control over patent result sets reasonably suggests implementing negative filtering—i.e., designating concepts as irrelevant and removing patents mapped only to those concepts—from a patent pool. Tran’s disclosure that a user can delete patents from a result set furnishes the specific teaching of removing patents from a set based on user action, and Chung furnishes the teaching of concept mapping and concept pools tied to patent content. The combination supplies the missing link between designating concepts and removing patents mapped only to those concepts.\n- For these reasons the Examiner continues to find that the asserted combination renders claim 1 unpatentable under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. § 103(a).\nApplicant’s traversal may be pursued in reply by argument and/or amendments addressing the noted deficiencies. If Applicant believes the cited references do not in fact disclose the cited teachings, provide pointed citation to language in the prior-art documents and explain why the cited passages do not support the Examiner’s position.\nDisposition and next steps\n- This is a non-final action continuing the rejections under 35 U.S.C. § 101 and § 103(a) for claim 1 and maintaining the provisional double patenting ground until a compliant terminal disclaimer or evidence of common ownership is of record.\n- Applicant is given an opportunity to respond. To expedite resolution please consider the following in the reply: (1) submit claim amendments that (a) recite additional technical features or limitations demonstrating how the claimed combination effects a technological improvement or produces a technical solution beyond the abstract idea, and/or (b) make the claims patentably distinct from the cited art and the co-pending/issued patent in the double patenting ground; (2) submit a compliant terminal disclaimer (if Applicant desires to obviate the double patenting ground and the subject matter is commonly owned) or evidence/documentation of common ownership; (3) submit argument and citations to the specification and prior art to rebut the Examiner’s position on 101 and 103. If Applicant desires an interview to discuss claim amendment strategies or the Examiner’s rationale, contact the Examiner to schedule.\nEvidence cited in this action\n- Applicant’s specification amendments and remarks: IQVENMBMRXEAPX0, IQVENMBORXEAPX0, ISCBWSX8RXEAPX4, ISCBWSXARXEAPX4, ISCBWSX9RXEAPX4.\n- Prior Office Action noting grounds and references: IN64MD6MPXXIFW4.\nLimitations\n- The prosecution record available to the Examiner does not include a terminal disclaimer document or documentary proof of common ownership; the Examiner cannot treat a disclaimer as filed unless the terminal disclaimer is actually of record. If Applicant has already filed a terminal disclaimer, indicate the receipt date and any filing identifier; otherwise file a terminal disclaimer to resolve the double patenting ground.\n- The Examiner’s 103 rationale relies on the cited passages in the prior art as identified in the prior action (IN64MD6MPXXIFW4). If Applicant has additional authoritative evidence (for example, exemplar passages from the prior-art documents or technical declarations) demonstrating lack of the cited teachings, supply those materials for consideration.\nConcluding statement\nThe Applicant is encouraged to respond within the statutory period with claim amendments, a terminal disclaimer if applicable, and/or arguments supported by citation to the record and prior art. This is a non-final action; a reply may traverse the rejections and present amendments and evidence for consideration prior to any final action.",
  "limitations": [
    "No terminal disclaimer or evidence of common ownership is present in the prosecution record available to the Examiner; double patenting ground remains outstanding until such a document is of record.",
    "The Examiner's 101 and 103 rationales are based on the contents of the prior Office Action and the cited prior-art documents as summarized therein; if Applicant supplies contrary authoritative excerpts from the cited references, the Examiner will consider them."
  ]
}

