  "office_action": "Examiner Action — Non-Final (Summary and Reasons)\nOverview\nThis communication follows the March 19, 2014 Office action (HSYPHDHZPXXIFW4). The March 19 action set forth rejections under 35 U.S.C. 112 (first paragraph and second paragraph), anticipation under pre‑AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) over Eppstein (U.S. Pat. No. 6,692,456), and obviousness combinations (Eppstein with Jung and/or Aceti). Applicant responded by filing amended claim listings on September 19, 2014 and a corrected submission on September 26, 2014 (I0JYPE5NPXXIFW3). The September 2014 filings are recorded in the file as proposed amendments; the record shows the claims that were actually examined in the March 19 Office action were the claim set filed February 21, 2011 (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2). The March 19 Office action relied on that examined claim set. The examiner has reviewed the applicant's September 2014 submissions and the file history, and provides the following next action.\nProcedural status and how this action treats applicant filings\n- The rejections and objections made in the March 19, 2014 Office action were based on the claim set that was of record and examined at that time (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2). Those rejections remain in the record and are maintained as to that examined claim set. The examiner therefore MAINTAINS the grounds of rejection and the claim objections set out in the March 19, 2014 Office action for the claims that were examined in that action (claims arising from GKFY5I21PPOPPY2).\n- Applicant's September 19 and corrected September 26, 2014 submissions (I0JYPE5NPXXIFW3) are acknowledged as proposed amendments to the claims. The record indicates these proposed claims have not been entered into the application as the claims of record. The examiner cannot treat those proposed claims as the claims of record until they are entered per the rules. Accordingly, this communication does two things: (1) continues the outstanding rejections and objections as to the claim set that was examined (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2); and (2) explains the requirements for entry and the additional matters the examiner will consider after entry of the proposed amendments.\nSummary of outstanding grounds (as made in the March 19, 2014 Office action)\n1) Means-plus-function (35 U.S.C. 112(f)/pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 112 sixth paragraph): The March 19 action treated the presence of the generic term \"means\" (coupled with functional language) in several asserted claim limitations as invoking 35 U.S.C. 112(f). The Office identified claims in the examined set that invoked 112(f) and stated the corresponding structure was the structures shown in the specification (Figs. 1–5). The examiner MAINTAINS that characterization for the examined claim set. See HSYPHDHZPXXIFW4: \"Claim limitation 'means for' has/have been interpreted under 35 U.S.C. 112(f)...\" and the specific claim list cited therein. If applicant seeks to avoid application of 112(f) to any limitation, the applicant must amend the claim to use structural language rather than a generic \"means\" placeholder, or provide a clear showing that the claim as written recites sufficient structure for performing the claimed function. The examiner requested applicant identify, with page/line or figure references, the corresponding structure for each means‑function limitation if applicant disputes the examiner's interpretation.\n2) Enablement (35 U.S.C. 112(a)/pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 112 first paragraph): The March 19 action rejected claims 357–366 and 372 (the claims depending from the claim identified as 357 in the examined listing) under 112(a) as not enabled for their full scope. The examiner MAINTAINS this rejection for the examined claim set. If applicant files amended claims that narrow the scope, or otherwise enters the September 2014 amended claims, the examiner will reassess enablement with respect to the entered claims.\n3) Indefiniteness / clarity (35 U.S.C. 112(b)/pre-AIA second paragraph): The March 19 action objected to and rejected several claims (e.g., claims identified as 346, 349, 353, 354, 359, 363, 364 in the examined set) for using imprecise terms such as \"may\" or \"optionally\" and other formal problems. Those objections and rejections are MAINTAINED for the examined claim set. Applicant's September 2014 amendments removed many of the flagged terms in the proposed claims; however, these proposed claims must be entered before the examiner can withdraw the objections as to the claims of record.\n4) Anticipation / obviousness (pre‑AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) and 103(a)): The March 19 action set forth detailed 102 and 103 rejections principally over Eppstein (U.S. Pat. No. 6,692,456), with Jung and Aceti cited in combinations for specific features (biopsy/microneedles; sensing/imaging). Those prior‑art rejections are MAINTAINED against the examined claim set (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2). The examiner cited specific portions of Eppstein and the other references in the action; those citations remain the factual basis for the rejections of the examined claims.\nHow applicant can obtain reconsideration or withdrawal of the outstanding grounds\nBecause applicant submitted proposed amended claims (I0JYPE5NPXXIFW3), the examiner will consider the amended claim set for entry and substantive examination provided applicant complies with the entry requirements below. The examiner will not withdraw or cancel the outstanding March 19 rejections until the file either: (A) has the amended claims entered and the examiner has had an opportunity to consider the claims on the merits; or (B) applicant files arguments and/or evidence that persuade the examiner that one or more of the prior rejections were in error with respect to the claims that actually were examined. To move forward, applicant should take one of these courses.\nA. Request entry of the Sept. 2014 corrected amended claims and show basis/support for added limitations\nIf applicant wants the Sept. 19/Sept. 26, 2014 amended claims (I0JYPE5NPXXIFW3) to be treated as the claims of record, do the following:\n1) File a request for entry of the proposed amended claims (or a formal amendment under 37 C.F.R. 1.121). If there are any formal issues (multiple dependent claims, inconsistent dependencies, capitalization, punctuation errors, or other form problems) correct them in the formal amendment or provide clean replacement claim pages that comply with 37 C.F.R. 1.121. The record shows corrections were filed on Sept. 26, 2014; if further clean claim pages are necessary, submit them.\n2) For any newly added or newly‑worded limitation in the proposed claims that was not present in the claims examined in the March 19 action, identify the specific support in the specification for that limitation (page/paragraph and/or figure reference). The examiner has previously requested this mapping for means‑plus‑function limitations and for any functional or structural limitation that might be treated under 35 U.S.C. 112(f). Identifying the support will enable the examiner to determine whether the amendment is proper under 35 U.S.C. 112 and whether any previous 112 rejections should be withdrawn.\n3) If applicant believes any of the earlier rejections (112(f), 112(a), 112(b), 102, or 103) were in error with respect to the previously examined claim set, submit a focused argument explaining the error and cite specific passages in the references or in the specification that support applicant's position. General statements that the prior art is topical rather than implantable are noted in the record, but when the examined claim language (the one used by the examiner to make the rejection) includes phrasing such as \"means for administering\" or broadly recites topical delivery, you must demonstrate why the particular cited passages do not anticipate or render obvious the claim limitation at issue.\nIf the amendments are properly entered the examiner will consider withdrawal or modification of the outstanding rejections with respect to the entered claims. If the amendments cannot be entered (for example, lack of written description or new matter issues are found), the examiner will continue prosecution on the previously examined claim set and maintain the rejections applicable to those claims.\nB. Respond to the outstanding 35 U.S.C. 112 issues for the examined claim set\nIf applicant prefers to keep the claims as they were in the March 19 examined set (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2), then applicant should address the outstanding 112 issues (means‑function and enablement/indefiniteness) with arguments and citation to the specification/drawings that identify the structures that correspond to the recited functions (for means‑plus‑function) and to show how the specification enables the full scope of the claims that the Office determined were insufficiently enabled. The March 19 action already identified the examiner's view of the corresponding structures (Figs. 1–5) for the means limitations; however, if applicant has a different mapping between claim language and structure, present that mapping now with specific citations. Otherwise the examiner will maintain the 112(f) characterization and apply the statutory construction rules accordingly.\nSpecific points to address in any response\n- If you assert that a claim limitation does not invoke 35 U.S.C. 112(f), explain why the claim language recites sufficient structure (provide the claim language and point to the supporting specification text and drawings). The Office may still treat a limitation as a means‑plus‑function element if it uses a generic placeholder and lacks structure.\n- If you request entry of the amended claims, supply the support mapping (spec paragraph/figure) for each new limitation and for each limitation that was previously characterized as means‑plus‑function.\n- If you contest the examiner's characterization of a cited prior art passage, point to the exact language or figure in the cited reference and explain why it does not disclose the claimed element; do not restate broad distinctions without specific citations. For example, simply asserting \"topical vs. implantable\" is not sufficient where the examined claim wording (in the claim set that was rejected) was broad and permitted topical implementation; instead identify the exact claim language you contend is not read on by the reference and the precise portion of the reference relied upon by the examiner. If applicant has experimental or other evidence showing non‑equivalence, submit that evidence with an appropriate statement explaining relevance.\nConclusion / Next steps\n1) The examiner MAINTAINS the rejections and objections made in the March 19, 2014 Office action with respect to the claim set that was examined in that action (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2). Those rejections remain pending.\n2) The Sept. 19 and Sept. 26, 2014 amended claim listings (I0JYPE5NPXXIFW3) are of record as proposed amendments but have not been entered as claims of record. The examiner will consider entry upon applicant filing a formal request for entry and meeting the formal and substantive entry requirements specified above (37 C.F.R. 1.121; identification of specification support for new limitations; resolution of any form defects). Upon entry the examiner will act on the merits of the entered claims and will reassess the pending grounds in view of the entered claims.\n3) Applicant is invited to respond by either (A) requesting entry of the amended claims and simultaneously providing the required support mapping and any arguments; or (B) addressing the outstanding rejections directed to the examined claim set (as identified in the March 19, 2014 action). If applicant files neither a request for entry of the proposed amendments nor a substantive reply to the outstanding rejections within the statutory time period, prosecution will continue on the basis of the earlier examined claim set and the outstanding rejections will remain.\nLimitations\n- The examiner's rejections in this communication remain tied to the claim set that was actually examined in the March 19, 2014 Office action (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2). The September 2014 claim listings are presently recorded as proposed amendments; they were not entered prior to the March 19 Office action and thus were not the basis for the rejections in that action. If the applicant wants the September 2014 claims to be treated as claims of record, the applicant must properly request entry and show support for any new or modified limitations as explained above.\n- The examiner's citation to portions of Eppstein, Jung, and Aceti in the March 19, 2014 Office action is the examiner's characterization of those references as they bear on the examined claim set. A citation in the Office action is the examiner's position about what the reference discloses; it is not a verbatim substitution of the reference text into the application. If applicant wishes to rebut the examiner's factual characterization, point to the specific portions of those references (with quotations) and explain why they do not disclose the claimed limitations.\nExaminer contact and availability\nIf applicant wishes to discuss this action, contact Examiner CHU CHUAN (JJ) LIU at the Art Unit telephone number (provided in the March 19, 2014 action). The examiner is available for telephone interviews to discuss how to place the proposed September 2014 claims into the record and to resolve the outstanding formal and substantive issues.\nSigned by:\nCHU CHUAN (JJ) LIU\nExaminer, Art Unit 3777",
  "limitations": [
    "The March 19, 2014 Office action rejections were made against the claim set that was of record and examined at that time (GKFY5I21PPOPPY2); the applicant's Sept. 19 and corrected Sept. 26, 2014 claim listings are recorded as proposed amendments and have not been entered — this affects whether the examiner can treat them as claims of record.",
    "If applicant wants the Sept. 2014 amended claims entered, applicant must request entry and identify local support in the specification (page/paragraph or figure) for newly added or modified limitations; without entry the examiner maintains the prior rejections against the previously examined claim set.",
    "Some claim-support and formal‑practice uncertainties remain (e.g., mapping of \"means\" limitations to structure, written‑description support for newly added limitations) — the record does not establish entry of amendments or show the full mapping; applicant needs to provide the specific support."
  ]
}

