  "office_action": "Examiner Communication (Non-Final) — Claims 6–9 (Group II elected)\nSummary\nApplicant filed an amendment dated October 17, 2016 (A1). The Office previously issued an action on July 22, 2016 (O1) restricting the application to Group II (claims 6–9) and rejecting those claims under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) (indefiniteness), pre‑AIA 35 U.S.C. 102 (anticipation), and pre‑AIA 35 U.S.C. 103 (obviousness). Applicant has submitted arguments and proposed claim changes in the A1 paper. This communication: (1) confirms the outstanding rejections from the July 22, 2016 action; (2) explains what is necessary for the Office to consider withdrawing any rejection; and (3) requests specific actions from Applicant to complete examination of the newly-proposed amendments.\nStatus of the Record and Entry of Applicant Amendments\n- The examiner notes the amendment filed 2016-10-17 (A1). The application file (PAIR) must reflect entry of these amendments before the Examiner can withdraw previously-stated rejections based on earlier claim text. The record available to the Examiner as of the cutoff for this communication shows the claims that were examined in the July 22, 2016 Office action (claims 6–9 as then of record) and the outstanding rejections set forth in that action. The A1 amendment purports to cancel claims, amend claims 6, 8, 9 and to add claim 20; however, the A1 amendments are currently a reply by Applicant and are not yet shown in the file as entered for examination as of the cutoff date. If Applicant desires the Examiner to consider the October 17 amendment in place of the previously‑examined claim set, Applicant must ensure the amendment is properly entered into the record and indicate whether entry was made under 37 CFR 1.116 (after‑final practice) or otherwise. If the amendment has already been entered, notify the Examiner and provide the PAIR entry confirmation page or identify the transaction date so the Examiner can proceed with examination of the entered claims.\nRejection under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) (indefiniteness)\n- The prior Office action (7/22/2016) stated that claims 6–9 were rejected under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) for lack of particular and distinct claim language and listed specific terms that lacked antecedent basis (for example: \"the area\", \"the liquid crystal layer side\", \"the same potential\"). That rejection remains outstanding. Applicant has amended the specification and drawings and argued that the informalities have been corrected (A1). If the amendment and specification changes have been entered, the Examiner will re-evaluate whether the previously-identified antecedent and definiteness problems have been cured. If entry has not yet occurred, the rejection remains on the record and will be maintained in any next action until the amendments are entered and found to remediate the earlier deficiencies. Applicant is directed to: (a) confirm the amendments have been entered; or (b) if not, resubmit the amendment and corrected drawing sheets in accordance with 37 CFR 1.121 and 1.84 with a request to enter the amendment, and identify precisely which previously-called-out indefiniteness defects have been addressed.\nRejection under pre‑AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) (anticipation) and 103(a) (obviousness)\n- The prior action found claims 6, 8, and 9 anticipated by Choi et al. (US 2011/0242468) and claim 7 obvious over Choi in view of Andrews (US 2009/0174855). Applicant's A1 traversal argues that the amended claim 6 requires (inter alia) that (i) the groove be in a light‑shielding region and (ii) the liquid crystal layer at the groove position has thickness substantially equal to or larger than the liquid crystal layer in an active area which is not shielded from light — and that Choi's disclosed contact hole/groove has a thickness smaller than the active area, so Choi does not anticipate. Applicant asserts that Andrews does not cure Choi's deficiency for the combination claim 7 objection. These are substantive arguments; the Examiner will address them once the Applicant's amended claim text is entered for examination. At this time:\n  - The record (O1) shows the Office applied Choi to the then‑of‑record claims and considered Choi's contact hole/groove to correspond to the claimed groove/contact hole and Choi's polymer/PS teaching to correspond to the claimed polymer layer. The Examiner relied on Choi's disclosures (as summarized in O1) to reject claims 6, 8 and 9 under 102. The Examiner will re-open consideration of those rejections and will either withdraw or maintain them after entry of the A1 amendment and re‑analysis of Choi against the entered claim language.\n  - For claim 7, the Examiner's prior reasoning that Andrews teaches small photospacer diameters and that it would have been obvious to apply such sizes to the arrangement in Choi remains the articulated basis for the 103 rejection (see O1). Applicant's assertion that there would be no motivation or reason to modify Choi must be supported by specific record evidence (for example, a teaching‑away passage in Choi or Andrews, experimental data, or claim limitations added by amendment that distinguish over the cited combination). Broad statements that the combination is improper are insufficient.\nRequired Applicant Actions and Next Steps\n1) Entry confirmation: If Applicant's October 17, 2016 amendment (A1) is intended to be entered and considered now, identify the exact transaction that caused entry in the Office record (for example, confirm the replacement drawing sheet was accepted and the claims were entered). If entry has not yet occurred, Applicant should file a corrected replacement drawing sheet(s) and/or claim/specification amendments explicitly complying with 37 CFR 1.121 and 1.84 as necessary, and request entry. If Applicant requests entry, include redlined (if possible) or a clear indication of the changes and the complete set of amended claims intended to be of record. The Examiner will not withdraw the outstanding rejections until the amendment is of record and examined.\n2) For the 112(b) rejection: After entry, the Examiner will decide whether the prior 112 objections are resolved. If Applicant believes the 112 objections were fully resolved by the October 17, 2016 amendments, provide a short statement pointing to the exact places in the amended specification or drawings that supply antecedent basis for the previously‑called-out phrases (cite paragraph numbers and figure numbers/labels from the entered material). If the Examiner still finds indefiniteness, a new 112 rejection or an explanation of remaining defects will be issued.\n3) For the 102 and 103 rejections: After entry, the Examiner will re-evaluate Choi and Andrews against the entered claims. If Applicant relies on the position that Choi does not disclose the relative-thickness limitation, Applicant must identify where in Choi (by figure and paragraph) Choi allegedly discloses a contrary relationship (i.e., show that Choi's groove/contact-hole region has LC thickness smaller than active area) or otherwise provide evidence to support non-anticipation or non-obviousness. If Applicant has experimental evidence or other objective evidence to rebut the Examiner's prima facie case of anticipation or obviousness, submit it now.\n4) If Applicant maintains traversal of the restriction requirement (Group II election was made on record earlier), the Applicant may file a divisional application to pursue the non-elected Group I claims (Applicant stated a reservation to file a divisional). That is procedural and does not affect the present prosecution of Group II claims.\nConclusion / Action Requested\n- The Examiner maintains the outstanding rejections from the July 22, 2016 Office action (112(b) for claims 6–9 and 102/103 rejections as set forth). Applicant's October 17, 2016 amendment and arguments have been noted. To proceed, Applicant must ensure the 10/17/2016 amendment is entered; and, to the extent Applicant requests withdrawal of any rejection, Applicant should point to the exact passages in the now‑entered specification/drawings that cure the earlier-cited defects or provide specific record evidence that distinguishes Choi and Andrews from the entered claims. After entry, the Examiner will re-evaluate and issue a subsequent Office action withdrawing or maintaining the cited rejections with written explanation.\n- If Applicant intends to have the October 17, 2016 amendment enter the record, please promptly confirm entry or re-submit the corrected replacement drawing sheet(s) and claims per the requirements listed above. If Applicant instead files further amendment or arguments, please ensure they address the specific issues identified in this communication.\nLimitations\n- The Examiner relied on the application file contents as of the USPTO cutoff date. The A1 (10/17/2016) paper is of record but the Amendment must be entered for substantive examination; this action does not presume the Amendment was entered. If the A1 amendment was entered after the cutoff, notify the Examiner and provide PAIR confirmation so the Examiner may re-evaluate prior rejections against the entered claim set.\n- The Examiner's earlier characterization of Choi and Andrews appears in the 7/22/2016 action. A citation to the full text of those references was used in the prior action; Applicant is encouraged to cite specific portions of the cited references if arguing non-anticipation or lack of motivation to combine.\nAny inquiry concerning this communication or earlier communications from the examiner should be directed to the primary examiner named in the file.\n",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant's 2016-10-17 amendments are recorded in the file wrapper but their formal entry for examination is not confirmed as of the cutoff; the Examiner cannot withdraw prior rejections until amendments are entered and examined.",
    "The Examiner's prior rejections relied on the examiner's summarization of Choi and Andrews in the 7/22/2016 action (IQUUZV68RXEAPX5); full-text passages of those foreign/third-party references are not included among the provided input documents, so the Examiner's characterization in O1 is used as record evidence.",
    "If Applicant submits additional evidence (for example, specific citations to Choi or Andrews or experimental data), that evidence must be in the file for the Examiner to consider it in evaluating novelty or obviousness."
  ]
}

