  "office_action": "ACTION ON APPLICANT'S APRIL 5, 2016 SUBMISSION\nStatus summary\n- Claims 1 and 2: maintained as allowable for reasons stated in the January 5, 2016 Office action. Applicant's amendment to claims 1 and 2 is recognized; no further substantive objection is made for patentability over the record prior art relied on to date. Proceed with allowance formalities after applicant addresses processing and formality matters as noted below. \n- Claim 3: the 35 U.S.C. § 103 rejection over Edmond (US 5,539,217) is maintained. Applicant's traversal has been considered but does not, without additional evidence, overcome the prima facie case of obviousness.\nDiscussion and reasons for action\n1) Claims 1–2 (method claims) The Examiner's prior statement that claims 1 and 2 are allowable stands. The record in the Office action (Jan. 5, 2016) explains that the particular temperature-gradient relationship (formula (1)) recited in claim 1 is not taught or suggested by the prior art of record. Applicant has submitted amended claim text for claims 1–2 (A1 amendment). No persuasive evidence or argument in the A1 contradicts the examiner's prior determination of allowability for these claims. Therefore claims 1 and 2 remain allowable. Applicant is advised to submit a clean corrected copy of the allowed claim set and to complete any outstanding formalities (e.g., any necessary drawing corrections, inventor or attorney signatures, or payment of the issue fee and publication/printing fees) to proceed toward allowance. If applicant intends to further amend any allowed claims, note that such amendments could reopen examination.\nSupporting record excerpts:\n- \"Claims 1-2 are allowed prior art of record.\" (Jan. 5, 2016 Office Action)\n- As filed claim language defining the temperature-gradient formula (formula (1)) and ranges appears at ID99A9HOPXXIFW3 (claims).\n2) Claim 3 (product claim) — 35 U.S.C. § 103 (maintained)\nGround and examiner analysis: Claim 3 recites \"A p-type SiC single crystal containing no inclusions and having resistivity of 35 to 120 mΩ·cm.\" The prior Office action (Jan. 5, 2016) rejected claim 3 under 35 U.S.C. § 103 over Edmond (US 5,539,217). The Examiner relied on Edmond's disclosure of a p-type SiC substrate and discussed that Edmond's resistivity figures (as cited in the action) overlap the claimed resistivity range. The Examiner also cited the well-established legal principle that selecting a workable range within a broader prior-art disclosure is prima facie obvious absent a showing of criticality or unexpected properties; the specification in this application does not provide the evidence of criticality required to rebut that presumption.\nApplicant's arguments in A1 and Examiner response:\n- Applicant argument: Applicant contends Edmond's statement that substrate 11 \"contains no n-type dopant\" is not equivalent to the present claim language \"containing no inclusions,\" and argues that the claimed crystal free of inclusions and having the recited low resistivity was first obtained by the present invention. Applicant asserts that a person of ordinary skill would have assumed a p-type SiC single crystal could not both be free of inclusions and have resistivity 35–120 mΩ·cm based on Edmond and other art.\n- Examiner response: The Examiner acknowledges the semantic distinction applicant makes between \"n-type dopant\" and \"inclusions.\" However, that semantic distinction does not, without corroborating evidentiary support, rebut the legal principle applied in the prior Office action: where the prior art discloses conditions that overlap a claimed range, selection of a sub-range is prima facie obvious unless applicant shows that the claimed range is critical, yields unexpected properties, or that the cited prior art would not have suggested the claimed combination. The Examiner points to the O1 text: \"With reference to Fig. 1, Edmond teaches a p-type SiC single crystal 11 containing no inclusions (interpreted as containing no n-type dopant) and having resistivity of 100 to 200 mΩ.cm (col. 5, lines 35-36).\" The Examiner also cited the legal authorities regarding range selection and the need for a showing of criticality or unexpected results (In re Aller, In re Kulling, etc.). Applicant's remarks do not include experimental data, declarations, or documentary evidence that establish the claimed resistivity range is critical or that the absence of inclusions in the claimed crystals is an unexpected property that would not have been arrived at by routine experimentation.\nWhat is required to overcome the rejection of claim 3:\n- If applicant wishes to overcome the rejection of claim 3, applicant should file one or more of the following: (a) evidence (e.g., declarations by inventors or qualified practitioners, experimental data, comparative tables) demonstrating that: (i) the claimed resistivity range (35–120 mΩ·cm) produces unexpected results compared to the closest prior art (for example, improvements in inclusion absence, crystal quality, device performance attributable to the claimed range), or (ii) the claimed combination of \"no inclusions\" and the recited resistivity is not disclosed or suggested by Edmond and cannot be obtained by routine optimization; or (b) proposed claim amendments that carve out a patentable distinction from Edmond supported by the specification. Declarations or data should be specific and show why the claimed range is critical and not the result of routine optimization. Broad conclusory statements are not sufficient. If applicant can point to particular passages in Edmond that do or do not teach the alleged features, supply specific citations and, if available, copies of relevant Edmond passages (if not already of record).\nProcedural disposition and next steps\n- The 35 U.S.C. § 103 rejection of claim 3 is maintained. The application remains with at least one outstanding rejection (claim 3). Therefore a final allowance/patent grant cannot be completed until the outstanding rejection is resolved. This communication is a non-final action continuing prosecution (CTFR). Applicant may respond to this action by either: (1) submitting persuasive evidence or amendments that overcome the rejection of claim 3, or (2) filing a request for continued examination (RCE) if appropriate, or (3) electing to cancel claim 3 and proceed to allowance of the remaining claims (1–2) by completing allowance formalities.\n- If applicant cancels claim 3 in a reply and completes the issue/allowance formalities for claims 1–2, the Examiner will process the application toward allowance for the remaining claims in accordance with the prior allowance statement.\nLimitations / uncertainties\n- The Examiner relies on the record of Edmond as cited in the January 5, 2016 Office action; the Examiner has not independently reproduced Edmond's experimental data. If applicant contends Edmond does not disclose any of the relied-upon features, applicant should point to the specific Edmond passages or submit a copy of Edmond with highlighted text to show the alleged difference.\n- Applicant's statement that the invention was the first to obtain a low-resistance p-type SiC single crystal containing no inclusions is a factual assertion that requires supporting evidence (declarations, experimental data). The specification as filed does not supply data showing that the claimed resistivity range is critical or yields unexpected results sufficient to overcome the prima facie case of obviousness applied to claim 3.\nConcluding remark\nApplicant is invited to respond within the statutory time period for reply to this non-final action. If applicant submits evidence or amendments, the Examiner will consider them and either withdraw the rejection if appropriate or maintain/confirm the rejection with further explanation. If applicant elects to cancel claim 3 and proceed to allowance of claims 1–2, the Examiner will continue processing allowance formalities upon receipt of all required documents and fees.\nExaminer: TRUNG Q. DANG (Art Unit 2819)\nSupporting record citations (verbatim excerpts):\n- \"Claims 1-2 are allowed prior art of record.\" (IIU8VJYWPXXIFW4, Jan. 5, 2016 Office Action)\n- \"Claim 3 is rejected under 35 U.S.C. 103 as being unpatentable over Edmond et al. (US 5,539,217).\" (IIU8VJYWPXXIFW4, Jan. 5, 2016 Office Action)\n- \"With reference to Fig. 1, Edmond teaches a p-type SiC single crystal 11 containing no inclusions (interpreted as containing no n-type dopant) and having resistivity of 100 to 200 mΩ.cm (col. 5, lines 35-36).\" (IIU8VJYWPXXIFW4, Jan. 5, 2016 Office Action)\n- \"Where the general conditions of a claim are disclosed in the prior art, it is not inventive to discover the optimum or workable range by routine experimentation.\" (IIU8VJYWPXXIFW4, Jan. 5, 2016 Office Action)\n",
  "limitations": [
    "The maintained 103 rejection for claim 3 rests on the Examiner's interpretation of Edmond as cited in the Jan. 5, 2016 Office action; if applicant provides primary source evidence (passages from Edmond or admissible declarations/data) showing Edmond does not teach the cited resistivity range or that the term 'inclusions' is treated materially differently, the Examiner will reconsider.",
    "Applicant's A1 remarks do not include experimental data or declarations showing criticality or unexpected results; absence of such evidence prevents withdrawal of the 103 rejection of claim 3 at this time."
  ]
}

