  "office_action": "Action on Claims and Grounds\nSummary\nThis action responds to the applicant's A1 submission filed February 11, 2014 (documents HRJYYOX2PXXIFW3 et seq.) and considers the prior Office communication of record (CTNF dated October 11, 2013, HMKNOW2XPXXIFW4). Applicant canceled claims 2–4 and amended claim 1 and several dependents; replacement drawings and specification amendments were filed. The Office withdraws the earlier drawing objection regarding depiction of the driving device because the specification and figures of record show element 2 and Fig. 3 as a block diagram of the driving device and its control. The remaining rejections are maintained for the reasons below. This is a non-final Office action (CTNF).\nClaims Pending\nEntered claim set (as examined) included claims 1–9 (GYDG1G10…). Applicant filed a proposed amended claim listing (HRJYYOY0PXXIFW3) with cancellation of claims 2–4 and proposed amended and new claims 1, 5–11. The entry status of the proposed amended claims is not established by the record; the examiner has reviewed the application as originally entered and the applicant’s A1 submission. The Office treats the applicant’s cancellations as effective to remove claims 2–4 from further consideration. The amendments to claim 1 and other claims in A1 will be considered for entry if the applicant files the formal claim set in accordance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 and the amendment is in condition for entry. At present the outstanding rejections apply to the claim scope of record, and to the pending claim as entered if the amendment is entered.\nDrawings\nApplicant argued that the drawing objection has been resolved by pointing to Fig. 3. Examiner note: the earlier drawing objection is withdrawn because the specification and figures of record (Fig. 3) show the driving device and motor control circuitry. Evidence: specification and Fig. 3 discussion (pgpub_description). If applicant elects to add new claim limitations that require particular drawing detail not present, corrected replacement sheets should be submitted as needed.\n35 U.S.C. 112 (Clarity / Antecedent Basis)\nPreliminary: Applicant cancelled claims 2–4. The Office’s prior 35 U.S.C. 112(b) objections to those dependent claim numbers are withdrawn as moot. If the applicant reintroduces the substance of those dependents in other claim form, antecedent and clarity will be re-evaluated.\nClaim 1: Applicant incorporated previously-dependent material into claim 1 in the A1 filing. If applicant’s amended claim 1 is formally entered, the Office will examine claim 1 for compliance with 35 U.S.C. 112. Where applicant’s amendment uses terms such as “the detection unit” or “the jacket member,” sufficient antecedent support and precise definition of these elements must be present in the claim and in the specification. If claim 1 is entered and the Office finds lack of antecedent or indefiniteness, an explicit 112 objection will be issued identifying the precise claim wording at issue and the required clarification. At this time claims 2–4 are canceled and no 112 objection remains against those canceled claim numbers.\n35 U.S.C. 102 / Anticipation\nClaims 1, 5, 6, 8 and 9 (claims that depend or incorporate the features of claim 1) remain rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) as anticipated by Yamaguchi (WO 2008/099611). The record shows that Yamaguchi teaches an operating device (remote control) with multiple detection elements (push buttons, baffles and optical sensors) and shows that different sensor outputs correspond to different indicated travel directions (examples include forward, backward, and diagonal directions as described in FIGS. 22 and 23 and paragraphs [0329]–[0335]). The examiner’s position is that the cited passages disclose multiple detection units arranged and operated to indicate or select movement directions of the controlled object, thereby meeting the claim limitations.\nApplicant’s argument that Yamaguchi does not disclose “a plurality of individual detection units corresponding to movement directions of the object” has been considered. The specification passages in Yamaguchi cited by Applicant ([0329]–[0335]) describe push buttons and sensors which change the indicated movement when the casing orientation changes and when different push buttons or combinations are activated, including diagonals. The Office interprets that disclosure as disclosing multiple detection elements associated with different directions. Because anticipation requires that a single prior-art reference disclose each and every claimed element, and because the Office finds such disclosure in Yamaguchi, the anticipation rejection is maintained.\nApplicant may respond by either (1) amending the claims to recite structure or limitations not found in the cited disclosure (for example, a particular physical arrangement, specific sensor type or particular claimed association), or (2) submitting persuasive evidence demonstrating that the cited passages do not teach the claimed limitation. If amendment is chosen, please submit a formal claim set under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121. If evidence is submitted, it should be documentary and explain why the cited passages are not reasonably read to disclose the limitation.\n35 U.S.C. 103 / Obviousness\nClaim 7 (illuminating device forming a light spot as a notification unit) remains rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) over Yamaguchi. The Office’s position is that Yamaguchi’s teaching of visual/optical signaling and direction indication renders it within the routine skill of the art to employ an illuminating spot to indicate travel direction to observers. Applicant has not submitted persuasive technical evidence of unexpected results or a showing that the prior art teaches away from an illuminating notification unit. The rejection is therefore maintained. To overcome the 103 rejection, applicant may amend the claims to include a particular configuration or function of the illuminating device that is not shown or suggested in Yamaguchi, or present experimental or documentary evidence that establishes non-obviousness.\nProcedural and Entry Matters\nApplicant submitted an amended claim listing (HRJYYOY0PXXIFW3) and amendments to the specification and drawings. The record does not establish formal entry of the amended claims. Applicant is advised to file a replacement formal claim set (with markings showing amendments) in compliance with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 for prompt entry. If applicant wishes the examiner to consider the present amended claim text as the operative claim set, please submit a clear statement that the claims are being presented for entry along with a formal replacement claim set. If the amended claims are entered, the examiner will re-evaluate the rejections against the entered text and issue a further action as appropriate.\nConclusion and Requirements\n1) The Office withdraws the earlier drawing objection concerning depiction of the driving device in view of Fig. 3 and the specification text. Applicant’s replacement drawing sheets are of record but formal corrected sheets should be submitted under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 if not already in compliance.\n2) Claims 2–4: the prior 35 U.S.C. 112 objections to those claim numbers are withdrawn because applicant canceled those claims. If applicant reintroduces these limitations in other claims, the Office will re-examine antecedent and clarity.\n3) Claims 1, 5, 6, 8 and 9: the rejection under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 102(b) over Yamaguchi is maintained. The examiner finds disclosure in the cited Yamaguchi passages of multiple detection elements and association of detection outputs with movement directions.\n4) Claim 7: the rejection under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) over Yamaguchi is maintained.\n5) Applicant may overcome the rejections by one or more of the following (preferably in a single reply): (a) submitting a formal replacement claim set for entry under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121 with any further amendments that distinguish over Yamaguchi, (b) amending claims to recite structural features not shown or suggested in Yamaguchi (supported by the specification), and/or (c) submitting persuasive documentary evidence (for example experimental data, declarations, or other printed publications) that the cited prior art does not disclose or render obvious the claimed features.\n6) If applicant wishes to teleconference to discuss possible claim amendments and the cited references, contact the examiner to arrange a time (telephone number listed in the prior Office action). If applicant believes additional time is required to prepare a substantive reply, applicant may request an extension under 37 C.F.R. § 1.136. Any petition for extension should explain the reasons and include required fees.\nLimitations\n- Applicant’s A1 submission contains OCR and formatting errors in the proposed claim text; those errors create uncertainty whether the submitted amended claims were intended exactly as printed. The Office requires a clean, formal claim set for entry under 37 C.F.R. § 1.121. If applicant expects the examiner to consider the amended claim text, submit corrected formal claims.\n- The examiner relies on the cited passages of Yamaguchi as supplied in the October 11, 2013 Office action (HMKNOW2XPXXIFW4). The examiner’s factual findings about disclosure are based on those passages in the record. If applicant submits additional prior art or documentary evidence beyond the cutoff, the Office will consider it as permitted under applicable rules.\nExaminer: Michael E. Gallion, Art Unit 3654",
  "limitations": [
    "Uncertainty whether applicant's messy OCR claim text in HRJYYOY0PXXIFW3 was intended as formally entered claims; applicant must file a clean replacement claim set under 37 C.F.R. §1.121 for entry.",
    "Responses rely on Yamaguchi passages as printed in the October 11, 2013 Office action (HMKNOW2XPXXIFW4); if applicant submits further evidence, prosecution may be affected.",
    "Canceled claims 2–4 withdraw earlier 112 objections as moot; if reintroduced, clarity/antecedent review will be required."
  ]
}

