      "rationale": "The Office action initially rejected claims 1-20 under 35 U.S.C. 112(b) for vagueness/indefiniteness (e.g., the term “representative,” various antecedent-basis problems, and unclear scope of cost function / MPC terms). Applicant amended claim text and argued the amendments overcome the objections. After review, some informalities are resolved by the amendments (antecedent for 'their' → 'longitudinal axes of the blades' in some claims), but significant 112(b) issues remain: (a) the term “representative future wind speed values” remains indefinite because the claims still do not define what “representative” means (i.e., representative of what) and an antecedent basis for “the electrical energy generated / converted aerodynamic energy” is still not established in several dependent claim recitations; (b) some dependent claim recitations still contain unclear singular/plural references (e.g., “the wind speed value” vs. plural values) and recitations such as “assuming the wind speed value to be constant over a rotor swept area” remain ambiguous as to whether a step is being added or a limitation of an earlier step; and (c) the scope of “Model Predictive Control strategy or a non-linear Model Predictive Control strategy” remains functional and must be tied to the specification for definite scope or be limited to “utilizes a linear or non-linear predictive model” for examination purposes. Claims remain indefinite for these reasons.",
