  "office_action": "Examiner Communication — Non-Final Action (continued prosecution)\nSummary of Action\nThis communication follows the Office action mailed June 13, 2016 (the first action, IPPLGGCBRXEAPX1) and Applicant’s reply and amendment filed September 22, 2016 (ITEPH30NRXEAPX4; ITEPH30MRXEAPX4; ITEPH30ORXEAPX4). Applicant has (a) amended the specification and title, (b) submitted replacement Figures 4 and 5, and (c) amended and cancelled various claims per the September 22 filing. The record shows the Applicant cancelled claims 8, 9, 11 and 13; the Examiner treats those claims as withdrawn. Applicant argues that the amendments overcome the prior objections and rejections. After careful consideration of the amendments and arguments, the Examiner reaches the following determinations.\nDrawings and Title\n- Title: Applicant amended the title in the specification and submitted a Supplemental ADS per the A1 filing. The Examiner accepts the amended title for record purposes. (See ITEPH30MRXEAPX4: amended title text.)\n- Drawings: Applicant submitted replacement Figures 4 and 5 (ITEPH30ORXEAPX4; ITG2IBLYPXXIFW2). The Examiner notes support for elements shown in the replacement figures is present in the specification (see pgpub_description/I2Y16E1MPXXIFW3). However the earlier Office action objected to missing axis labels on Figures 1, 2b, 2c, 2d, 2e and 3b. Applicant contends the keys/legends in those figures are sufficient. The Examiner maintains the drawing objection in part: graphs used for quantitative or comparative presentation must include ordinate (Y-axis) labels that clearly identify the plotted quantity and units where applicable. If Applicant believes a figure’s legend provides the necessary identification, Applicant should either (a) submit replacement figure sheets that include clear Y-axis labels (and units where appropriate) for the listed figures, or (b) provide a clear written explanation pointing to specification text that unambiguously identifies the ordinate(s) and units for each contested figure and confirm that replacement figures are unnecessary. Replacement sheets must comply with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121(d) and be labeled appropriately. Until the axis-label issue is corrected or formally disclaimed, the drawing objection will remain outstanding but will not by itself prevent further examination of claim issues.\nClaim Formalities and 35 U.S.C. 112(b) (Indefiniteness)\n- Background: The June 13, 2016 Office action raised multiple claim informalities and 112(b) issues (IPPLGGCBRXEAPX1). Applicant amended many claims and submitted remarks disputing the 112 rejections (ITEPH30ORXEAPX4). The Examiner has reviewed the amendments and applicant remarks.\n- Determination: Some informalities were corrected by Applicant’s claim amendments (for example, antecedent language in several places was clarified). Nonetheless, 35 U.S.C. 112(b) rejections are maintained for the pending claims in part because the claims, even as amended, contain terms and formulations that are vague or lack clear antecedent basis or clear claim scope for examination. Representative unresolved issues are summarized below and example corrective actions are suggested.\n  1) \"Representative future wind speed values\": The phrase remains in independent claim 1 and several dependents. The claims do not define what \"representative\" means in the claim context (representative of what: averages, measurements at particular rotor sections, modal values, statistical summaries?), nor do they limit the form of the representative values. The specification contains multiple possible meanings (single-value assumption, per-sector values, LIDAR upstream estimates, empirical statistical adjustments). Because the claim language covers multiple different constructions without clear limiting language, it is indefinite as written. To resolve, Applicant may (a) define the term in the claim (e.g., \"wherein 'representative future wind speed values' are one or more future wind speed estimates for the rotor swept area obtained from an upstream LIDAR measurement and optionally adjusted using site-specific empirical statistics\"), or (b) amend to remove the adjectival term 'representative' and use precise wording (e.g., \"future wind speed estimates\").\n  2) Singular vs. plural and antecedent basis: Several dependent claims (e.g., original claims 6, 7 / amended claims 6, 7) use singular wording such as \"the wind speed value\" while the parent claims recite \"values\". It is unclear whether a single value or multiple values are intended. Amend to consistent pluralization or otherwise clarify.\n  3) \"Assuming the wind speed value to be constant over a rotor swept area\": It is unclear whether this is an additional method step (i.e., a step of assuming) or a limitation of the estimating step. For clarity, recast as either an additional step (e.g., \"further comprising assuming that the representative future wind speed values are constant over the rotor swept area\") or a limiting clause modifying the estimating step (e.g., \"wherein the estimating assumes that the representative future wind speed values are constant over the rotor swept area\").\n  4) Cost-function terminology and antecedent basis: Applicant amended claim 1 to recite particular cost-function choices (converted aerodynamic energy; electrical energy + kinetic energy; electrical energy while setting a rotor-speed boundary condition). Some dependent claims still reference \"the electrical energy generated\" or \"the converted aerodynamic energy\" without explicit antecedent definitions; this creates ambiguity as to whether these are distinct items or synonyms for the \"energy output\" recited in claim 1. To resolve, ensure each term has explicit antecedent or be stated in claim 1 as alternative embodiments (e.g., 'wherein the cost function is one of: (a) ...; (b) ...; (c) ...') and remove later dependent claims that reintroduce ambiguity.\n  5) \"Model Predictive Control strategy / non-linear Model Predictive Control strategy\": For examination purposes, the Examiner construes these to require the control strategy utilize a predictive model (linear or non-linear). If Applicant intends these terms to carry special algorithmic meaning, Applicant should either (a) recite more specific structural or algorithmic steps (prediction horizon length, model equations, optimizer type), or (b) point to specific, unambiguous support in the specification demonstrating the exact algorithmic limitations intended.\n- Conclusion on 112(b): For the reasons above, the Examiner maintains the 35 U.S.C. 112(b) rejection as to the pending claims. Applicant may overcome the rejection by amendment or by explicitly identifying precise supporting passages in the specification that unambiguously define the disputed terms and by clarifying singular/plural inconsistencies. If Applicant submits an amendment that clarifies the claim scope in the suggested manner, the Examiner will reconsider the 112(b) standing.\nObviousness Rejections under 35 U.S.C. 103\n- Background: The June 13 Office action rejected the claims as obvious over Spruce et al. (US 2013/0106107 A1) in view of Kusiak et al. for general claims and added Hammerum (WO 2010/060772 A2) for claims reciting deformable trailing edge sections. Applicant filed arguments and amendments (ITEPH30ORXEAPX4) asserting the amended cost functions are not taught or suggested by the cited references and thus the rejections should be withdrawn.\n- Examiner analysis and determination: The combined teachings of Spruce and Kusiak (and Hammerum where relevant) still provide the teachings relied upon in the original action. The Examiner finds the following points persuasive support for the 103 rejections as previously articulated:\n  • Spruce discloses use of upstream wind sensing (including LIDAR) to predict wind in a future time window and a predictive controller that optimizes a cost function over a finite horizon (see original Office action citations and IPPLGGCBRXEAPX1 statements referencing Spruce: \"sensor 15, including LIDAR system 26, is used to predict future wind speed values\" and \"predictive controller 27\" optimizing a cost function). Those disclosures correspond to the claimed predictive estimation and horizon-based optimization aspects. (See IPPLGGCBRXEAPX1.)\n  • Kusiak discloses optimization of energy capture / power using controllable parameters including pitch and generator torque and models that include rotor-speed dynamics; figures and discussion in Kusiak address maximizing energy captured and set up cost/objective functions related to power/energy and rotor-speed constraints. Thus Kusiak supplies teachings regarding energy-based objectives and the explicit use of torque and pitch as decision variables. (See IPPLGGCBRXEAPX1 passages quoting Kusiak: \"The two controllable parameters [are] pitch angle x1 and generator torque x2\"; \"Maximizing energy captured from the wind\").\n  • Hammerum discloses deformable trailing-edge sections and control thereof, supplying the missing teaching for claims that recite deformable trailing edges.\n- Taken together, Spruce's upstream prediction and predictive control and Kusiak's energy-maximizing control and torque/pitch control render the claimed subject matter obvious to a person of ordinary skill in the art because the combination provides a reasoned basis to apply the energy-maximization objective with predictive wind estimates and torque/pitch control to improve energy capture. Applicant's amendment to recite particular alternative forms of the cost function does not, by itself, point to a non-obvious difference because Kusiak and Spruce address energy-related objectives and predictive control; the combination teaches using energy-based objectives over a prediction horizon with constraints relating to rotor speed and actuator penalties. For those reasons the Examiner maintains the 103 rejections for the pending claims.\n- How to overcome the 103 rejections: Applicant may overcome the 103 rejections by one or more of the following:\n  1) Amend the claims to add a limiting feature not suggested by Spruce+Kusiak (and Hammerum where relevant). Examples: a specific mathematical form of the cost function tied to a prediction model and constraints that produce a provable technical effect not suggested by the cited art; a particular predictor structure or horizon tied to LIDAR measurement characteristics; a novel constraint or soft-constraint implementation shown in the specification and not taught by the cited art.\n  2) Submit persuasive objective evidence (e.g., experimental data, test reports, model-based comparative results) showing unexpected results achieved by the claimed combination that would not have been predicted by one of ordinary skill in view of the cited references.\n  3) Argue facts of non-analogous art, lack of motivation to combine the references, or that the combined references fail to teach or suggest a specific limitation (with precise citations). Broad attorney argument alone is not persuasive; point-by-point record citations and factual showings are required.\nDisposition and Requirements for Next Reply\n- The Examiner maintains the 35 U.S.C. 112(b) and 103 rejections for the pending claims as set forth above. Claims cancelled by Applicant (8, 9, 11, 13) are given no further consideration.\n- Applicant's reply may consist of one or any combination of the following:\n  • Amend claims to address the identified 112(b) clarity issues (as discussed above) and, if appropriate, to add limitations that distinguish over the cited references.\n  • Provide claim-by-claim remarks addressing the Examiner’s 112(b) findings and 103 rationale with pinpoint citations to the specification in support of claim language (for example, identify exact page/line ranges where a disputed term is defined unequivocally). If Applicant relies on specification disclosure to overcome a 112(b) objection, state the exact location(s) of support. The Examiner will only accept clear, specific citation-based support.\n  • Provide persuasive evidence (test data or declarations) or argument (with record citations) that the combined prior art does not teach or suggest every claimed limitation or that the combination would not have been made by one of ordinary skill for the stated purpose.\n  • If Applicant elects to file amended drawings instead of arguing axis-label sufficiency, submit replacement drawing sheets complying with 37 C.F.R. § 1.121(d) that include explicit Y-axis labels (and units where applicable) for Figures 1, 2b-2e, and 3b, or provide a written explanation with precise specification citations that unambiguously identify the plotted quantities and units for each figure.\n- If Applicant files an amendment, provide a clean set of claims (with changes highlighted or an explanation of the amendments) and identify the specific support in the original/specification for any newly recited language. If Applicant files new evidence (declarations, test reports), please comply with the evidence rules (e.g., identification of declarant, attestation of authenticity, foundation, etc.).\nLimitations\n- The Examiner relied on the record available at the June 13, 2016 Office action and Applicant’s September 22, 2016 submissions. The Examiner's characterization of Spruce, Kusiak and Hammerum are based on the references cited in the June 13 action as summarized in that action (IPPLGGCBRXEAPX1). The Examiner's conclusions about what the references teach for prosecution purposes follow reasonable claim constructions used to permit meaningful prior art comparison; these constructions were stated in the June 13 action and applied here.\n- If Applicant submits further amendments that change claim scope materially from what is presently pending, the Examiner will re-evaluate 103 in light of the new scope and may require new prior art or reframe the rejections accordingly.\n- If Applicant believes any factual statement in this communication is incorrect, identify the specific statement, provide the correct citation or evidence, and the Examiner will reconsider.\nExaminer's Contact\nIf Applicant believes telephone discussion would clarify the issues and aid in resolving outstanding matters, contact the Examiner by telephone during normal hours. A response to this action must be timely filed and must address the outstanding rejections and objections detailed above.\nEND OF COMMUNICATION",
