the claims as amended. The applicant's amendments and arguments have been considered but do not supply record evidence sufficient to overcome the prior finding that Samuelson discloses the claimed elements and their arrangement.\nDiscussion and reasoning: The applicant amended claims 1, 12, and 20 to add express phraseology describing the neutral connector's electrical connection \"with a neutral bar on a top of the neutral bar,\" a shield having a \"connector side\" abutting the neutral connector and a \"spring side\" abutting the bias spring opposite the connector side, and corresponding method language referencing connection \"to the top of the neutral bar.\" The applicant argues that Samuelson discloses only clamping to opposing side walls of a neutral bar and therefore does not disclose connection on the top of the neutral bar or the claimed spring - shield - connector arrangement.\nThe prior Office action cited Samuelson's disclosure and figures as showing a conductive clip member (identified as element 27 in Samuelson) with an end portion in contact with neutral bar 110 and an insulating cover/shield (28) cooperating with an upper/bias member (26) and associated housing. The Office action expressly stated that Samuelson discloses a neutral connector (27) including a first side configured to make electrical connection with a neutral bar (110; FIG. 3), a shield (28) of an insulating material abutting a second side of the neutral connector opposite the first side, and a bias spring (26) abutting the shield and configured to bias the neutral connector. Those statements are in the record and form the basis for the prior rejections (see IXSXY3W6RXEAPX4).\nOn the present record, the examiner finds that Samuelson's conductive clip(s) and cover reflect the claimed structural relationship wherein a conductive member engages the neutral bar and the insulating cover/shield cooperates with a biasing member to bias the conductive member. The applicant's reformulation of the positional relationship (stating \"top of the neutral bar\" and naming connector vs. spring sides of the shield) does not, on the present record, introduce a material distinction that is not encompassed by Samuelson's disclosure as relied upon in the Office action. Samuelson's figures and descriptive text were relied on in the prior action to support the correspondence between Samuelson elements and the claimed elements. Therefore the 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(2) rejections over Samuelson are maintained for the amended independent claims and those dependent claims that were previously rejected under 102 in the January 13, 2017 action.\nApplicant response guidance: If applicant believes that Samuelson in fact does not disclose the claimed \"top of the neutral bar\" contact or the claimed relative arrangement spring - shield - connector, applicant should do one of the following: (a) point to specific Samuelson passages or figure callouts and explain precisely why each recited claim limitation is absent from those passages/figures, or (b) propose further amendments adding clear, supported structural limitations from the present specification that distinguish over the specific Samuelson passages identified, and provide pointer(s) to support in the specification for any added limiting language. Broad conclusory statements that Samuelson 'only clamps sides' without citation to the particular portions of Samuelson or without claim amendments that capture a distinguishing structural detail are insufficient to overturn the present record finding.\n2) 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections (Samuelson; Samuelson + Cornell)\nClaims 5 and 16 (second extension stop/second limit stop): The prior action found Samuelson teaches an extension stop but not an explicitly claimed second limit stop; nevertheless, the addition of a second limit stop was found to be within routine design choice and thus obvious. That reasoning remains on the record and the 103 rejection is maintained. If applicant believes the combination of references or the rationale for obviousness is improper, applicant should present argument or submit amendments adding structural detail (supported in the present specification) that distinguishes from Samuelson's teaching.\nClaims 6 and 17 (neutral conductor wire fastened to neutral connector): The prior action combined Samuelson with Cornell for the fastened conductor wire limitation. That combination and associated reasoning are maintained on the present record. If applicant disputes the combination, applicant should explain why Cornell is not combinable or propose narrowing amendments supported by the present specification.\nConclusion and Next Steps\nThe record as presently constituted supports maintaining the rejections: the 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(2) rejections of independent claims 1, 12, and 20 and the identified dependent claims over Samuelson are maintained; the 35 U.S.C. 103 rejections of claims 5 and 16 over Samuelson and of claims 6 and 17 over Samuelson in view of Cornell are maintained.\nApplicant is invited to respond to this communication by one or more of the following:\n- Present persuasive argument with citations to the applied references and/or to the present specification that demonstrates why the cited Samuelson passages and figures do not disclose the presently claimed limitations as interpreted; and/or\n- Amend the claims to add clear, supported structural limitations that distinguish over the specific Samuelson disclosures relied upon in the prior action; or\n- If applicant believes a claim set is now allowable, provide a clean set of claims in the formal amendment format for entry and consideration.\nIf applicant provides further amendments, the examiner will consider them and any accompanying arguments and will take appropriate further action (which may include withdrawal of a rejection if the amendment overcomes the cited references, or issuance of a subsequent Office action if the rejections are maintained or other issues are found). The present communication is non-final; applicant may continue prosecution in the normal course by filing a response within the statutory period.\nReferences relied upon in the prior action (for convenience in preparing applicant's response): Samuelson (US 2014/0321005) as cited in IXSXY3W6RXEAPX4; Cornell (US 3,001,172) as cited in IXSXY3W6RXEAPX4.\nNo allowance is entered at this time. Further prosecution is invited per the guidance above.",
  "limitations": [
    "The record extract shows that applicant's March 28, 2017 claim amendments were filed as proposed; USPTO formal entry procedures are not visible in the provided record. It is unknown whether the amended claims were formally entered into the application file at cutoff.",
    "The examiner's position relies on the prior Office action's identification of Samuelson passages and figures. The record contains the examiner's characterizations of Samuelson; the full text/figures of Samuelson are not included in the provided documents for independent verification within this task.",
    "If applicant supplies further narrowing amendments, the examiner will require support in the present specification; specific support citations should be provided by applicant to expedite examination."
  ]
}

