      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The limitations of the elected species/claims are considered to be disclosed or rendered obvious by Hayashi (US 2008/0094551) as set forth in the October 4, 2016 restriction/rejection action (ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4). Where applicant argues that some limitations are functional, the specification and the prior art disclose structures (PDLC layer, GHLC layer, electrodes, substrates) that perform those functions; apparatus claims are given weight for the structural elements and the references teach those structures or their equivalents. Thus the anticipation/102 rejections for claims 1, 7-10 and 20 are maintained for the reasons below.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Claims 1, 7-10, 20 are rejected under 35 U.S.C. 102(a)(1) as being anticipated by Hayashi et al. (US 2008/0094551).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Regarding claim 7, Hayashi et al. (figures 1A-1B) discloses the PDLC layer and the GHLC layer are realized in a transparent mode for transmitting incident light when no voltage is applied to the first and second electrodes or when a voltage difference between a first voltage applied to the first electrode and a second voltage applied to the second electrode is smaller than a first reference voltage.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "IQY4XL7NRXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "1. (Original) A light controlling apparatus, comprising: first and second substrates facing each other; a first electrode on the first substrate; a second electrode on the second substrate; and a polymer dispersed liquid crystal (PDLC) layer and a guest-host liquid crystal (GHLC) layer between the first electrode and the second electrode, wherein the PDLC layer includes first liquid crystals having droplets, and the GHLC layer includes second liquid crystals and dichroic dyes.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        14,
        15,
        16,
        21
      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "IQY4XL7NRXEAPX4",
        "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The combination of Hayashi with Jin was previously applied in the Office action (ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4) to reject claims 14, 15, 16 and 21 under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a). Hayashi teaches the basic multilayer liquid crystal structure and modes; Jin teaches transparent display panels with transmissive/emissive areas and top-emission arrangements. The combination provides the missing panel-level features and top-emission teaching relied upon in the rejection. The rejections are maintained because applicant's traverse does not supply persuasive evidence or claim amendments that introduce new structural limitations absent from the applied references.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Claims 14-16 and 21 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Hayashi et al. (US 2008/0094551) in view of Jin et al. (US 2008/0303982).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Hayashi et al. discloses the limitations as shown in the rejection of claim 14 above. However, Hayashi et al. is silent regarding a transparent display panel including a transmissive area and an emissive area, the emissive area having pixels displaying an image. Jin et al. (figure 3) teaches a transparent display panel including a transmissive area and an emissive area, the emissive area having pixels displaying an image.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "IQY4XL7NRXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "14. A transparent display device, comprising: a transparent display panel including a transmissive area and an emissive area, the emissive area having pixels displaying an image; and a light controlling apparatus on one surface of the transparent display panel, wherein the light controlling apparatus includes: first and second substrates facing each other; a first electrode on the first substrate; a second electrode on the second substrate; and a plurality of liquid crystal layers including a polymer dispersed liquid crystal (PDLC) layer and a guest-host liquid crystal (GHLC) layer between the first electrode and the second electrode,...",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        }
      ],
      "evidence_status": "record_supported"
    },
    {
      "claim_ids": [
        2,
        3,
        4,
        6,
        17,
        18,
        19
      ],
      "ground": "103",
      "claim_version_doc_ids": [
        "IQY4XL7NRXEAPX4",
        "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4"
      ],
      "status": "maintained",
      "rationale": "The Office action combined Hayashi with Zang (and where necessary Jin) to reject the dependent claims directed to walls/dams, alignment films, and uniform ratios of dyes/liquid crystals. The record supports that Zang teaches partitioning liquid composition with walls and adhesive structures that would render the claimed wall/partitioning and sealing features obvious in combination with Hayashi's multilayer teaching. The applicant's argument that Zang lacks a 'concavo-convex' teaching is noted but does not persuasively distinguish the references: the legal question in an obviousness rejection is whether a skilled artisan would have had reason to apply the partitioning/wall/adhesive teachings of Zang to the multilayer GHLC/PDLC teaching of Hayashi to achieve the claimed structural results.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "doc_id": "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Claims 2-4 and 6 are rejected under pre-AIA 35 U.S.C. 103(a) as being unpatentable over Hayashi et al. (US 2008/0094551) in view of Zang (US 2008/0020007).",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
        },
        {
          "doc_id": "ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4",
          "support_span": "Regarding claim 3, Zang (figures 4a-4c) teaches ratios of the second liquid crystals to the dichroic dyes between the plurality of liquid crystal areas have a difference within 1%.",
          "source_scope": "record_text"
