        2,
        3,
        4,
        6,
        14,
        16,
        18,
        20
      ],
      "argument_summary": "Applicant argues (A1) that Zang \"only discloses that liquid composition 45 is divided by a wall and an adhesive layer 43 is on the wall,\" and therefore Zang fails to teach a wall with a concavo-convex shape, and fails to teach first and second alignment films adhered to each other to encompass GHLC material. Applicant also argues Hayashi does not disclose the dam/wall features and that Jin does not supply detailed structures.",
      "response": "The record evidence does not establish a persuasive structural distinction that overcomes the applied combination rejections. The Office action relied on Hayashi for the multilayer PDLC/GHLC teaching and on Zang for partitioning/wall/adhesive structures. The assertion in the Office action that Zang provides wall/partitioning teaching is reflected in the prior action (ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4). Applicant’s characterization of Zang is noted; however, the correct legal standard for obviousness is whether a skilled artisan would have been motivated to combine the partitioning/adhesive teachings of Zang with Hayashi’s multilayer structure to arrive at the claimed arrangement. The cited record text shows the Examiner considered Zang’s wall/adhesive teaching relevant. Applicant’s reply does not supply persuasive evidence (e.g., an express passage in Zang or other prior art teaching that the particular concavo-convex wall geometry and adhered alignment films claimed were unknown or unobvious) that would distinguish the claimed combination structurally and nonobviously. Consequently, the prior rejections (35 U.S.C. 102 and 103) are maintained. If applicant believes a particular claim limitation is not met by the cited references, applicant may: (1) amend claims to add additional structural details not taught by the applied references, or (2) submit documentary evidence (e.g., highlighted copies of the cited references showing the absence of the asserted features or expert declaration) that directly and specifically supports the traversing position. At present, the record statements made in the reply (IYUQ7HZ6RXEAPX5 REM and IYUQ7HZ3RXEAPX5 A1) are argumentative but do not point to new documentary evidence that would overcome the established prior-art combinations.",
      "support_doc_id": "IYUQ7HZ6RXEAPX5",
      "support_span": "However, Zang only discloses that liquid composition 45 is divided by a wall and an adhesive layer 43 is on the wall. In other words, Zang fails to teach the wall having the concavo-convex shape. Also, Zang fails to teach that a first alignment layer and a second alignment layer including an adhesive material for encompassing the second liquid crystals and the dichroic dyes. Accordingly, Zang fails to teach that the first alignment film and the second alignment film are attached to each other on the wall."
    }
  ],
  "office_action": "Examiner Action — Non-Final Rejection (Mailing date: [date of action])\n\nA. Claims Under Consideration and Procedural Background\nClaims 1–4, 6–10, 14, 16 and 18–21 are currently pending. Applicant elected the species corresponding to the Figure 2 embodiment (the elected species) during the restriction requirement and identified claims 1–4 and 6–21 for continued prosecution on the elected species. The record includes the Office restriction/rejection mailed October 4, 2016 (ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4) which sets forth prior-art rejections over Hayashi and combinations with Jin and Zang. Applicant filed an amendment and arguments dated February 6, 2017 (IYUQ7HZ3RXEAPX5, IYUQ7HZ5RXEAPX5, IYUQ7HZ6RXEAPX5). The present Office action responds to the arguments and amended claim set.\n\nB. Summary of the Examiner’s Determinations\n1) The 35 U.S.C. § 102(a)(1) rejections of claims 1, 7–10 and 20 over Hayashi (as presented in the Office action of record) are maintained. The record supports that Hayashi discloses the structural elements recited by those claims (substrates, electrodes, PDLC and GHLC layers, and the modes of operation in which the layers transmit in one state and scatter/absorb in another). The Examiner’s prior factual finding that Hayashi provides the cited structural elements has not been rebutted by documentary evidence in the present reply.\n2) The 35 U.S.C. § 103(a) rejections of claims 14–16 and 21 over Hayashi in view of Jin are maintained. Hayashi provides the multilayer liquid crystal/PDLC+GHLC teaching; Jin provides teachings about transparent display panels with transmissive/emissive areas and top-emission arrangements. The combination supplies the structural/panel-level features recited in those claims.\n3) The 35 U.S.C. § 103(a) rejections of claims 2–4, 6 and 17–19 over Hayashi in view of Zang (and, where applied, further in view of Jin) are maintained. Zang teaches partitioning of liquid composition with walls and adhesive structures which the Examiner reasonably relied upon to show it would have been obvious to apply Zang’s wall/adhesive partitioning to Hayashi’s multilayer GHLC/PDLC structure to achieve sealed liquid crystal areas and desired mechanical adhesion between alignment films.\n\nC. Response to Applicant Arguments (A1)\nApplicant’s primary argument (IYUQ7HZ6RXEAPX5 REM and IYUQ7HZ3RXEAPX5 A1) asserts that Zang does not teach a wall with a concavo-convex shape and does not disclose adhered first and second alignment films that encompass GHLC material; applicant further argues Hayashi and Jin do not supply those specific structures.\n\nExaminer Response:\n- The Examiner acknowledges applicant’s point of emphasis regarding the particular wording \"concavo-convex\" and the particular arrangement of adhered alignment films. However, for both anticipation and obviousness inquiries the key question is whether the claimed structural elements are taught or would have been obvious to a person of ordinary skill in the art in view of the cited art. The October 4, 2016 Office action put applicant on notice that Hayashi supplies the multilayer PDLC/GHLC teachings and that Zang supplies wall/adhesive partitioning teachings (ITPYMYW7RXEAPX4). The Examiner relied on Zang's teaching of walls and adhesive on or near the wall to supply the claimed partitioning and sealing structure in combination with Hayashi's multilayer arrangement.\n- Applicant’s reply is argumentative but does not present new documentary evidence—e.g., a copy of Zang with highlighted passages showing an absence of relevant structures nor a declaration or other evidence establishing that one skilled in the art would not combine Zang’s partitioning with Hayashi’s multilayer structure. Absent such evidence, the Examiner maintains the rejections because the cited references disclose the structural elements or provide a clear rationale for combination. The Examiner also notes that functional language in applicant’s claims (modes realized upon application or removal of voltage) is given patentable weight only to the extent it corresponds to structural elements. The PDLC and GHLC structural elements in Hayashi are what enable the claimed modes; therefore the Examiner continues to rely on Hayashi for those mode-related limitations.\n\nD. Specific Points Where Applicant May Amend or Submit Evidence\n1) If applicant contends the claims require a specific geometry (for example, a particular concavo-convex wall profile or a specific manner of adhesion of two alignment films) that neither Hayashi nor Zang discloses, applicant may amend the claims to add explicit structural limitations (for example, a recitation of wall geometry parameters, materials or a structural fastening/adhesive layer with defined placement). Adding such structural limitations may avoid the present combinations.\n2) If applicant has documentary evidence that Zang lacks the wall/adhesive teaching relied upon by the Examiner, applicant should submit a clearly marked copy of Zang pointing to the passages supporting that position, or a declaration explaining why a skilled artisan would not have combined the cited references or why such combination would have been unpredictable.\n3) Applicant may also file proposed substitute claims that further limit the elected species to structural features not present in the cited art.\n\nE. Conclusion and Next Steps\nFor the reasons given above and supported by the record (see citations in Section B), the rejections under 35 U.S.C. §§ 102 and 103 identified in the October 4, 2016 Office action are maintained with respect to the currently pending claims listed at the top of this action. Applicant’s traverse has been considered but is not persuasive of patentability in view of the currently cited prior art and the present claim scope.\n\nApplicant is invited to reply by:\n- Amending the claims to include additional structural limitations that distinguish over the applied references; and/or\n- Presenting documentary evidence or a declaration that directly rebuts the Examiner’s basis for combination (for example, a copy of Zang with highlighted passages and explanation); and/or\n- Presenting persuasive argument explaining why a skilled artisan would not have combined the specific teachings of Hayashi and Zang/Jin to arrive at the claimed subject matter.\n\nIf applicant files amended claims, include a claim listing showing changes, and indicate any dependent claims that remain. If applicant files evidence, it should be submitted as an information disclosure or with an appropriate affidavit/declaration as required.\n\nF. Administrative Notes\n- The examiner confirms applicant’s election of the Figure 2 species and acknowledges the amendment to the title (IYUQ7HZ4RXEAPX5). The title objection is considered addressed by the amendment.\n- If applicant requests an interview to discuss the references and potential claim amendments, the examiner is available; contact information is on the record.\n\nExaminer: Lauren Nguyen\nArt Unit: 2871\n(571) 270-1428\n\n(End of Office Action)",
  "limitations": [
    "Applicant's A1 arguments are largely argumentative and did not supply documentary new evidence (e.g., highlighted prior art pages or a declaration) to rebut the Examiner's combination findings — record limited to documents provided through cutoff.",
    "The Examiner relied on prior Office action findings about what parts of Hayashi, Jin, and Zang disclose; if applicant submits marked copies showing contrary content or a supporting declaration, the findings can be reconsidered.",
    "Some applicant amendments contain drafting errors/typos; any further amendment should correct grammar/clarity to avoid entry objections."
  ]
}

